// Puzzle data for Redact, the daily uncover-the-article game. Imported ONLY by
// the server page (app/redact/page.js), which filters live <= today and ships
// each day base64-packed (an obfuscation against casual view-source, not a
// security boundary; the win still requires naming the subject).
//
// AUTHORING RULES (scripts/verify-redact.mjs gates every edit)
//
//   text     an original, in-house capsule article about one famous subject,
//            roughly 250-350 words (Sundays may run longer), encyclopedic in
//            register, editorial in detail. Facts stay at the textbook level;
//            hedge anything contestable. No em dashes, no double quotes, ASCII
//            apostrophes only. On a tier 3+ board the FIRST PARAGRAPH may not
//            contain the whole title, and should open one step to the side of
//            the obvious: the discovery, the material, the misconception, the
//            person who first wrote it down. Never open with the sentence a
//            player could complete from the category chip alone.
//   answer   the display title. The WIN CONDITION is revealing every content
//            word of the title (freebies and words under 3 letters ride free,
//            so 'Leonardo da Vinci' needs leonardo + vinci). The title may and
//            usually should appear in the body, where it renders as blocks.
//            NO SKELETON LEAK: the headline prints every free-riding word
//            verbatim and every other word as a block of its exact letter
//            width, before the clock starts, so only a generic connective
//            (the, a, an, of, and, in, on, at, to, for) may ride free. A
//            distinctive particle (da, de, di, von, st), a roman numeral, or
//            a digit fingerprints the subject and is banned from titles.
//   cat      Person | Place | Thing | Event | Work, shown as a chip from the
//            start so the opening minutes have direction (the one deliberate
//            improvement on the classic redacted-article formula).
//   diff     2-5, shown pre-start, and set by the WEEKDAY RAMP: Mon/Tue 2,
//            Wed/Thu 3, Fri/Sat 4, Sun 4-5 (a harder subject, not a different
//            mechanic; the game is in lib/sunday-editions.js). diff 1 is
//            RETIRED. The rating is an authored judgment and no gate can
//            prove it was earned, so hold to these criteria by hand:
//              2  a household name. Most solvers will get it. The floor, not
//                 a freebie: a board solved in three guesses is a failure.
//              3  well known, but needs a few reveals before it is pinned.
//              4  an educated reader knows the subject but will not guess it
//                 from the chip. Genuine work.
//              5  specialist or obscure. Sunday only.
//            Difficulty lives in the ARTICLE as much as the subject: a famous
//            subject earns its tier by withholding the giveaway vocabulary,
//            which is what the tier 3+ opening rule above enforces.
//   aka      word-level alias map: common misspellings or joined forms of the
//            KEY title words only ({ davinci: 'vinci' }). Never map a common
//            English word to a title word.
//
//   Identity fields (num/quizId/live/dateLabel/sunday) follow CLAUDE-QUIZZES
//   section 7e exactly. Subjects never repeat across the bank.
export const PUZZLES = [
  {
    num: 1, quizId: 'redact-8-6-26', live: '2026-08-06', dateLabel: 'August 6, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'Pompeii', aka: { pompei: 'pompeii' },
    text: 'Pompeii was an ancient Roman city in the Campania region of southern Italy, close to the modern city of Naples. Founded around the seventh or sixth century BC, it grew into a prosperous port and resort town of roughly eleven thousand residents, with an amphitheater, public baths, taverns, bakeries, and villas decorated with elaborate frescoes and mosaics.\n\nIn the year 79, the nearby volcano Vesuvius erupted with catastrophic force. Over two days, the eruption buried the city under more than four meters of ash and pumice. Thousands of residents died, many overcome by a surge of superheated gas. A famous letter by the writer Pliny the Younger, who watched the disaster from across the bay, remains the only surviving eyewitness account.\n\nThe buried city was largely forgotten for more than fifteen centuries. Formal excavation began in 1748, and the digs revealed streets, houses, and public buildings preserved in remarkable detail beneath the volcanic layer. The ash had sealed loaves of bread in ovens, election notices painted on walls, and graffiti left by ordinary residents. Excavators later found that the bodies of victims had left hollows in the hardened ash, and by pouring plaster into these voids they produced casts of people in their final moments.\n\nToday the site is a World Heritage Site and one of the most visited attractions in Italy, drawing millions of tourists each year. It remains the richest single source of evidence about daily life in an ancient Roman city, and its name has become a byword for sudden destruction.',
  },
  {
    num: 2, quizId: 'redact-8-7-26', live: '2026-08-07', dateLabel: 'August 7, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 1, answer: 'The Titanic', aka: {},
    text: 'The Titanic was a British passenger liner operated by the White Star Line, and the largest ship afloat when she entered service in 1912. Built in Belfast over three years, she stretched nearly nine hundred feet, carried a swimming pool, squash court, and grand staircase, and was widely described in the press as practically unsinkable thanks to her sixteen watertight compartments.\n\nOn her maiden voyage from Southampton to New York, carrying more than two thousand two hundred passengers and crew, the liner struck an iceberg in the North Atlantic late on the night of April 14, 1912. The collision buckled the hull plates along her starboard side and opened too many compartments to the sea. She sank in under three hours, in the early morning of April 15. There were lifeboats for barely half the people aboard, and many launched only partly full. About fifteen hundred people died in the disaster, while some seven hundred survivors were rescued at dawn by the liner Carpathia.\n\nThe sinking shocked the public on both sides of the Atlantic, ruined the unsinkable boast, and led to sweeping reforms: lifeboats for every soul aboard, round-the-clock radio watches, and an international ice patrol that still operates today.\n\nThe wreck itself was lost for more than seventy years, until a 1985 expedition led by Robert Ballard found it resting in two pieces on the seabed, around twelve thousand five hundred feet down. Artifacts, films, books, and a record-breaking 1997 movie have kept the story alive, and the ship remains the most famous maritime disaster in history.',
  },
  {
    num: 3, quizId: 'redact-8-8-26', live: '2026-08-08', dateLabel: 'August 8, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 1, answer: 'Albert Einstein', aka: { einstien: 'einstein' },
    text: 'Albert Einstein was a theoretical physicist, born in the German city of Ulm in 1879, who became the most famous scientist of the twentieth century. As a young man he struggled to find an academic post and took a job examining patent applications in Bern, Switzerland. It was there, in 1905, that he published four papers that changed physics: on the photoelectric effect, on Brownian motion, on special relativity, and on the equivalence of mass and energy, expressed in the most famous equation in science.\n\nA decade later he completed his general theory of relativity, which described gravity not as a force but as the curvature of space and time around mass. When measurements of starlight bending during a 1919 solar eclipse matched his predictions, newspapers around the world made him an overnight celebrity. He received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921, awarded chiefly for the photoelectric effect rather than relativity.\n\nHe left Germany for good when the Nazis came to power in 1933, settling in Princeton, New Jersey, where he spent the rest of his career. In 1939 he signed a letter to President Roosevelt warning that atomic weapons were possible, though he played no part in building them and spent his later years arguing for disarmament and civil rights. He declined an offer to serve as president of Israel in 1952.\n\nHe died in 1955, leaving behind an image recognized everywhere: the wild white hair, the mustache, the gentle wit. His name has become a plain synonym for genius, and his theories still pass every experimental test physicists can devise.',
  },
  {
    num: 4, quizId: 'redact-8-9-26', live: '2026-08-09', dateLabel: 'August 9, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 4, answer: 'The Rosetta Stone', aka: { rosseta: 'rosetta', rossetta: 'rosetta' },
    text: 'The Rosetta Stone is a broken slab of dark granodiorite that became the key to reading the writing of ancient Egypt. It carries a single decree, issued at Memphis in 196 BC on behalf of the young king Ptolemy the Fifth, inscribed three times: once in hieroglyphic script, once in the everyday Egyptian script called Demotic, and once in ancient Greek.\n\nFrench soldiers rebuilding a fort near the Egyptian town of Rosetta uncovered the slab in 1799, during the Napoleonic campaign in Egypt. Scholars traveling with the army instantly recognized what the triple text might mean: since the Greek could be read, the two Egyptian versions of the same words might finally be cracked. When the French forces surrendered, the slab passed to Britain, and it has been displayed in the British Museum since 1802, where it remains the most visited object in the collection.\n\nDecipherment took two decades of rivalry. The English polymath Thomas Young worked out that some hieroglyphs in oval rings spelled royal names. The French scholar Jean-Francois Champollion then made the decisive breakthrough in 1822, showing that the script recorded the sounds of the Egyptian language, not just symbols for ideas. His announcement founded the modern field of Egyptology and unlocked three thousand years of inscriptions, from temple walls to tax receipts.\n\nThe slab itself is incomplete: the top section, which likely carried more hieroglyphic text, has never been found. Egypt has repeatedly requested its return, and the debate over where it belongs continues. Its name, meanwhile, has entered the language itself, used for anything that unlocks a code, including a popular language-learning course and a comet-chasing space probe.',
  },
  {
    num: 5, quizId: 'redact-8-10-26', live: '2026-08-10', dateLabel: 'August 10, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 1, answer: 'The Great Wall of China', aka: {},
    text: 'The Great Wall of China is the largest building project ever undertaken, a chain of fortifications stretching thousands of miles across northern China, from the deserts of the west toward the sea in the east. It is not a single wall but many overlapping walls, built and rebuilt by different dynasties over more than two thousand years, linked by watchtowers, garrison forts, and beacon platforms that could flash warnings by smoke and fire.\n\nThe earliest connected barriers were joined in the third century BC under the first emperor, Qin Shi Huang, who linked older state walls into one line against raids from the northern steppe. Hundreds of thousands of soldiers, peasants, and prisoners labored on the early ramparts of packed earth, and countless workers died doing it, a grief remembered in Chinese folk songs and legends.\n\nMost of what visitors see today is far younger, built of brick and cut stone by the Ming dynasty between the fourteenth and seventeenth centuries, after Mongol armies had shown how dangerous the frontier could be. The Ming sections near Beijing, with their crenellated parapets and steep gray towers climbing along mountain ridges, are the postcard image of the monument.\n\nAs a military barrier it had a mixed record, and invaders sometimes simply bribed or rode around it. As a symbol it has no rival: it marked the edge of an empire and now stands for the country itself. Contrary to a persistent myth, it cannot be seen from the Moon with the naked eye. It was named a World Heritage Site in 1987 and draws some ten million visitors a year, most of them to a few restored stretches while remote sections crumble quietly into the hills.',
  },
  {
    num: 6, quizId: 'redact-8-11-26', live: '2026-08-11', dateLabel: 'August 11, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Cleopatra', aka: {},
    text: 'Cleopatra the Seventh was the last active ruler of the Ptolemaic kingdom of Egypt, and the most famous queen of the ancient world. Though she ruled Egypt, her family was Macedonian Greek, descended from a general of Alexander the Great, and had governed from the city of Alexandria for nearly three centuries. Unusually for her dynasty, she learned the Egyptian language, one of perhaps nine she spoke, and presented herself to her subjects as the living goddess Isis.\n\nShe came to the throne as a teenager, was driven out by rivals at court, and won her crown back by allying with Julius Caesar, who arrived in pursuit of a Roman civil war. Legend says she had herself smuggled into his presence rolled inside a carpet or bedsack. She bore him a son and lived in Rome as his guest until his assassination.\n\nShe then formed a political and romantic alliance with Mark Antony, one of the three men contending for control of Rome. Together they ruled the richest realm of the eastern Mediterranean, had three children, and staged a famous ceremony distributing eastern territories to their heirs. Rome declared war, and their combined fleet was defeated at the battle of Actium in 31 BC by the forces of Octavian, the future emperor Augustus.\n\nThe pair retreated to Alexandria, where both took their own lives the following year, Antony by his sword and the queen, according to tradition, by the bite of a smuggled asp. Egypt became a Roman province, ending three thousand years of rule by pharaohs. Shakespeare, Hollywood, and countless painters have retold her story ever since, usually preferring the legend of the seductress to the record of a shrewd, multilingual head of state.',
  },
  {
    num: 7, quizId: 'redact-8-12-26', live: '2026-08-12', dateLabel: 'August 12, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 1, answer: 'The Eiffel Tower', aka: { eifel: 'eiffel', eiffle: 'eiffel' },
    text: 'The Eiffel Tower is a wrought-iron lattice tower on the Champ de Mars in Paris, built as the entrance arch and centerpiece of the 1889 World Fair, held to mark the centennial of the French Revolution. It was designed and erected by the engineering company of Gustave Eiffel, whose name it carries, from a concept by two of his engineers. Some three hundred workers assembled more than eighteen thousand iron pieces with about two and a half million rivets, finishing the job in just over two years.\n\nAt roughly one thousand feet, it nearly doubled the previous tallest structure on Earth and held the world record for four decades, until a New York skyscraper, the Chrysler Building, finally passed it in 1930. Many Parisian artists and writers loathed it at first, publishing protests against what they called a useless and monstrous smokestack, and the plan was to dismantle it after twenty years. It was saved largely because it proved perfect for radio transmission, a role that later expanded to broadcasting television across the region.\n\nVisitors ride elevators, or climb more than six hundred stairs, to platforms on three levels, with restaurants partway up and a champagne bar at the summit. The structure is repainted by hand every few years, taking around sixty tons of paint, and it sways slightly in strong wind while growing measurably taller in summer heat as the iron expands.\n\nIt has become the universal symbol of Paris and of France, appearing in countless films and photographs, sparkling with thousands of flashing lights for five minutes every hour after dark. It is among the most visited paid monuments anywhere, welcoming around six million people in a typical year.',
  },
  {
    num: 8, quizId: 'redact-8-13-26', live: '2026-08-13', dateLabel: 'August 13, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 3, answer: 'Moby-Dick', aka: { mobydick: 'moby' },
    text: 'Moby-Dick is a novel by the American writer Herman Melville, published in 1851, widely regarded today as one of the greatest works of fiction in the English language. It opens with one of the most famous first lines in literature, three words in which the narrator asks to be called by a plain biblical name.\n\nThat narrator signs onto a whaling ship out of Nantucket, sharing a bed at a crowded inn with a tattooed harpooneer from the South Seas who becomes his closest friend. Only once at sea do the crew learn the true purpose of the voyage: their one-legged captain, Ahab, is hunting the enormous white sperm whale that took his leg on a previous voyage, and he has no interest in commerce compared with revenge. He nails a gold coin to the mast as a prize for the first man to sight the beast.\n\nThe book itself is as strange and sprawling as its captain, mixing high adventure with sermons, stage drama, and long factual chapters on whale anatomy and the work of whaling, drawn from the author\'s own years at sea. Readers in 1851 were baffled: the novel sold poorly, reviews were mixed, and its author died in 1891 largely forgotten, working as a customs inspector in New York.\n\nA revival in the 1920s changed everything. Critics recognized the book as a profound meditation on obsession, fate, nature, and the limits of knowledge, and it became a fixture of classrooms and adaptations, including a 1956 film with Gregory Peck. The white whale now names everything from coffee chains to impossible obsessions in everyday speech.',
  },
  {
    num: 9, quizId: 'redact-8-14-26', live: '2026-08-14', dateLabel: 'August 14, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Amazon River', aka: {},
    text: 'The Amazon River of South America is the greatest river on Earth by any measure of sheer volume. It discharges more water into the sea than the next several largest rivers combined, roughly one fifth of all the fresh water that flows off the planet\'s land, and during the rainy season its mouth pushes drinkable water far out into the Atlantic. By length it runs about four thousand miles from the Peruvian Andes across Brazil, and partisans have long argued over whether it edges out the Nile as the longest river of all.\n\nThe river and its more than one thousand tributaries drain the largest rainforest in the world, a basin nearly the size of the continental United States. Its waters hold pink river dolphins, giant otters, electric eels, flesh-eating piranhas, and thousands of fish species, more than the entire Atlantic Ocean. Seasonal floods raise the water level by dozens of feet and spread it miles into the forest, where fish swim among the trees eating fallen fruit.\n\nThe first European to descend the river was the Spaniard Francisco de Orellana in 1542, whose party reported attacks by female warriors, which is how the river came to be named for the warrior women of Greek myth. Remarkably, for a waterway in places over one hundred feet deep and many miles wide, not a single bridge crosses its main stem along its entire length; the cities and villages along its banks are linked by boat and by air.\n\nOcean freighters can steam nearly a thousand miles upstream to the Brazilian city of Manaus, a metropolis of two million people in the middle of the forest, where the dark Rio Negro and the sandy-colored main channel famously flow side by side for miles without mixing.',
  },
  {
    num: 10, quizId: 'redact-8-15-26', live: '2026-08-15', dateLabel: 'August 15, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Leonardo da Vinci', aka: { davinci: 'vinci' },
    text: 'Leonardo da Vinci was an Italian painter, engineer, anatomist, and inventor of the High Renaissance, often called the most gifted person who ever lived. Born in 1452 near the Tuscan hill town whose name he carried, the illegitimate son of a notary, he trained in a Florence workshop and spent his career serving patrons in Florence, Milan, Rome, and finally France.\n\nAs a painter he left fewer than twenty finished works, yet two of them are arguably the most famous images on Earth: a Milanese wall painting of a final meal, capturing the instant a table of followers learns of a coming betrayal, and a small portrait of a Florentine merchant\'s wife whose faint smile draws enormous crowds at the Louvre. His technique of softly blurred edges and layered glazes gave faces a living quality no contemporary could match.\n\nHis restless mind ranged far beyond art. He filled thousands of notebook pages, written right to left in mirror script, with studies of water, birds in flight, war machines, flying machines, and the human body, based on dissections of corpses he performed himself. He designed canals, fortresses, stage sets, and a giant bronze horse that was never cast, and he described devices resembling helicopters, tanks, and diving suits centuries before they were feasible. Almost none of it was published in his lifetime, so his science had to be rediscovered later.\n\nHe died in 1519 in the Loire valley, in the service of the French king. Five centuries on, his notebooks sell for fortunes, his paintings are beyond price, and his name has become shorthand for boundless curiosity, the original Renaissance man.',
  },
  {
    num: 11, quizId: 'redact-8-16-26', live: '2026-08-16', dateLabel: 'August 16, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 5, answer: 'The Antikythera Mechanism', aka: { antikithera: 'antikythera', anticythera: 'antikythera', antikythira: 'antikythera' },
    text: 'In the spring of 1901 sponge divers, driven by a storm into shelter off a small Greek island between Crete and the Peloponnese, found a Roman era wreck lying on the sea floor. Over the following months they raised bronze statues, marble figures, glassware and coins, luxury cargo apparently bound for Italy. Among the salvage was a shapeless mass of corroded bronze and rotted wood, roughly the size of a large book, which was catalogued with the rest and set aside.\n\nAt first the corroded lump drew little attention. Only when it split open to reveal precisely cut bronze gearwheels did scholars realize something was wrong with the standard story of ancient technology: nothing approaching this complexity appears again in the surviving record for well over a thousand years, until the astronomical clocks of medieval Europe.\n\nDecades of study, and modern X-ray and CT scanning of the surviving fragments, have reconstructed most of its workings. A hand crank turned dozens of interlocking gears, one carrying over two hundred teeth, driving pointers on multiple dials. The front face tracked the Sun and Moon through the zodiac, showed the Moon phase with a rotating silvered ball, and probably displayed the five planets known to antiquity. Rear dials followed a nineteen-year calendar cycle, predicted eclipses along an eighteen-year cycle, and even counted down to the next Olympic games. A clever pin-and-slot arrangement reproduced the Moon speeding up and slowing down in its orbit.\n\nInscriptions in tiny Greek letters, effectively a user manual, cover its plates. Its maker is unknown, though the island of Rhodes and the school of the astronomer Hipparchus are frequent suspects, and the design suggests a tradition of such instruments now entirely lost. The original fragments rest in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, while researchers and hobbyists keep building working replicas, some out of Lego.',
  },
  {
    num: 12, quizId: 'redact-8-17-26', live: '2026-08-17', dateLabel: 'August 17, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 2, answer: 'Monopoly', aka: { monopoloy: 'monopoly' },
    text: 'Monopoly is the best-selling branded board game in history, a real-estate trading contest in which players circle a square track buying properties, building houses and hotels, and driving one another into bankruptcy. Its familiar furniture includes two dice, pastel paper money, a jail corner, a luxury tax, and a parade of small metal tokens such as the top hat, the racecar, the thimble, and a dog.\n\nThe official corporate legend long credited an unemployed Pennsylvania salesman, Charles Darrow, with inventing the game in his basement during the Depression and selling it to Parker Brothers in 1935, becoming a millionaire. The fuller truth surfaced decades later in a court case: the design descends from the Landlord Game, patented back in 1904 by Elizabeth Magie, a follower of the economist Henry George, who intended her game to teach players how rent enriches property owners and impoverishes tenants. Homemade versions circulated for years, and Darrow learned the game from friends before selling it as his own.\n\nThe classic American board uses street names from Atlantic City, New Jersey, running from the cheap purple lots up to the dark blue pair of Park Place and the most expensive square of all. The British edition swapped in London streets, with Mayfair at the top, and licensed versions now exist for thousands of cities, movies, and franchises.\n\nGames are famous for lasting hours and straining friendships, partly because house rules like cash under Free Parking slow the design down. World championships have been held since the 1970s, and the brand has expanded into electronic banking editions, a long-running fast-food promotion, and one of the most recognizable mascots in gaming, a mustachioed tycoon in a top hat.',
  },
  {
    num: 13, quizId: 'redact-8-18-26', live: '2026-08-18', dateLabel: 'August 18, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Colosseum', aka: { colloseum: 'colosseum', collosseum: 'colosseum' },
    text: 'It did not always carry the name it has now. Ancient writers called it the Flavian Amphitheatre, after the family of emperors who paid for it. The name in use today seems to derive from a colossal bronze statue that stood beside it, a figure some thirty metres high originally made to represent the emperor Nero and later reworked as a sun god. The statue is long gone; the nickname attached itself to its neighbour and stuck.\n\nWork began in Rome under Vespasian around 70, on ground that had been the ornamental lake of Nero\'s enormous palace complex, a choice with an obvious political point: private pleasure ground returned to public use. His son Titus opened the building in 80 with games said to have lasted a hundred days, and a second son, Domitian, added the top storey and the chambers below the arena floor. The travertine came from quarries near Tivoli, about twenty miles away.\n\nThe Colosseum is an ellipse of roughly one hundred and eighty nine metres by one hundred and fifty six, built of travertine blocks, tufa, brick and concrete. Eighty numbered entrances fed a system of staircases that separated the crowd by rank, and small tokens told a spectator which arch to use. Capacity is disputed; most estimates fall between fifty and seventy thousand. A great awning, worked by sailors detailed from the fleet, could be drawn over part of the seating against the sun.\n\nGames of the old kind ended in late antiquity, and the structure was put to other uses: workshops, housing, a fortress for a Roman family in the middle ages. Earthquakes brought down much of the southern outer wall, and for centuries the ruin served as a quarry for cut stone and iron clamps. In 1749 Benedict XIV declared the site consecrated by the blood of Christian martyrs, a claim later scholars have questioned but which helped stop the stripping of stone.',
  },
  {
    num: 14, quizId: 'redact-8-19-26', live: '2026-08-19', dateLabel: 'August 19, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 3, answer: 'Stonehenge', aka: { stonehendge: 'stonehenge', stonhenge: 'stonehenge', stonehedge: 'stonehenge' },
    text: 'In 1740 the antiquarian William Stukeley published a survey of a ring of standing stones on the chalk downs of southern England, noting that its main axis pointed toward the midsummer sunrise. He credited the builders to the Druids, an attribution that proved durable in the popular imagination and wrong in fact, since that priesthood belonged to the Iron Age and the monument was already ancient by the time it appeared. Twentieth century excavation replaced his romance with a chronology drawn from radiocarbon dates and buried postholes.\n\nStonehenge stands on Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire. Construction proceeded in stages over roughly fifteen centuries, beginning around 3000 BC with a circular ditch and bank enclosing a ring of pits called the Aubrey Holes. The sarsen stones, some weighing more than twenty tons, came from the Marlborough Downs about twenty five kilometres north and were raised in a circle capped by lintels, with five taller trilithons set inside. The smaller bluestones were brought from the Preseli Hills in west Wales, roughly two hundred and forty kilometres away.\n\nThe joinery is unusual for stonework. Lintels rest on tenons cut into the uprights and lock to one another with tongue and groove, techniques borrowed from carpentry. The axis aligns with sunrise at the summer solstice and sunset at the winter solstice, and many archaeologists now treat the winter alignment as the more important of the two. Cremated human remains recovered from the Aubrey Holes and elsewhere indicate that the enclosure served as a cemetery for several centuries.\n\nAn avenue links the stones to the River Avon, and a large timber settlement at Durrington Walls, two miles away, appears to have housed the people who built and used the monument. A local barrister bought the site at auction in 1915 and presented it to the nation three years later. It was inscribed as a World Heritage property in 1986 together with Avebury, and proposals to reroute the busy road that passes nearby have been argued over for decades.',
  },
  {
    num: 15, quizId: 'redact-8-20-26', live: '2026-08-20', dateLabel: 'August 20, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 3, answer: 'The California Gold Rush', aka: {},
    text: 'On a January morning in 1848 a carpenter named James Marshall, building a sawmill on the American River for a Swiss immigrant landowner, noticed flakes of yellow metal glinting in the tailrace. He tested them by hammering: they flattened rather than shattered. His employer, John Sutter, tried to keep the find quiet, fearing that a stampede would overrun his land grant, but the secret escaped within weeks, and a San Francisco merchant marched through the streets waving a bottle of dust, having first bought up every shovel in town.\n\nWord crossed the continent and the oceans, and in 1849 tens of thousands of fortune seekers, the forty-niners, poured in by wagon over the plains, by ship around Cape Horn, and by a fever-ridden shortcut across the isthmus of Panama. They came from Chile, China, Mexico, Australia, and all over Europe, making the goldfields one of the most cosmopolitan places on Earth. San Francisco exploded from a village of eight hundred into a city of tens of thousands, its harbor choked with ships abandoned by crews who ran for the hills.\n\nThe early arrivals could pan nuggets from the streambeds; later miners needed sluices, hydraulic hoses that washed away whole hillsides, and deep quarries. A lucky few struck fortunes, but most found only hard labor at ruinous prices, where eggs could cost a dollar apiece. Merchants did better, among them a dry-goods seller named Levi Strauss and the wagon makers Studebaker and Wells and Fargo. For California natives the rush was catastrophic, bringing disease, dispossession, and violence.\n\nThe territory vaulted straight to statehood in 1850, and the rush stamped the state with its identity forever: the golden state, whose motto Eureka means I have found it.',
  },
  {
    num: 16, quizId: 'redact-8-21-26', live: '2026-08-21', dateLabel: 'August 21, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 4, answer: 'The Nazca Lines', aka: { nasca: 'nazca', nazka: 'nazca' },
    text: 'The technique was subtraction rather than construction. A crust of reddish pebbles, darkened by iron oxide, covers the coastal plain, and beneath it lies paler, yellowish sediment. Sweeping the stones aside into low banks exposes that lighter ground, and the resulting stripe stays visible for a very long time, because the plain receives almost no rain and the air near the surface is unusually still. The marks are shallow, and could have been laid out with cord and stakes.\n\nThey were noticed from the ground long before they were understood. An archaeologist walking the foothills in 1927 recorded them as ceremonial roads. Commercial pilots flying the coastal route in the following decade reported strange markings below, and by 1941 the American historian Paul Kosok had begun to survey them. Maria Reiche, a German mathematician, took up the work and spent roughly half a century measuring, mapping and sweeping the surfaces clean.\n\nThe Nazca Lines, as the markings on this stretch of pampa in southern Peru are usually called, are mostly geometric. Straight lines run for miles across dry riverbeds and low hills without deviating, and there are hundreds of trapezoids, triangles and spirals. The animal and plant figures, perhaps seventy of them, are a small minority: a hummingbird, a monkey with a coiled tail, a spider, a condor. Many are drawn as a single unbroken line. Most are attributed to the Nazca culture, roughly 100 BCE to 700 CE, with some earlier examples credited to the Paracas.\n\nPurpose remains open. Kosok and Reiche argued for an astronomical function, with lines pointing to solstice risings, but a statistical test published in 1968 found no more alignments than chance would produce. Later researchers have emphasized water, since many figures sit near seasonal channels and the lines may have been walked in procession. The site was inscribed as a World Heritage property in 1994, and much of the damage since has come from squatter settlements, a highway, and vehicles whose tire scars the wind will not erase.',
  },
  {
    num: 17, quizId: 'redact-8-22-26', live: '2026-08-22', dateLabel: 'August 22, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Ada Lovelace', aka: { lovlace: 'lovelace', lovelance: 'lovelace' },
    text: 'In 1843 a London scientific journal printed an English translation of a French paper by an Italian military engineer, describing a calculating machine that had never been built. Appended to the translation were seven notes, signed only with the initials A.A.L., which together ran to considerably more than the length of the article itself. The translator had been asked why she did not write an original paper on the subject, and replied that it had not occurred to her.\n\nShe had been educated for it. Her mother, who separated from her father weeks after the birth and raised her alone, insisted on a rigorous program of mathematics and logic, in part, according to a long tradition, to guard against the temperament of the poet she had married. Among the daughter\'s tutors and correspondents were the logician Augustus De Morgan and the science writer Mary Somerville, and it was Somerville who introduced her, at seventeen, to the inventor of the unbuilt machine.\n\nThe initials belonged to Augusta Ada King, Countess of Lovelace, born in 1815 as the only legitimate child of Lord Byron, and the machine was Charles Babbage\'s Analytical Engine. Note G of her commentary sets out, step by step, how the engine could be made to compute Bernoulli numbers, and it is often described as the first algorithm published for a machine. Ada Lovelace also argued that such a device need not be confined to numbers: if relationships between pitched sounds could be expressed in symbols, she wrote, it might compose music.\n\nShe died in 1852, at thirty-six, of what was then called cancer of the womb, and the engine was never completed in her lifetime. Historians disagree about how much of the notes was hers and how much Babbage\'s, and the surviving letters between them are read both ways. What is less disputed is the scope of the claim she made for a general machine. A programming language developed for the United States military was named for her in 1980.',
  },
  {
    num: 18, quizId: 'redact-8-23-26', live: '2026-08-23', dateLabel: 'August 23, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 4, answer: 'Timbuktu', aka: { timbuctoo: 'timbuktu', tombouctou: 'timbuktu', timbucktu: 'timbuktu' },
    text: 'The city sits where two kinds of travel meet. To the north lies the Sahara, crossed by camel caravans carrying slabs of rock salt cut from desert mines; to the south runs the great northward bend of the Niger, worked by boats bringing gold, ivory and grain up from the forest lands. Founded around the twelfth century as a seasonal camp of Tuareg nomads, the settlement in between grew rich largely by standing where it did. Salt traded for gold, sometimes weight for weight, and the place in the middle became a legend.\n\nIts golden age came under the Mali and Songhai empires. The Malian ruler Mansa Musa, often called the richest man who ever lived, passed through in the fourteenth century on his famous pilgrimage to Mecca, scattering so much gold that its price reportedly sagged for years, and he commissioned the mud-brick Djinguereber mosque that still stands. By the sixteenth century the city held tens of thousands of people and a university district centered on the Sankore mosque, where scholars taught law, astronomy, and medicine. Books became the most prized trade goods of all, and families amassed private libraries whose hundreds of thousands of manuscripts survive, in part, to this day.\n\nTo Europeans the city hardened into a myth of unreachable wealth, a name still used for the ends of the earth. A Geographical Society prize for the first explorer to reach it and return was claimed in 1828 by a Frenchman who found not a city of gold but a fading desert town of mud and sand.\n\nDecline followed shifting trade routes and Moroccan invasion. Occupied by militants in 2012, when shrines were smashed and residents smuggled manuscripts out to safety, the World Heritage city endures as a symbol of African scholarship and of heritage under threat.',
  },
  {
    num: 19, quizId: 'redact-8-24-26', live: '2026-08-24', dateLabel: 'August 24, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'William Shakespeare', aka: { shakespear: 'shakespeare', shakespere: 'shakespeare', wiliam: 'william' },
    text: 'In 1623, seven years after the author\'s death, two of his former colleagues in the acting company gathered his plays into a single large volume. John Heminges and Henry Condell collected thirty six of them, and about half had never been printed before. Without that book, Macbeth, Twelfth Night, The Tempest and Julius Caesar would be lost. It sold for roughly a pound, carried a stiff engraved portrait by Martin Droeshout, and is now among the most valuable printed books in existence.\n\nWilliam Shakespeare was baptised at Stratford-upon-Avon in April 1564, the son of a glover and wool dealer who held municipal office and later fell into debt. At eighteen he married Anne Hathaway, who was some years older, and they had three children: Susanna, and the twins Hamnet and Judith. Nothing certain is known of the years between the twins\' baptism and 1592, when a rival writer sneered in print at an upstart crow who thought he could match the university men.\n\nHe prospered as a working man of the theatre rather than as a poet with a patron. He was an actor and, more importantly, a sharer in the Lord Chamberlain\'s Men, which became the King\'s Men under James I; a sharer took a portion of the takings, and later a portion of the Globe, built on the south bank in 1599. Individual plays appeared in cheap quarto editions, some of them badly garbled, and a sequence of one hundred and fifty four sonnets was printed in 1609.\n\nHe returned to Stratford, bought property there, and died in 1616, leaving a will that mentions his second best bed and says nothing about manuscripts. His standing grew slowly and then enormously; by the eighteenth century editors were arguing over his commas, and by the nineteenth his plays had been translated across Europe. Almost everything read today descends from that 1623 collection, and the gaps in the record have kept several centuries of biographers busy inventing what the documents do not say.',
  },
  {
    num: 20, quizId: 'redact-8-25-26', live: '2026-08-25', dateLabel: 'August 25, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Marie Curie', aka: { curry: 'curie' },
    text: 'Marie Curie was a physicist and chemist who became the most celebrated woman in the history of science. Born Maria Sklodowska in Warsaw in 1867, when Poland was under Russian rule, she studied in secret classes barred to women, worked as a governess to fund her sister\'s education, and finally moved to Paris to enroll at the Sorbonne, where she lived on bread and tea in a garret while taking degrees in physics and mathematics.\n\nWith her husband Pierre, a physicist she married in 1895, she began investigating the mysterious rays given off by uranium. She coined the word radioactivity for the phenomenon, showed it was a property of atoms themselves, and after processing tons of a waste ore called pitchblende in a leaky shed, announced two new elements: polonium, named for her homeland, and radium, whose faint blue glow fascinated the world.\n\nShe shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics with her husband and Henri Becquerel, the first woman ever to win one, and after Pierre was killed by a horse-drawn wagon in a Paris street she took over his professorship, the first woman to teach at the Sorbonne. In 1911 she won a second Nobel, this time in Chemistry, and she remains the only person honored in two different sciences. During the First World War she equipped mobile X-ray cars, nicknamed little Curies, and drove them to the front herself.\n\nDecades of handling radioactive material took their toll, and she died in 1934 of a blood disorder linked to radiation. Her laboratory notebooks are still radioactive enough to be stored in lead. Her daughter Irene also won a Nobel in chemistry, and in 1995 she herself was reburied in the Pantheon in Paris, the first woman laid there on her own merits.',
  },
  {
    num: 21, quizId: 'redact-8-26-26', live: '2026-08-26', dateLabel: 'August 26, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 3, answer: 'The Venus Flytrap', aka: {},
    text: 'Most plants draw their nitrogen from the soil. In the wet, sandy, nutrient-poor savannas of the Carolinas, a handful of species solved a shortage of it differently, by catching and digesting animals instead. The best known of them is a low rosette of hinged leaves, each ending in a pair of toothed lobes that shut on an insect in a fraction of a second, among the fastest movements known in the plant kingdom. Charles Darwin, who devoted a whole book to plants that eat animals, called it one of the most wonderful plants in the world.\n\nEach trap is a leaf that ends in two reddish lobes fringed with stiff bristles that interlock like fingers when it closes. On the inner surface sit tiny trigger hairs, and the plant can count: a single touch is ignored, but two touches within about twenty seconds slam the trap shut in a tenth of a second, fast enough to catch a fly, spider, or beetle. Further struggling seals the lobes into a green stomach that floods with digestive juices, dissolving the soft parts of the prey over about a week before the trap reopens and the empty husk blows away. Each trap works only a handful of times before it blackens and is replaced.\n\nThe reason for this elaborate machinery is poor soil. The plant grows wild only in a small patch of damp pine savanna and bog within roughly a hundred miles of Wilmington, North Carolina, spilling just over the border into South Carolina, where the sandy ground is starved of nitrogen. Eating insects supplies the nutrients the dirt cannot. It flowers on tall stalks in spring, holding its blossoms far above the traps so pollinators are not eaten.\n\nDespite its Hollywood reputation, magnified by a certain man-eating musical, the traps are barely larger than a postage stamp and harmless to people. Poaching from the wild is a felony in its home counties, and conservationists count the remaining wild populations in the low hundreds of thousands.',
  },
  {
    num: 22, quizId: 'redact-8-27-26', live: '2026-08-27', dateLabel: 'August 27, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Frida Kahlo', aka: { frieda: 'frida', khalo: 'kahlo' },
    text: 'She was born in 1907 in Coyoacan, on the edge of Mexico City, in the cobalt-blue house her father built and in which she would eventually die. She preferred to give her birth year as 1910, so that her life would begin with the Mexican Revolution. Her father was a photographer of German descent who taught her to handle a camera and to retouch prints, work that trained her eye long before she thought of painting.\n\nPolio damaged her right leg as a child, and at eighteen a streetcar smashed into her school bus, breaking her spine, pelvis, and leg and ending her plan to study medicine. During the long recovery, painting in bed with a mirror mounted overhead, she found her lifelong subject: herself, unsmiling under joined dark brows, surrounded by monkeys, parrots, thorns, and hummingbirds drawn from Mexican folk art. Of her roughly two hundred paintings, more than a third are self-portraits, many depicting her medical ordeals with surgical frankness, her torso opened to show a shattered column or a body held together by straps.\n\nShe married the celebrated muralist Diego Rivera, twenty years her senior, in a stormy union of affairs, divorce, and remarriage that gossip columns called the elephant and the dove. She dressed in the embroidered skirts and ribbons of the Tehuana tradition, turning herself into a walking work of art, hosted Leon Trotsky in her blue house, and exhibited in Paris, where the Louvre bought one of her works, reportedly its first by a twentieth-century Mexican artist.\n\nShe died in 1954, shortly after attending her only solo show in Mexico carried in on a four-poster bed. Long overshadowed by her husband, she was rediscovered by feminists and collectors, and her face now appears on everything from currency to sneakers, an icon of endurance turned into art.',
  },
  {
    num: 23, quizId: 'redact-8-28-26', live: '2026-08-28', dateLabel: 'August 28, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 4, answer: 'The Bayeux Tapestry', aka: { bayeaux: 'bayeux', bayuex: 'bayeux', tapistry: 'tapestry' },
    text: 'It is not a tapestry. A tapestry is woven, with the design formed on the loom; this is an embroidery, worked with woolen thread on a ground of bleached linen in eight colors, dyed from woad, madder and weld. The stitching uses two main techniques, an outline stitch for drawing and a laid and couched filling for blocks of color. Nine lengths of linen were joined end to end to make a strip roughly seventy meters long and half a meter high.\n\nIts survival was not assured. The first firm record of it is an inventory of 1476, in which it is listed among the possessions of a cathedral in Normandy and described as hung around the nave once a year. In 1792 revolutionary volunteers requisitioned the cloth to cover a wagon, and a local official is said to have substituted something else in time. It was carried to Paris for exhibition in 1803, returned, and moved again for safekeeping during the Second World War.\n\nThe Bayeux Tapestry takes its name from that cathedral town, where it is still kept, in a museum a short walk away. French tradition long credited it to the wife of William the Conqueror, but most scholars now think it was commissioned by his half brother Odo, bishop of Bayeux, and worked in England, possibly by embroiderers in Kent, within about a generation of the events it shows. Those events are the disputed succession to the English throne and the invasion of 1066, told in fifty-eight scenes with short captions in Latin.\n\nThe borders carry beasts, fables and, in the battle scenes, the dead. A comet appears above a newly crowned king, generally identified with the return of the object later named for Edmond Halley. The most argued figure is a soldier clutching at an arrow near his eye, beside a caption naming Harold; whether that figure is Harold, and whether the arrow was added by a later restorer, has been debated for over a century. The final panel is missing.',
  },
  {
    num: 24, quizId: 'redact-8-29-26', live: '2026-08-29', dateLabel: 'August 29, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Mansa Musa', aka: { munsa: 'mansa', mannsa: 'mansa', moosa: 'musa' },
    text: 'Almost nothing about him survives in his own words. What is known comes from Arabic authors who wrote at a distance: an Egyptian official who interviewed people in Cairo about a dozen years after the visit, a Moroccan traveler who reached the region in the following generation, and a historian writing later in the same century. To these written sources oral historians of the western Sudan add a body of tradition transmitted for centuries, which preserves genealogy and reputation more reliably than dates.\n\nThe visit was a pilgrimage. In 1324 the ruler of a large West African state crossed the Sahara toward Mecca with a caravan whose size later accounts inflate freely, though all agree it was enormous and that it carried a great deal of gold. He spent and gave away so much in Cairo that, according to the Egyptian official\'s informants, the price of gold there was depressed for years afterward. He borrowed heavily on the return leg, and the loans were repaid at unfavorable rates.\n\nThe pilgrim was Mansa Musa, who ruled the empire of Mali from about 1312 to about 1337. Mansa is a title meaning roughly lord or king in the Manding languages. His empire straddled the upper Niger and controlled the trade in gold from the south and salt from the desert. He brought back with him an Andalusian poet and architect, and mosques and a palace were built at Timbuktu and Gao during or shortly after his reign, including the Djinguereber mosque begun in 1327.\n\nThe pilgrimage made him known in Europe. A Majorcan chart of 1375 shows a crowned figure enthroned south of the Sahara holding a gold nugget, with a legend describing him as the richest of that country\'s kings. Modern lists that rank him as the wealthiest individual in history rest on no accounting that can be checked, since the empire\'s revenues were never recorded in a form that converts. What the sources support is that his wealth astonished contemporaries who were not easily astonished.',
  },
  {
    num: 25, quizId: 'redact-8-30-26', live: '2026-08-30', dateLabel: 'August 30, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 5, answer: 'The Voynich Manuscript', aka: { voynitch: 'voynich', vojnich: 'voynich' },
    text: 'A Polish book dealer bought a chest of old volumes from a Jesuit college outside Rome in 1912, and among them was a small codex of roughly two hundred and forty vellum pages that he could not identify. Its text is written in a flowing, looped script found nowhere else, running left to right in an alphabet of two dozen or so characters, with almost no visible corrections anywhere in it. Every attempt at decipherment since has failed, including by codebreakers who cracked military ciphers for a living.\n\nThe text alone would be puzzle enough, but the pictures deepen the strangeness. Page after page shows plants that match no known species, their roots and flowers combined as if assembled from parts. Other sections offer zodiac wheels and astronomical diagrams, rows of small naked women bathing in green liquid flowing through systems of connected tubs and pipes, pharmaceutical jars beside plant clippings, and dense pages of starred paragraphs that read like recipes. Scholars conventionally divide it into herbal, astronomical, balneological, and pharmaceutical sections while admitting they cannot actually read a word of any of them.\n\nCarbon dating places the calfskin in the early fifteenth century, and its known trail runs through the court of the Holy Roman Emperor Rudolf the Second, a Prague alchemist, and a Jesuit library, before the rare-book dealer Wilfrid Voynich bought it from an Italian villa in 1912 and gave the object its modern name. It now rests in the Beinecke Rare Book and Manuscript Library at Yale University.\n\nThe roll call of failed codebreakers includes the teams that cracked wartime ciphers, modern computer scientists, and countless amateurs whose confident solutions arrive every year and convince no one. Statistical studies show the text behaves like a real language, with word frequencies unlike random gibberish, keeping alive every theory from lost tongue to elaborate hoax, and the book remains exactly what it has always been: unread.',
  },
  {
    num: 26, quizId: 'redact-8-31-26', live: '2026-08-31', dateLabel: 'August 31, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'Mount Everest', aka: { everast: 'everest', evrest: 'everest' },
    text: 'The measurement came first. Surveyors of the Great Trigonometrical Survey of India, working with theodolites from stations on the plains more than a hundred miles away, could not enter the closed kingdom to the north, so they calculated the heights of its peaks by triangulation. In 1852 the computers of the survey office reported that the peak then catalogued as XV was higher than any other. The result was checked for years and announced in 1856 as twenty nine thousand and two feet.\n\nIn 1865 the Royal Geographical Society named the peak for Sir George Everest, a former Surveyor General who had retired some years earlier. He objected. His own rule had been to use local names where they existed, and he pointed out that Mount Everest could not be written easily in the languages of the region. Local names did exist: Chomolungma in Tibetan, and Sagarmatha, adopted in Nepali in the twentieth century. The English name was fixed by then and has not moved.\n\nThe mountain stands on the border between Nepal and Tibet, in a range thrown up by the collision of the Indian plate with Asia, a process still under way. Its summit rock is marine limestone, laid down on an ancient sea floor. After a joint survey the governments of Nepal and China announced an agreed height of eight thousand eight hundred and forty eight metres in 2020. Above about eight thousand metres the air holds too little oxygen to sustain life for long, a zone climbers cross rather than inhabit.\n\nBritish expeditions approached from the north in the 1920s, when Nepal was closed and Tibet was not. George Mallory and Andrew Irvine disappeared high on the mountain in 1924, and whether they reached the top is still argued. The first confirmed ascent was made on 29 May 1953 by Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay, by the southern route through the Khumbu Icefall. Guided commercial climbing followed from the 1990s, along with crowded seasons and continuing debate over the work asked of Sherpa staff.',
  },
  {
    num: 27, quizId: 'redact-9-1-26', live: '2026-09-01', dateLabel: 'September 1, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Jane Austen', aka: { austin: 'austen' },
    text: 'Jane Austen was an English novelist whose six completed books, published two centuries ago, remain among the most read, adapted, and loved fiction in the language. She was born in 1775 in the Hampshire village of Steventon, the seventh of eight children of a country rector, and lived her whole life inside the close world her novels describe: parsonages, country houses, seaside resorts, and the marriage market of the rural gentry.\n\nShe wrote from her teens, drafting early versions of her most famous stories in her twenties, but publication came late and anonymously. Her books appeared with the byline By a Lady, and though the Prince Regent kept a set and requested a dedication, her name never appeared on a title page in her lifetime. The novels earned her modest sums and a quiet reputation for a new kind of fiction: stories with no castles or ghosts, only the comedy and moral drama of ordinary social life, told with an irony so fine readers are still arguing over her meanings.\n\nHer masterpiece opens with perhaps the most quoted first sentence in English fiction, a truth universally acknowledged about single men, fortunes, and wives, and its sparring couple have been reincarnated in countless films, from wet-shirted television adaptations to Bollywood musicals and a zombie parody. Her other books trace sense against sensibility, persuasion and second chances, and a heroine who imagines herself a matchmaker and is wrong about nearly everything.\n\nShe died in 1817 at forty-one, probably of a then-unnamed illness, and was buried in Winchester Cathedral under a stone that praised her charity and never mentioned her writing. Her fame has only compounded since: societies meet in Regency dress, and her portrait now appears on the British ten-pound note.',
  },
  {
    num: 28, quizId: 'redact-9-2-26', live: '2026-09-02', dateLabel: 'September 2, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Johannes Gutenberg', aka: { gutenburg: 'gutenberg', guttenberg: 'gutenberg' },
    text: 'Printing from carved wooden blocks was already old in Europe, and movable type had been used in China and Korea for centuries before anyone in the West attempted it. What was missing was a way to make thousands of identical letters quickly, cheaply, and to a tolerance fine enough that a page of them would sit flat under pressure. The craftsman who solved that problem was born around 1400 in the Rhineland city of Mainz, trained in metalworking and gem cutting, and spent years in Strasbourg on secretive ventures, including polished mirrors for pilgrims, before returning home.\n\nHis system was a marriage of existing crafts and new precision: individual letters cast in a lead alloy from copper molds, each one reusable and interchangeable, arranged into pages, inked with a new oil-based formula, and pressed onto paper with a machine adapted from the screw presses used for wine and olives. Where a monastery scribe might spend years copying a single volume, his workshop could pull hundreds of identical pages a day.\n\nIts masterpiece was a Latin Bible completed around 1455, printed in two fat volumes with forty-two lines to the page, its type mimicking manuscript calligraphy so well that buyers added hand-painted decorations as if it were scribal work. Roughly one hundred eighty copies were made, and the four dozen or so that survive are among the most valuable books on Earth. Triumph brought ruin: his financier Johann Fust sued him just as the Bible neared completion, and court judgment stripped him of his press and types. He died in 1468, modestly pensioned by the local archbishop, without ever putting his own name on a printed book.\n\nThe technology spread down the Rhine and across Europe within a generation, multiplying books by the millions, fueling the Renaissance and the Reformation, and setting off the media age in which everything since has happened.',
  },
  {
    num: 29, quizId: 'redact-9-3-26', live: '2026-09-03', dateLabel: 'September 3, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 3, answer: 'The Galápagos Islands', aka: { galapagoes: 'galapagos' },
    text: 'A volcanic hotspot beneath the Pacific has been pushing up cones along the equator for millions of years, some six hundred miles west of the South American mainland. The land it built was never joined to any continent, so everything living there arrived by accident: seeds and insects on the wind, reptiles rafting on mats of vegetation, birds blown off course. Those castaways bred in isolation, and many diverged into forms found nowhere else on Earth.\n\nThe results are legendary. Giant tortoises weighing hundreds of pounds, whose domed or saddle-backed shells differ island by island, gave the archipelago its Spanish name. Marine iguanas, the only seagoing lizards in the world, graze algae underwater and sneeze salt. Cormorants here have lost the power of flight, tiny penguins live farther north than any of their kind, and boobies with startling blue feet dance for mates. Because large predators never arrived, the animals show almost no fear of people.\n\nA young naturalist named Charles Darwin spent five weeks among the islands in 1835 aboard the survey ship Beagle, collecting mockingbirds and finches whose subtle differences from island to island later helped seed his theory of evolution by natural selection, published decades afterward in On the Origin of Species. The finches that bear his name, whose beaks range from seed-crackers to tool users, remain a textbook example of adaptation, still measured by resident scientists as droughts visibly reshape their beaks within a few generations.\n\nDeclared Ecuador\'s first national park in 1959 and the first site ever inscribed on the World Heritage list in 1978, the islands now balance a booming population of visitors against invasive goats, rats, and blackberry vines, with strict quotas, quarantines, and guides required nearly everywhere ashore.',
  },
  {
    num: 30, quizId: 'redact-9-4-26', live: '2026-09-04', dateLabel: 'September 4, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 4, answer: 'The Dead Sea Scrolls', aka: { scrols: 'scrolls', scroles: 'scrolls' },
    text: 'Most of them are scraps. Of the roughly nine hundred manuscripts recovered from eleven caves in a limestone cliff, only a handful came out whole; the rest arrived as tens of thousands of fragments, many no larger than a fingernail, which scholars have spent decades sorting by handwriting, ruling, skin thickness and the shape of a torn edge. The material is mostly prepared animal skin, with some papyrus, and one text is engraved on sheets of hammered copper.\n\nThe first of them reached scholars by an unlikely route. According to the account generally given, a young herdsman looking for a stray goat in 1947 found jars in a cave and took the contents to dealers in Bethlehem. Some passed to a Syrian archbishop, who eventually advertised four of them for sale in an American newspaper in 1954; they were bought, through an intermediary, on behalf of the state of Israel, and reunited with three others that a Jerusalem archaeologist had acquired directly.\n\nThe caves lie near Qumran, on the northwestern shore of the Dead Sea, and the collection is known as the Dead Sea Scrolls. The texts are written mainly in Hebrew, with some Aramaic and a little Greek, and are usually dated between the third century BCE and the first century CE. About a fifth are copies of books of the Hebrew Bible; every book except Esther is represented in some form. The rest include commentaries, psalms, calendars, and rules for a community that many scholars associate with the Essenes described by Josephus.\n\nTheir importance is chronological. Before 1947 the oldest substantial Hebrew manuscripts of the biblical books dated from around the tenth century CE; the best preserved scroll of Isaiah is roughly a thousand years older, and its text is close to the later standard, though not identical. Publication was slow and became a scandal: a small committee held exclusive rights for decades, and the full corpus only became generally available after 1991. Most of the material is now in Jerusalem.',
  },
  {
    num: 31, quizId: 'redact-9-5-26', live: '2026-09-05', dateLabel: 'September 5, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Mary Anning', aka: { aning: 'anning', annin: 'anning', annig: 'anning' },
    text: 'In August 1800, according to the story her family told, a lightning strike killed three women standing under a tree at an outdoor show in a small coastal town in the south of England. One of them was holding a baby girl of fifteen months, who was carried home and revived in a bath of warm water. The child had been listless before, the neighbors said, and was quick and curious afterward.\n\nHer father was a cabinetmaker who supplemented his income by collecting curiosities from the crumbling cliffs nearby and selling them to visitors. He died in 1810, leaving the family in debt, and the trade passed to his children. Within two years they had found and extracted the skull and then the skeleton of a large marine reptile from the shale, which was bought by a local landowner and eventually reached London, where it was described in print without their names attached.\n\nMary Anning worked those cliffs, at Lyme Regis in Dorset, for the rest of her life, and at some risk: a landslip killed her dog beside her. She found the first reasonably complete plesiosaur in 1823, so unlike any known animal that Georges Cuvier at first suspected a forgery and then publicly accepted it, and the first pterosaur found outside Germany in 1828. She taught herself anatomy and geology, read the papers of the men who bought from her, and dissected modern fish for comparison.\n\nShe could not join the Geological Society of London, which did not admit women until 1904, and her name appears in few of the papers her specimens made possible. In 1838 the British Association arranged a small annuity for her, and when she died of breast cancer in 1847, at forty-seven, the society\'s president read a eulogy to its members, an unusual gesture for someone who was not a fellow. Her work on the coprolites, with William Buckland, helped establish what the animals had eaten.',
  },
  {
    num: 32, quizId: 'redact-9-6-26', live: '2026-09-06', dateLabel: 'September 6, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 5, answer: 'Gobekli Tepe', aka: { gobeklitepe: 'gobekli', gobelki: 'gobekli', goebekli: 'gobekli' },
    text: 'In 1963 a joint survey team from Istanbul University and the University of Chicago walked over a low mound in the dry hills of the upper Euphrates basin and wrote it down as unremarkable. The field notes recorded broken slabs of limestone poking through the surface and suggested the rise was a medieval cemetery, the stones its grave markers. The report was published and the mound was left alone for thirty years. In 1994 Klaus Schmidt, a German archaeologist looking for early Neolithic settlement in the region, read the entry and thought the description of dressed limestone sounded wrong for a graveyard. He visited the following season and began excavating in 1995.\n\nWhat the trenches exposed were circular and oval enclosures walled in rough stone, each ringed with pillars shaped like the letter T and cut from the limestone bedrock nearby. The largest stand more than five metres tall and are estimated to weigh several tons apiece; two of the tallest occupy the centre of each enclosure, facing one another. Many carry carved reliefs: foxes, boars, snakes, scorpions, vultures, cranes and wild cattle, along with arms, hands and belts that suggest the pillars were meant to be read as stylised human figures. Others are plain. An unfinished pillar still lies in a quarry a few hundred metres away.\n\nRadiocarbon dates place the earliest construction in the Pre-Pottery Neolithic, roughly between 9500 and 8000 BCE, which makes Gobekli Tepe older than pottery, metal tools, writing and, on current evidence, farming. The site lies in southeastern Turkey, a short distance from the city of Sanliurfa, and its modern name is Turkish for potbelly hill. Schmidt argued that the enclosures were built by hunter-gatherers as a place of gathering and ritual rather than a settlement, and that the effort of raising them may have encouraged, rather than followed, the shift to agriculture.\n\nThat interpretation has been qualified since his death in 2014. Later excavation has found domestic buildings, grinding stones and water cisterns on the mound, and many researchers now describe an occupied place with monumental architecture rather than a sanctuary standing alone. Whether the enclosures were deliberately buried, as Schmidt believed, is also debated. Excavation continues, alongside work at related sites nearby such as Karahan Tepe, and the mound was added to the World Heritage list in 2018.',
  },
  {
    num: 33, quizId: 'redact-9-7-26', live: '2026-09-07', dateLabel: 'September 7, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Berlin Wall', aka: {},
    text: 'The Berlin Wall was the fortified barrier that divided a great European capital for twenty-eight years and became the defining symbol of the Cold War. After the Second World War the defeated German capital, though it lay deep inside the Soviet occupation zone, was itself split among the victorious powers, and by 1961 the eastern government was hemorrhaging citizens: millions had simply walked into the western sectors and flown out to a new life. In the early hours of August 13, 1961, soldiers unrolled barbed wire across streets, tram lines, and even cemeteries, and the concrete followed within days.\n\nWhat began as wire grew into a layered killing zone nearly a hundred miles around the western half of the city: an outer wall, a floodlit strip of raked sand nicknamed the death strip, tripwires, dog runs, watchtowers manned by guards with shoot-to-kill orders, and finally the smooth twelve-foot inner wall topped with a rounded pipe to defeat climbing fingers. Crossing was allowed only at controlled points, the most famous being the Allied post known as Checkpoint Charlie. Well over a hundred people died trying to get past it, leaping from windows, swimming canals, tunneling from basements, and in one case flying a homemade balloon.\n\nAn American president declared himself a citizen of the divided city in 1963, and another stood at the Brandenburg Gate in 1987 and demanded that his Soviet counterpart tear the barrier down. The end came suddenly: on the night of November 9, 1989, after a botched press conference about eased travel rules, crowds surged to the crossings and bewildered guards opened the gates. Berliners danced on the concrete and chipped it apart with hammers. Germany reunified within a year, and painted fragments of the barrier now sit in museums and plazas on every continent.',
  },
  {
    num: 34, quizId: 'redact-9-8-26', live: '2026-09-08', dateLabel: 'September 8, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Panama Canal', aka: {},
    text: 'The Panama Canal is the fifty-mile waterway across the narrow waist of Central America that joins the Atlantic and Pacific oceans, sparing ships the eight-thousand-mile voyage around the tip of South America. It is not a sea-level ditch but a water staircase: locks at each end lift vessels some eighty-five feet up to a vast artificial lake, created by damming the Chagres River, and lower them again on the far side. Each lock chamber fills by gravity alone, draining millions of gallons of fresh water to the sea with every transit.\n\nA French company under Ferdinand de Lesseps, fresh from triumph at Suez, began digging in 1881 and met catastrophe: yellow fever and malaria killed workers by the thousands, the jungle rains ate the excavations, and the venture collapsed in a scandal that ruined investors across France. The United States took over in 1904, after backing the province of Panama in its split from Colombia to secure the right to build. Army doctors led by William Gorgas attacked the mosquitoes first, draining and fumigating until the fevers loosened their grip, while steam shovels gnawed through the continental divide at the Culebra Cut, where landslides kept undoing months of work.\n\nThe first ship steamed through in August 1914, just as war broke out in Europe, capping what is still called one of the seven wonders of the modern world. Almost as remarkable was the handover: under treaties signed by President Carter in 1977, the waterway passed peacefully to Panamanian control at the end of 1999, and it now supplies a major share of the small nation\'s economy.\n\nExpanded in 2016 with a third set of locks for giant container ships, it handles over thirteen thousand transits a year, though recent droughts have forced managers to ration the fresh water that makes every crossing possible.',
  },
  {
    num: 35, quizId: 'redact-9-9-26', live: '2026-09-09', dateLabel: 'September 9, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Nikola Tesla', aka: { nicola: 'nikola', nickola: 'nikola', tesler: 'tesla' },
    text: 'In the summer of 1884 a Serbian engineer stepped off a boat in New York carrying a letter of introduction to Thomas Edison and, by his own later account, a few coins and some poetry. He was set to work improving the direct current dynamos that lit lower Manhattan, quarreled with his employer over payment, and left within a year. The dispute mattered beyond the two men, because the engineer already believed that alternating current, which Edison distrusted, was the practical way to move electricity over distance.\n\nNikola Tesla was born in 1856 in the village of Smiljan, then part of the Austrian Empire and now in Croatia. He studied engineering at Graz and worked for telephone and electrical companies in Budapest and Paris before emigrating. In 1888 he patented a practical induction motor driven by polyphase alternating current, and George Westinghouse licensed the patents. The system was shown at the Chicago world\'s fair of 1893 and used for the generating station at Niagara Falls that began delivering power in 1895.\n\nHe also built the resonant transformer that carries his name, capable of producing very high voltages at high frequency, and used it in experiments with wireless transmission at a laboratory in Colorado Springs. With backing from J. P. Morgan he began a tower at Wardenclyffe on Long Island, intending to send messages and eventually power without wires. The money ran out, the tower was never finished, and it was demolished in 1917.\n\nHis later years were spent in New York hotels, where he gave interviews on the anniversaries of his birth, announced inventions he did not build, and fed the pigeons in nearby parks. He died in 1943, in debt and largely out of public view. The unit of magnetic flux density was named the tesla in 1960, and interest in his work revived later in the century, sometimes to the point of exaggeration, with claims made on his behalf that the record does not support.',
  },
  {
    num: 36, quizId: 'redact-9-10-26', live: '2026-09-10', dateLabel: 'September 10, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 3, answer: 'The Hindenburg Disaster', aka: { hindenberg: 'hindenburg', hindenburgh: 'hindenburg', disater: 'disaster', dissaster: 'disaster' },
    text: 'Among the largest aircraft ever flown, the German passenger airship of 1936 was designed to float on helium, an inert gas that does not burn. Almost all of the world\'s supply came from the United States, which restricted its export under a law of 1927, so the builders filled the ship with hydrogen instead. It crossed the Atlantic more than thirty times in its first season, carrying passengers in cabins with a dining room, a lounge, and a smoking room sealed off from the gas cells.\n\nThe Hindenburg Disaster took place on 6 May 1937 at the Naval Air Station in Lakehurst, New Jersey. The ship had crossed from Frankfurt and was delayed by thunderstorms before it approached the mooring mast in the early evening. As the landing lines were dropped, fire appeared near the stern, spread along the hull within seconds, and the airship settled to the ground in flames. Of the ninety seven people aboard, thirty five died, along with one member of the ground crew.\n\nBecause the arrival had been advertised, reporters and newsreel cameras were waiting, and the radio account recorded by Herbert Morrison became one of the most replayed broadcasts of the century. American and German inquiries agreed that hydrogen leaking from the ship had been ignited, most likely by an electrical discharge in the storm charged air, though the exact source has been argued over ever since. A later theory blaming the flammable coating on the outer fabric has not persuaded most investigators.\n\nCommercial passenger service by airship ended almost immediately. The older Graf Zeppelin was retired within weeks, its intended successor never entered scheduled service, and both were eventually broken up for scrap. Long distance passenger travel by air passed to the flying boat and then to the landplane. Airships survive today in small numbers for advertising and observation, filled with helium, and the events at Lakehurst are still raised whenever a new lighter than air project is proposed.',
  },
  {
    num: 37, quizId: 'redact-9-11-26', live: '2026-09-11', dateLabel: 'September 11, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 4, answer: 'Krakatoa', aka: { krakatoe: 'krakatoa', crakatoa: 'krakatoa', krakatowa: 'krakatoa' },
    text: 'The most detailed account of what happened was assembled by a mining engineer in the colonial service, who spent two years measuring ash layers, interviewing survivors and charting the seabed where an island had been. His report, published in 1885, worked out the sequence of events from physical evidence rather than testimony alone, and it is often called the first modern monograph on a volcanic eruption. He also predicted that a new cone would eventually appear in the flooded caldera.\n\nThe island lay in the Sunda Strait, the channel separating Java from Sumatra, and was uninhabited and wooded, with three cones on a low platform that was itself the rim of a much older collapse. Activity resumed in May 1883 after a long quiet period and continued intermittently through the summer. On 26 and 27 August the eruption reached its climax in four immense explosions, the last of which destroyed most of the island and dropped the sea floor where it had stood.\n\nKrakatoa, spelled Krakatau in Indonesian, killed most of its victims by water rather than fire. Waves reported at up to thirty meters swept the shores of the strait and destroyed the town of Merak and dozens of villages; the official death toll recorded by the colonial administration was 36,417, and some estimates run higher. The sound was heard at Rodrigues in the Indian Ocean, thousands of miles away, and the pressure wave was traced on barographs around the world for several days as it circled the planet.\n\nFine ash stayed in the upper atmosphere for months, producing vivid sunsets in Europe and North America and a modest global cooling the following year. The remnant cone of Rakata survived as a cliff, and in 1927 a new island broke the surface inside the caldera, as the engineer had expected. It has grown and collapsed repeatedly since; in December 2018 a flank of it slid into the sea and generated a tsunami that killed more than four hundred people on the same two coasts.',
  },
  {
    num: 38, quizId: 'redact-9-12-26', live: '2026-09-12', dateLabel: 'September 12, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 4, answer: 'The Tunguska Event', aka: { tunguzka: 'tunguska', tungaska: 'tunguska', tungusca: 'tunguska' },
    text: 'Nineteen years passed before anyone with scientific training reached the place. The first expedition, led by a mineralogist from the Soviet academy, set out in 1927 expecting to find a crater and a mass of meteoric iron. Instead it found swamp, and trees: about eighty million of them thrown flat over an area of some two thousand square kilometers, lying in a radial pattern that pointed back toward a center where the trunks were still standing, stripped of branches and bark.\n\nThe morning in question was 30 June 1908. Herders scattered across the taiga described a column of blue light moving across the sky, brighter than the sun, followed by a flash, a heat so strong that one man at a trading post about sixty kilometers away said his shirt seemed to catch fire, and then a series of concussions that knocked him from his chair. Barographs in England registered the pressure wave, and seismic stations across Eurasia recorded the shock.\n\nThe Tunguska Event takes its name from the Podkamennaya Tunguska, the river whose basin it flattened, in what is now Krasnoyarsk Krai in central Siberia. The absence of a crater is the central fact: no fragment of the body has ever been securely identified, and the accepted explanation is that a stone asteroid, or possibly a fragment of a comet, tens of meters across, broke up and released its energy in the air several kilometers above the ground. Estimates of that energy vary widely, most falling in the range of several megatons.\n\nBecause the area was almost empty, no death is firmly documented, though reindeer herds were destroyed and at least one herder is reported to have died of injuries. For several nights afterward the skies over northern Europe were bright enough to read by, an effect usually attributed to dust in the upper atmosphere. The site has been surveyed many times since, and one team proposed in 2007 that a nearby lake is an impact crater, a claim other researchers reject.',
  },
  {
    num: 39, quizId: 'redact-9-13-26', live: '2026-09-13', dateLabel: 'September 13, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 5, answer: 'The Codex Gigas', aka: { codexgigas: 'codex', gigus: 'gigas' },
    text: 'By the end of the thirteenth century the small Benedictine house at Podlazice, in eastern Bohemia, was heavily in debt, and it settled part of what it owed by handing over the one object of real value it owned. The book passed in 1295 to the Cistercians at Sedlec, who sold it on to the Benedictine abbey at Brevnov. It reached Prague three centuries later, in the collection of the Emperor Rudolf II. In 1648, in the closing months of the Thirty Years War, a Swedish army looted the imperial castle and shipped the volume north to Stockholm, where it has stayed ever since.\n\nIt is the largest medieval manuscript known to survive. The bound volume stands roughly ninety two centimetres tall and half a metre wide, and weighs about seventy five kilograms. Its three hundred and ten surviving leaves were prepared from the skins of an estimated one hundred and sixty animals, usually described as donkeys or calves. The script is remarkably even from the first page to the last, and most scholars accept that a single hand wrote the whole book, probably over two or three decades in the first half of the thirteenth century.\n\nThe name The Codex Gigas is a later coinage and means simply the giant book. The contents amount to a monastic library bound as one object. They open with the complete Latin Vulgate Bible, into which two histories by Josephus have been inserted, and continue with the Etymologiae of Isidore of Seville, a group of medical treatises, and the Chronicle of the Bohemians by Cosmas of Prague. At the back are a calendar and a necrology, a penitential, and a set of formulas for exorcism.\n\nOne page carries a full length figure of the devil, far larger than any other illustration in the book, and facing it is a view of the heavenly city. That image gave rise to the popular name and to the tradition, first recorded long after the fact, that a monk sentenced to be walled up alive wrote the whole volume in a single night with the devil\'s help. The book survived a fire at the royal castle in Stockholm in 1697, reportedly by being thrown from a window. It is now held by the National Library of Sweden, which has published photographs of every page online.',
  },
  {
    num: 40, quizId: 'redact-9-14-26', live: '2026-09-14', dateLabel: 'September 14, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 2, answer: 'Chess', aka: {},
    text: 'Chess is the most studied board game on Earth, a war between two armies of sixteen pieces on a checkered board of sixty-four squares, played everywhere from park benches to world championship stages. Each side commands a king, a queen, two rooks, two bishops, two knights, and a row of eight pawns, and the object is not capture but checkmate: trapping the enemy king with no legal escape.\n\nThe game descends from chaturanga, played in India around the sixth century, whose four ancient divisions of infantry, cavalry, chariots, and elephants survive as the modern pawns, knights, rooks, and bishops. It traveled through Persia, where players warned the enemy king with the word shah, the root of the modern winning announcement, and Arab conquest carried it across the Islamic world into Europe. Around the fifteenth century, European players supercharged the rules, transforming the once-feeble counselor beside the king into the most powerful piece on the board, a change some historians link to the reign of mighty queens like Isabella of Castile.\n\nThe romantic age of sacrificial attacks gave way to professional rigor, world championships from 1886 onward, and Soviet domination for most of the twentieth century, interrupted by the American Bobby Fischer in 1972 in a match watched like a heavyweight fight. In 1997 a machine finally beat a reigning world champion when the computer Deep Blue defeated Garry Kasparov, and today engines on an ordinary phone outplay any human alive, while grandmasters use them as training partners.\n\nThe game has never been bigger: online platforms host hundreds of millions of accounts, streamers fill arenas, and a pandemic-era television drama about a fictional prodigy sent sales of sets soaring around the world.',
  },
  {
    num: 41, quizId: 'redact-9-15-26', live: '2026-09-15', dateLabel: 'September 15, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Harry Houdini', aka: { houdinni: 'houdini' },
    text: 'Harry Houdini was the most famous escape artist who ever lived, a magician whose name became, and remains, a common noun for anyone who slips out of an impossible spot. He was born Erik Weisz in Budapest in 1874, the son of a rabbi who moved the family to Wisconsin and then New York, where the boy worked as a necktie cutter and trapeze performer before taking a stage name honoring the French conjurer Robert-Houdin.\n\nAfter years of struggling in dime museums and circuses, he found his hook: challenge acts. He invited any police force to lock him up, and out he came, from handcuffs, leg irons, straitjackets, prison vans, and the jail cells of skeptical chiefs across America and Europe. The escapes grew ever more theatrical: hanging upside down from newspaper buildings in a straitjacket before crowds of thousands, buried alive, sealed in a galvanized coffin underwater, nailed into packing crates dropped into rivers, and his signature torture cell, in which he was lowered head first, ankles clamped, into a tank of water in full view of the audience.\n\nOffstage he was equally restless: an early aviator credited with one of the first controlled powered flights in Australia, a silent-film action star, a collector of magic history, and, after his beloved mother died, a ferocious debunker of spirit mediums. He exposed seance tricks from theater stages and testified before Congress, which ended his friendship with the credulous creator of Sherlock Holmes.\n\nHe died on Halloween 1926 of a ruptured appendix, days after taking surprise punches to the stomach from a student testing his famous iron midsection. For a decade his widow held annual seances awaiting a coded message he promised to send back. It never came.',
  },
  {
    num: 42, quizId: 'redact-9-16-26', live: '2026-09-16', dateLabel: 'September 16, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 3, answer: 'Machu Picchu', aka: { pichu: 'picchu', piccu: 'picchu', machupicchu: 'picchu', matchu: 'machu' },
    text: 'In July 1911 a lecturer from Yale named Hiram Bingham was led up a forested ridge in southern Peru by a local farmer and a small boy. He was searching for Vilcabamba, the refuge from which the last native rulers of the Andes had resisted Spanish rule, and he believed for years that he had found it. He had not, though the ruins he cleared and photographed, with support from the National Geographic Society, became far better known than the place he had gone looking for.\n\nMachu Picchu occupies a saddle between two peaks about eighty kilometres northwest of Cusco, some two thousand four hundred metres above sea level and high above the Urubamba river. Most scholars date its construction to the middle of the fifteenth century and connect it with Pachacuti, the ruler under whom the Inca state expanded rapidly. They generally regard the site as a royal estate rather than a town, occupied by a few hundred people, many of them attendants and craft specialists brought from elsewhere.\n\nThe masonry is the most admired feature. Blocks of granite were shaped so precisely that the finest walls need no mortar, and the buildings sit on terraces cut into a steep slope, underpinned by layers of rubble and gravel that carry away the heavy rain. Among the structures are a curved building often called the Temple of the Sun, a carved outcrop known as the Intihuatana, and a quarter of workshops and dwellings separated from the ceremonial buildings by a long plaza.\n\nThe site appears to have been abandoned in the sixteenth century, around the time of the Spanish conquest, and there is no evidence that Spanish forces ever reached it. Local people knew of the ruins long before Bingham arrived; his contribution was publicity, and a large collection of artifacts that Peru pressed Yale for decades to return. It was inscribed as a World Heritage property in 1983, and visitor numbers are now capped and routed along timed circuits.',
  },
  {
    num: 43, quizId: 'redact-9-17-26', live: '2026-09-17', dateLabel: 'September 17, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Alan Turing', aka: { turring: 'turing', tureing: 'turing', allan: 'alan' },
    text: 'In 1939 the British Government Code and Cypher School moved from London to a Victorian country house in Buckinghamshire called Bletchley Park. One of its recruits, a Cambridge mathematician not yet thirty, was assigned to the German naval cipher and worked in the wooden building known as Hut 8. Building on earlier analysis by Polish cryptanalysts, he and Gordon Welchman designed an electromechanical machine, the bombe, which tested candidate settings of the Enigma cipher machine and rejected those that produced a contradiction.\n\nAlan Turing was born in London in 1912 and read mathematics at King\'s College, Cambridge. In a paper of 1936 on a question posed by David Hilbert, he described an abstract device that reads and writes symbols on a tape according to a table of rules, and showed that no such device can decide in general whether an arbitrary program will ever halt. The idea of a single universal machine able to imitate any other became a foundation of computer science.\n\nAfter the war he worked on the design of a stored program computer at the National Physical Laboratory and then at Manchester. A paper published in 1950 opened with the question of whether machines can think and proposed replacing it with a test of conversation, now usually named after him. In 1952 he published a mathematical account of how chemical reactions spreading through tissue might generate stripes, spots and other patterns in living things.\n\nThat same year he was prosecuted for homosexual acts, then a criminal offence in Britain, and accepted hormone treatment as an alternative to prison. He lost his security clearance. He died in 1954 of cyanide poisoning, and the inquest recorded suicide, though some biographers have questioned that verdict. The British government apologized in 2009, a royal pardon followed in 2013, and his portrait was placed on the fifty pound note in 2021. The principal prize in computing carries his name.',
  },
  {
    num: 44, quizId: 'redact-9-18-26', live: '2026-09-18', dateLabel: 'September 18, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 4, answer: 'Guernica', aka: { guernika: 'guernica', gernica: 'guernica', guarnica: 'guernica' },
    text: 'The canvas is enormous and almost colorless: about three and a half meters high and nearly eight wide, painted in oil in grays, black and white, with a texture in places that imitates newsprint. It was finished in roughly five weeks in an attic studio on a narrow street on the left bank. His companion, the photographer Dora Maar, recorded the stages of the work as it changed.\n\nThe commission came from the Spanish Republic, which wanted a mural for its pavilion at the international exposition of 1937. The painter had accepted in January and spent months on other subjects, including a satirical print sequence about the leader of the rebellion. Reports of an attack in the north reached the city at the end of April, and within days he had abandoned what he had and begun the sketches, more than forty of which survive and are dated.\n\nOn 26 April 1937 aircraft of the German Condor Legion, with Italian support, bombed the Basque town of Guernica on a market day, destroying most of it by fire. Casualty figures were disputed at the time and remain so; estimates have ranged from around one hundred and fifty to well over a thousand. Pablo Picasso called the finished painting Guernica. It contains no aircraft and no bombs: a screaming horse, a bull, a woman holding a dead child, a broken statue of a warrior, a bare electric bulb, and a lamp held in from the right.\n\nThe painting spent the war and the four decades after it abroad, touring to raise money for refugees and then held in New York, on the painter\'s condition that it go to Spain only when public liberties were restored there. It arrived in 1981 and hangs in Madrid. A woven copy hangs at the United Nations in New York, where in 2003 it was covered with a curtain during a press appearance.',
  },
  {
    num: 45, quizId: 'redact-9-19-26', live: '2026-09-19', dateLabel: 'September 19, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 4, answer: 'The Silk Road', aka: { slik: 'silk', roade: 'road' },
    text: 'The name is a modern invention. In 1877 the German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen coined a phrase for the caravan tracks he was mapping across Central Asia, and it passed into English soon afterwards. No merchant of antiquity would have recognized it. Traders spoke instead of the route to a particular oasis town, and almost none of them travelled the whole distance; goods moved in relays, passing from one hand to the next across thousands of miles.\n\nWhat the phrase describes was never a single highway. It was a shifting web of tracks linking oasis towns, mountain passes and grasslands, running north and south of the Taklamakan desert and branching toward India and the Iranian plateau. Camels carried the loads, and caravanserais spaced roughly a day\'s march apart offered water, fodder and shelter. The cargo was varied: horses, jade, glass, furs, paper, spices and slaves moved alongside the bolts of cloth that later gave the network its name.\n\nThe Silk Road, as it is now called, is usually dated from the second century BCE, when the Han court sent the envoy Zhang Qian west and learned of settled kingdoms beyond the steppe. Chinese silk reached the Mediterranean, where Roman writers complained about the cost of it. Traffic ran the other way as well. Sogdian merchants from Samarkand and Bukhara dominated much of the business for centuries, and Buddhism travelled with them from India into China, leaving cave temples at Dunhuang and elsewhere along the way.\n\nOverland traffic declined as sea routes between the Indian Ocean and southern China grew cheaper and safer, a shift well advanced by the fifteenth century. Some scholars argue that the same networks carried disease as well as goods, though the evidence is debated. Rediscovery came through archaeology: expeditions led by Aurel Stein, Sven Hedin and others recovered manuscripts, frescoes and buried towns from the sand in the early twentieth century, and the phrase Richthofen invented has been attached to the region ever since.',
  },
  {
    num: 46, quizId: 'redact-9-20-26', live: '2026-09-20', dateLabel: 'September 20, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Srinivasa Ramanujan', aka: { ramanujam: 'ramanujan', ramanujen: 'ramanujan', srinavasa: 'srinivasa', shrinivasa: 'srinivasa' },
    text: 'Some time around 1903 a teenager in the southern Indian town of Kumbakonam borrowed a book that shaped everything he did afterwards. It was A Synopsis of Elementary Results in Pure and Applied Mathematics, a cramming manual compiled by G. S. Carr, which set out several thousand theorems with little or no proof. Working through it alone, he reconstructed the arguments and carried on past them into results of his own. The method left a permanent mark: for the rest of his life he tended to present conclusions without the demonstrations a trained mathematician would supply.\n\nHe entered the local government college on a scholarship in 1904 and lost it within a year, having neglected every subject but one; a second attempt at a degree in Madras ended the same way. For most of the next decade he lived poorly, tutoring and looking for work, filling notebooks with results in number theory, infinite series and continued fractions. In 1912, with the backing of local officials who could not follow the notebooks but were impressed by them, he was given a clerk\'s post in the accounts office of the Madras Port Trust, which paid little but left him time to write.\n\nIn January 1913 he posted a long letter of results to G. H. Hardy at Trinity College, Cambridge; two similar letters to other English mathematicians had gone unanswered. Hardy found the pages baffling and, after going through them with J. E. Littlewood, decided that the strangest results were too odd to have been invented. Srinivasa Ramanujan sailed for England in 1914 and stayed five years. Their joint work included results on the partition function and on highly composite numbers, and in 1918 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society.\n\nHis health failed in England, where wartime shortages made his strict vegetarian diet hard to keep. Doctors at the time diagnosed tuberculosis; some later writers have argued for a parasitic liver infection instead, and the question is not settled. He returned to India in 1919 and died the following April, aged thirty two. Three notebooks of unproved results survived him, and a further sheaf of pages from his last year was recognised in a Cambridge library in 1976 and is usually called the lost notebook. Mathematicians are still supplying proofs, and the mock theta functions set down in those pages found a general theory only decades later.',
  },
  {
    num: 47, quizId: 'redact-9-21-26', live: '2026-09-21', dateLabel: 'September 21, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Statue of Liberty', aka: { libirty: 'liberty', staue: 'statue' },
    text: 'The skin is thinner than most people expect. Three hundred or so sheets of copper, each around two and a half millimetres thick, were hammered into shape over wooden moulds by a technique used for centuries in metalwork, then hung on an iron skeleton designed by Gustave Eiffel and his engineer Maurice Koechlin. The sheets are not fixed rigidly; the armature lets them shift with wind and temperature. The copper was bright brown when new and took decades to weather to green.\n\nThe idea belonged to a group of French republicans, and the scheme is usually traced to a remark by the jurist Edouard de Laboulaye in the 1860s. The sculptor Frederic Auguste Bartholdi carried it through, raising money by subscription and lottery, exhibiting the finished hand and torch in Philadelphia in 1876 and the head in Paris in 1878. The completed figure was assembled in Paris, taken apart, and shipped across the Atlantic in 1885 in more than two hundred crates.\n\nThe pedestal was the American side of the bargain, and it nearly failed. Richard Morris Hunt designed a granite base on an old star shaped fort in New York Harbor, and the money ran out until Joseph Pulitzer used his newspaper to shame small donors into giving. The Statue of Liberty was dedicated on 28 October 1886 by President Grover Cleveland. Its formal title is Liberty Enlightening the World; the tablet in the left hand bears the date of American independence in Roman numerals.\n\nFor its first years it was run as a lighthouse, though it was of little use to shipping, and that role ended in 1902. Emma Lazarus wrote a sonnet for the pedestal fund in 1883, but its closing lines were only fixed to the base on a plaque in 1903, and the association with immigration grew afterwards. The island took its present name in 1956, and a thorough restoration for the centenary in 1986 replaced the corroded torch with a new one covered in gold leaf.',
  },
  {
    num: 48, quizId: 'redact-9-22-26', live: '2026-09-22', dateLabel: 'September 22, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Great Barrier Reef', aka: { barier: 'barrier', bariar: 'barrier', reaf: 'reef' },
    text: 'Late on the night of 11 June 1770 the Endeavour, working north along the coast of what is now Queensland, struck coral and stuck fast. James Cook had the guns, ballast and stores thrown overboard to lighten her, floated her off on the next high tide, and fothered the hull with a sail to slow the leak. The ship was beached at the mouth of a river that still carries her name, near the site of Cooktown, and lay there seven weeks under repair.\n\nMatthew Flinders, charting the same coast three decades later, wrote of the great barrier that lay offshore, and the phrase settled into use. The Great Barrier Reef runs roughly two thousand three hundred kilometres, from the Torres Strait in the north to the waters off Bundaberg, and is not one structure but some two thousand nine hundred separate reefs together with about nine hundred islands and cays. It sits on the continental shelf, in places more than two hundred kilometres offshore.\n\nThe structure is biological. Colonies of stony coral, each animal a polyp a few millimetres across, deposit skeletons of calcium carbonate and build on the remains of earlier colonies, so that the living layer is thin and everything beneath it is rock. Most estimates put the growth of the present formation over the last eight to ten thousand years. It supports roughly four hundred species of coral and about fifteen hundred of fish, along with dugongs, sea turtles and humpback whales that come north to calve in winter.\n\nAustralia established a marine park over most of the area in 1975 and administers it through a dedicated authority; the site was placed on the World Heritage list in 1981. The pressures on it are well documented. Mass bleaching, in which corals stressed by warm water expel the algae that feed them, has struck repeatedly since 1998, and outbreaks of the crown of thorns starfish, tropical cyclones and the runoff of sediment and fertiliser from the coast all take a toll.',
  },
  {
    num: 49, quizId: 'redact-9-23-26', live: '2026-09-23', dateLabel: 'September 23, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 3, answer: 'The Odyssey', aka: { oddysey: 'odyssey', odessy: 'odyssey', odyssy: 'odyssey', oddyssey: 'odyssey' },
    text: 'The poem was composed for performance long before it was written down. Its lines are in dactylic hexameter and full of repeated phrases, fixed epithets and whole scenes reused with small variation, features that scholars beginning with Milman Parry took as evidence of a tradition of oral composition stretching back generations. A written text seems to have been established at Athens by the sixth century BC, and the division into twenty four books is generally credited to scholars at Alexandria much later.\n\nThe Odyssey is the second of the two long epics attributed to Homer, and most estimates place its composition in the late eighth or early seventh century BC. It follows Odysseus of Ithaca through the ten years of wandering that come after the ten year war at Troy, and, in parallel, the troubles of the household he left behind, where his wife Penelope is besieged by suitors and his son Telemachus has grown up without him.\n\nThe narrative begins in the middle of the story. Odysseus is held on the island of Calypso, and his earlier adventures, with the Cyclops Polyphemus, the enchantress Circe, the ghosts of the dead, the Sirens and the twin hazards of Scylla and Charybdis, are told by the hero himself as a guest at the Phaeacian court. The second half returns home, where he arrives disguised as a beggar, is recognized by his old dog and his nurse, and reclaims his house by stringing a bow no suitor can bend.\n\nPenelope tests him afterwards with a question about their bed, which is built around a living olive tree and cannot be moved. The poem has been translated into English many times, by George Chapman, Alexander Pope, Robert Fitzgerald and Robert Fagles among others, and in 2017 by Emily Wilson, the first woman to publish a full English translation. Its shape survives in later work, notably the novel Ulysses, and the word odyssey has passed into ordinary English for any long and eventful journey.',
  },
  {
    num: 50, quizId: 'redact-9-24-26', live: '2026-09-24', dateLabel: 'September 24, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 3, answer: 'Frankenstein', aka: { frankinstein: 'frankenstein', frankenstien: 'frankenstein', frankestein: 'frankenstein' },
    text: 'The summer of 1816 was cold and wet across Europe, the aftermath of an enormous volcanic eruption in the Dutch East Indies the year before. A party of English travelers renting a villa on Lake Geneva spent the rainy evenings reading German ghost stories aloud, and Lord Byron proposed that each of them write one. The competition produced little from the poets, but an eighteen year old woman in the party, traveling with a married man she would later marry, began a story about a student who assembles a living being out of dead matter.\n\nFrankenstein, or The Modern Prometheus was published anonymously in London in January 1818, in three volumes, with a preface by Percy Bysshe Shelley that led some reviewers to assume he had written it. Mary Shelley\'s name appeared on the second edition of 1823. She revised the text substantially for an edition of 1831, softening the responsibility of her central character and adding an introduction that describes how the book began.\n\nThe story is told in letters from an explorer, Robert Walton, whose ship is trapped in Arctic ice and who takes an exhausted traveler aboard. That man, Victor Frankenstein of Geneva, describes his studies at Ingolstadt, the creature he made and then abandoned in horror, and the creature\'s own account of learning language by watching a poor family through a wall. Refused a companion of its own kind, it destroys everything its maker loves, and the pursuit ends in the ice.\n\nStage versions appeared within five years and were followed by many films, the most influential being the Universal picture of 1931, which gave the creature the flat head and neck bolts of popular imagination and little of the eloquence it has in the book. Through those adaptations the maker\'s surname drifted onto the creature. The novel is now often described as an early work of science fiction, since the animating power is not magic but the electricity and chemistry of its day.',
  },
  {
    num: 51, quizId: 'redact-9-25-26', live: '2026-09-25', dateLabel: 'September 25, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Hypatia', aka: { hypathia: 'hypatia', hipatia: 'hypatia' },
    text: 'Almost nothing she wrote survives in her own words. What is known comes from a thin set of sources: a dozen letters from a former pupil who became a bishop, a paragraph in a church history written a generation after her death, a hostile chronicle from the seventh century, and a brief entry in a Byzantine encyclopedia. Modern accounts of her life are built almost entirely from these fragments.\n\nHer father was Theon, a mathematician and astronomer who produced the editions of Euclid and Ptolemy that later copyists used. She worked in the same tradition, and ancient notices credit her with commentaries on the Arithmetica of Diophantus and the Conics of Apollonius, and with a share in her father\'s work on astronomical tables. The pupil whose letters survive, Synesius of Cyrene, wrote to her about an astrolabe and a device for measuring the density of liquids. She taught Neoplatonic philosophy publicly, and her students included pagans and Christians alike.\n\nHypatia lived and worked in Alexandria, then a large and volatile city in Roman Egypt. Her birth date is uncertain; most estimates place it between about 350 and 370. In 415 she was killed by a mob during a bitter quarrel between the imperial prefect Orestes, whom she was known to advise, and Cyril, the bishop of the city. The church historian Socrates Scholasticus, writing not long afterwards, describes her being dragged from her carriage into a church and killed, and treats the murder as a disgrace.\n\nHer death made her useful to later writers with arguments to press. Enlightenment authors turned her into a symbol of reason destroyed by zeal, and a Victorian novel fixed that reading in English. Historians have since been more cautious, pointing out that the quarrel was largely political, that she had Christian friends and students, and that the sources give little basis for the idea that she died defending science. What is not in doubt is that she held a recognized place in the intellectual life of her city.',
  },
  {
    num: 52, quizId: 'redact-9-26-26', live: '2026-09-26', dateLabel: 'September 26, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 4, answer: 'The Domesday Book', aka: { domsday: 'domesday', doomesday: 'domesday' },
    text: 'It did not always carry the name it has now. In the twelfth century treasury it was the book of Winchester, after the town where it was kept, or simply the king\'s book. The nickname it now bears came from below: people said its verdicts were as final as those of the last judgement, and by about 1179 the royal treasurer Richard FitzNeal was explaining the name in exactly those terms in his handbook on the exchequer.\n\nThe survey behind it was ordered at Christmas 1085, when William the Conqueror held his court at Gloucester and, according to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, had deep speech with his council about the land and how it was held. Commissioners rode out in circuits the following year, questioning juries in each hundred. They recorded who held each manor before 1066 and who held it now, how many plough teams and mills and fisheries there were, how many villagers, cottagers and slaves, and what the whole was worth.\n\nThe Domesday Book is in fact two volumes. The larger, Great Domesday, condenses the returns for most of England in a single scribe\'s abbreviated Latin. The smaller, Little Domesday, covers Norfolk, Suffolk and Essex in fuller and untidier detail, apparently because it was never boiled down. Coverage is not complete: the northernmost counties are largely absent, and London and Winchester have no entries. Even so the survey names more than thirteen thousand places, most of which still exist.\n\nIts practical life was long. English courts treated it as evidence of ancient tenure into the nineteenth century, and it was still being cited in disputes over land and customary rights when it was already seven hundred years old. The two volumes were kept with the treasury records and moved several times before settling at the National Archives at Kew. A facsimile edition marked the nine hundredth anniversary of the survey in 1986, by which time the manuscript itself had been rebound and was rarely shown.',
  },
  {
    num: 53, quizId: 'redact-9-27-26', live: '2026-09-27', dateLabel: 'September 27, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 5, answer: 'The Phaistos Disc', aka: { phaestos: 'phaistos', phaistros: 'phaistos', faistos: 'phaistos', phaistosdisc: 'phaistos' },
    text: 'On the third of July 1908 an Italian archaeologist named Luigi Pernier was working through the basement rooms on the north east side of a Minoan palace when his workmen lifted out a flat, roughly circular tablet of fired clay. It lay in a deposit of black earth with burnt debris, and near it was a tablet inscribed in Linear A, one of the undeciphered scripts of Bronze Age Greece. Pernier published a description within the year. The find has been argued over ever since, and almost every claim made about it beyond its physical description remains open.\n\nThe object is about fifteen centimetres across and a little over a centimetre thick. Both faces carry signs arranged in a spiral running between the rim and the centre, divided by incised lines into groups of two to seven characters. There are two hundred and forty one impressions in all, drawn from a repertoire of forty five distinct signs: a plumed head, a shield, a walking figure, a fish, a boat, a bird, an ear of grain and others. The signs were not scratched in but stamped, each with a prepared punch pressed into the soft clay, which means the maker held a set of at least forty five punches.\n\nThe palace stood at Phaistos, on the southern plain of Crete, and the object is generally called The Phaistos Disc after it. Dating has proved awkward. The Linear A tablet found nearby would put it around 1700 BCE, but Pernier recorded the layers loosely by later standards, and the deposit may have been disturbed; most estimates place it somewhere in the second millennium BCE without narrowing it much further. It is held by the Archaeological Museum of Heraklion, which displays it among its best known holdings.\n\nNobody has read it. Dozens of decipherments have been published, offering a prayer, a hymn, a calendar, an inventory and a list of place names, and none has convinced other specialists. The obstacles are basic: the text is far too short for the statistical attack that cracked other scripts, no second document in the same signs has been found, and the language behind it is unknown. Even the direction of reading is disputed, though the crowding of signs toward the centre has persuaded many that it runs inward from the rim. A few scholars have suggested the piece is a modern forgery; most reject that view and treat it as genuine.',
  },
  {
    num: 54, quizId: 'redact-9-28-26', live: '2026-09-28', dateLabel: 'September 28, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart', aka: { mozzart: 'mozart', motzart: 'mozart', amadaeus: 'amadeus', wolfgan: 'wolfgang' },
    text: 'In 1862 Ludwig von Kochel, an Austrian botanist and mineralogist, published a chronological catalogue of the works of a composer seventy years dead. He listed six hundred and twenty six items and numbered them in what he took to be order of composition. The numbers stuck, and remain the ordinary way of identifying the music. Later editors, notably Alfred Einstein in 1937, corrected many of the datings, but the original numbering was too well established to replace.\n\nWolfgang Amadeus Mozart was born in Salzburg on 27 January 1756, the son of Leopold, a violinist in the court chapel of the prince archbishop and the author of a widely used violin method. He was baptised with the Greek name Theophilus, which he usually rendered as Amade; the Latin form became standard only after his death. From 1762 his father took him and his older sister Maria Anna on long tours of the European courts, where the two children performed in Munich, Vienna, Paris and London.\n\nHe held a post as court musician at home until 1781, when a quarrel with his employer ended it, and settled in Vienna to work without a permanent appointment, living on teaching, subscription concerts, publishing and commissions. He married Constanze Weber the following year. The Vienna decade produced the piano concertos he wrote for his own concerts, six string quartets dedicated to Joseph Haydn, and three operas to librettos by Lorenzo Da Ponte, beginning with The Marriage of Figaro in 1786.\n\nHis last year brought The Magic Flute, an opera written for a coronation in Prague, and a commission for a requiem mass that he did not finish. He died in Vienna on 5 December 1791, aged thirty five; the cause was recorded vaguely and has never been established. His pupil Franz Xaver Sussmayr completed the requiem. The burial in a common grave, long treated as evidence of destitution, followed the ordinary practice of the city at the time.',
  },
  {
    num: 55, quizId: 'redact-9-29-26', live: '2026-09-29', dateLabel: 'September 29, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Sistine Chapel', aka: { sistene: 'sistine', sixtine: 'sistine', sistin: 'sistine', chappel: 'chapel' },
    text: 'In 1473 Pope Sixtus IV ordered the rebuilding of the Cappella Magna, the great hall of the Vatican palace in which the papal court assembled for its most solemn services. The work ran until 1481 under Giovannino de Dolci. The result was as much a stronghold as a place of worship, with massive lower walls and a guard walk above the vault. The interior measures about forty metres by thirteen, proportions that according to tradition follow the measurements given in the Old Testament for the Temple of Solomon.\n\nThe first decoration went up in 1481 and 1482, when painters brought in from Florence and Umbria, among them Perugino, Sandro Botticelli, Domenico Ghirlandaio and Cosimo Rosselli, covered the side walls with two parallel cycles, the life of Moses on one side and the life of Christ on the other. The ceiling was painted as a blue sky scattered with gold stars. The building took the name of the pope who had commissioned it, and is known as The Sistine Chapel.\n\nIn 1508 Julius II set Michelangelo to replace the starry ceiling. He worked until 1512 and produced more than three hundred figures: nine scenes from Genesis along the spine of the vault, prophets and sibyls enthroned below them, and ancestors of Christ in the lunettes. He painted standing on a scaffold of his own devising, bent backwards, rather than lying on his back as is often said. He returned nearly a quarter of a century later for the Last Judgment on the altar wall, finished in 1541.\n\nThe room remains in use. Most conclaves since the late fifteenth century have been held there, the ballots burned in a stove whose chimney signals the outcome to the crowd outside. Between 1980 and 1994 the frescoes were cleaned of centuries of soot, varnish and glue, work that revealed far brighter colour than anyone had seen in living memory and that a number of art historians criticised at the time as too severe.',
  },
  {
    num: 56, quizId: 'redact-9-30-26', live: '2026-09-30', dateLabel: 'September 30, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 3, answer: 'The Chernobyl Disaster', aka: { chernoble: 'chernobyl', chernobl: 'chernobyl', chernobyle: 'chernobyl', disater: 'disaster', dissaster: 'disaster' },
    text: 'On the morning of 28 April 1986 a worker arriving at the Forsmark nuclear plant in Sweden set off a radiation alarm on his way in rather than on his way out. Checks showed that the contamination was on clothing and shoes, that the plant itself was not leaking, and that similar readings were appearing across Scandinavia. Wind trajectories pointed south east, toward the western Soviet Union, and after a day of inquiries Moscow announced on the evening news that there had been an accident at a power station.\n\nThe Chernobyl Disaster had begun two days earlier, in the early hours of 26 April, at the fourth reactor of a station near the town of Pripyat in the Ukrainian republic. Operators were carrying out a test to see whether a slowing turbine could power cooling pumps during a loss of electrical supply. The reactor was held at a low and unstable power level for much longer than planned, and when the shutdown was ordered the output surged instead, and steam explosions destroyed the core and blew the roof off the building.\n\nThe graphite in the core burned for days, lifting radioactive material high into the air, where winds carried it across Europe. Firefighters and plant staff who worked at the ruined building in the first hours received extreme doses, and a number of them died of acute radiation sickness in the weeks that followed. Pripyat was evacuated the next afternoon, an exclusion zone of thirty kilometres was drawn, and helicopter crews dropped sand, clay, lead and boron onto the burning reactor.\n\nA concrete and steel shelter was built over the ruin before the end of the year, and a larger arch, designed to last a century, was moved into place over it in 2016. Investigations attributed the accident both to the actions of the operators and to faults in the design of the reactor type, including the behavior of its control rods as they entered the core. Estimates of the long term health toll vary widely and remain contested.',
  },
  {
    num: 57, quizId: 'redact-10-1-26', live: '2026-10-01', dateLabel: 'October 1, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 3, answer: 'The Terracotta Army', aka: { teracotta: 'terracotta', terracota: 'terracotta', terrecotta: 'terracotta' },
    text: 'In the spring of 1974 farmers digging a well in a drought stricken district of Shaanxi province, in northern China, struck fired clay several metres down. They brought up a head, then fragments of limbs, then bronze arrowheads and the triggers of crossbows. Archaeologists who came to look found that the pits extended for acres and lay roughly a mile and a half east of a great earthen mound raised over the tomb of the ruler who first united the warring Chinese states.\n\nThe Terracotta Army guards the mausoleum of Qin Shi Huang, who died in 210 BC. Three main pits have been opened. Most estimates put the total at about eight thousand figures, together with more than a hundred wooden chariots and several hundred horses, arranged in formation, with infantry in ranks, archers at the front and along the edges, and officers behind. A fourth pit was found empty, which may mean the work was left unfinished at the emperor\'s death.\n\nThe figures were made in workshops from standard parts. Legs, torsos, arms and heads were formed separately from local clay, assembled, then finished by hand so that faces, hair and armor differ from one another, and fired in kilns. Many bear stamped or incised names, apparently those of the foremen responsible. All were originally painted in bright colors over a lacquer ground, most of which flaked away soon after excavation exposed it to dry air, a problem that has slowed further digging.\n\nThe historian Sima Qian, writing about a century after the emperor\'s death, describes a tomb containing models of rivers and seas made of mercury and protected by crossbows set to fire at intruders. He says nothing about the figures. Soil surveys have found unusually high mercury readings over the mound, which has not been excavated, and the authorities have said it will stay closed until preservation methods improve. A museum opened on the site in 1979, and it became a World Heritage property in 1987.',
  },
  {
    num: 58, quizId: 'redact-10-2-26', live: '2026-10-02', dateLabel: 'October 2, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Ernest Shackleton', aka: { shackelton: 'shackleton', shakleton: 'shackleton' },
    text: 'On 9 January 1909 four exhausted men stopped on the polar plateau, planted a flag, and turned around. They were about ninety seven nautical miles from the South Pole, close enough that a determined party might have reached it and almost certainly would not have come back. The decision to retreat belonged to the expedition\'s leader, who wrote to his wife afterwards that he thought she would rather have a live donkey than a dead lion.\n\nHe was born in County Kildare in 1874 and raised in south London. He went to sea in the merchant service at sixteen and first travelled south in 1901 as a junior officer on Robert Falcon Scott\'s Discovery expedition, from which he was sent home early on grounds of ill health, a humiliation that shaped much of what followed. Between voyages he worked as a journalist and as secretary of a geographical society, and stood unsuccessfully for Parliament in Dundee in 1906.\n\nThe last great venture of Ernest Shackleton set out in 1914 to cross Antarctica on foot. The ship Endurance never landed her party: she was caught in the ice of the Weddell Sea, drifted for months, and was crushed and sunk in 1915. The men camped on floes, then sailed three small boats to Elephant Island. From there six of them made an open boat passage of roughly eight hundred miles to South Georgia and crossed its unmapped mountains to a whaling station. Every member of the party was eventually brought home alive.\n\nHe died of a heart attack in 1922 at South Georgia, at the start of another expedition, and was buried there at his widow\'s wish. For decades his reputation stood below Scott\'s in Britain. It rose again in the later twentieth century, when his record of bringing men back mattered more than his weakness as a planner and fundraiser. The recruiting advertisement often quoted in his name, promising small wages and bitter cold, has never been traced to any newspaper.',
  },
  {
    num: 59, quizId: 'redact-10-3-26', live: '2026-10-03', dateLabel: 'October 3, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 4, answer: 'Lake Baikal', aka: { baikel: 'baikal', baical: 'baikal' },
    text: 'It is not a glacial basin or a flooded valley. It sits in a continental rift, a place where the crust is pulling apart at a rate usually given as about two centimetres a year, and the trough it occupies is still deepening. Earthquakes are frequent. Beneath the water lie sediments several kilometres thick, laid down without interruption for a very long time, which is why it is generally reckoned the oldest large body of fresh water on the planet, at roughly twenty five million years.\n\nThat age has produced an unusual population. Most of the species living in the water are found nowhere else, among them hundreds of amphipod crustaceans, a translucent scaleless fish that bears live young, and a freshwater seal. How the seal arrived, more than a thousand miles from any sea, is not settled; the usual suggestion is a river connection during a colder period. Oxygen reaches even the deepest water, so animals live on the bottom, which is unusual at such depths.\n\nLake Baikal lies in southern Siberia, in the Russian Federation, curved for about six hundred kilometres between mountain ranges. Its greatest measured depth is a little over sixteen hundred metres, the greatest of any lake, and because it is so deep it holds an enormous volume of water: most estimates put its share of the world\'s unfrozen fresh surface water at around a fifth. More than three hundred rivers run into it, the largest being the Selenga, and only one, the Angara, runs out.\n\nIn winter the surface freezes hard enough to carry vehicles, and marked ice roads have long been used; a railway line was built around the southern shore in the early twentieth century so that trains would not have to cross. The largest island, Olkhon, holds sites important to local Buryat tradition. A paper mill on the southern shore polluted the water for decades before closing in 2013, and the lake was placed on the World Heritage list in 1996, partly in response to such pressure.',
  },
  {
    num: 60, quizId: 'redact-10-4-26', live: '2026-10-04', dateLabel: 'October 4, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 5, answer: 'The Uffington White Horse', aka: { uffingdon: 'uffington', ufington: 'uffington', uffinton: 'uffington' },
    text: 'A figure cut into chalk downland does not look after itself. Turf creeps back over the exposed surface within a generation, and without regular clearing the outline disappears altogether. For centuries the work was done at a communal event held roughly every seven years, when villagers went up the hill to scour the trenches and the local landowner paid for a fair afterwards, with wrestling, races and stalls on the slope. Thomas Hughes, better known for Tom Brown\'s School Days, wrote a whole book about the one held in 1857.\n\nExcavation has shown that the figure is not simply turf stripped away to show the chalk beneath. Trenches were dug into the hillside, in places close to a metre deep, and packed with crushed chalk, then recut and repacked over a very long period; the outline has drifted slightly up the slope as a result. The design is spare: a long body reduced almost to a line, a beaked head with a round eye, and legs that do not quite join the body, about one hundred and ten metres from nose to tail. It reads properly only from the vale below or from the air.\n\nThe Uffington White Horse takes its name from the village below it, in the part of Oxfordshire that belonged to Berkshire until 1974. Documents of the eleventh and twelfth centuries mention it, which makes it the oldest hill figure in Britain on record. Its actual age is less certain. Tests carried out in the 1990s on soil buried in the lowest layers of the trenches returned a late Bronze Age date, roughly 1000 BCE; other scholars have argued for the Iron Age, pointing to similar animals on coins of the period, and the question is not settled. Some have doubted that the animal was meant as a horse at all.\n\nThe setting is crowded with older work. Just above the figure is Uffington Castle, an Iron Age hillfort, and the prehistoric track called the Ridgeway runs along the crest. Below stands a flat topped natural mound, Dragon Hill, which local tradition connects with Saint George. During the Second World War the chalk was covered over so that enemy aircraft could not use it as a landmark. The National Trust has owned the site since 1979 and organises chalking days at which volunteers kneel along the trenches and hammer fresh chalk into them.',
  },
  {
    num: 61, quizId: 'redact-10-5-26', live: '2026-10-05', dateLabel: 'October 5, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Pele', aka: { pelee: 'pele', pelle: 'pele' },
    text: 'The goal that people describe most often was never filmed. In a league match at the Maracana in 1961 he is said to have beaten most of the opposing side from inside his own half before scoring, and Brazilian newspapers called it the goal of the plaque, since a plaque was later fixed at the stadium to record it. No footage survives, which has probably made it a better story.\n\nPele was born Edson Arantes do Nascimento in 1940 at Tres Coracoes in Minas Gerais, the son of a footballer whose own career had been cut short by injury. The nickname came from schoolmates, he disliked it at first, and he never gave a settled account of what it meant. He was playing for Santos at fifteen and for the national side at sixteen.\n\nHe arrived at the 1958 World Cup in Sweden as an unknown seventeen year old carrying a knee injury, scored three goals in the semi final against France and two in the final against the hosts, and finished the tournament in tears. Injury limited him in 1962, and in 1966 in England he was kicked out of the competition by defenders in an era before referees protected attacking players. He came back in 1970 with the Brazilian side often called the finest ever assembled, and he remains the only player to hold three World Cup winners medals.\n\nHe spent almost his whole club career at Santos, a provincial team that toured the world because he was in it, and he finished with the New York Cosmos from 1975, where he did more than anyone to establish the professional game in the United States. His career total is usually given as more than a thousand goals, a figure that includes friendly and exhibition matches and is disputed on that ground. He later served as his country\'s minister of sport, and he died in 2022.',
  },
  {
    num: 62, quizId: 'redact-10-6-26', live: '2026-10-06', dateLabel: 'October 6, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 2, answer: 'The Moon Landing', aka: { moonlanding: 'landing', lunarlanding: 'landing', moonlandings: 'landing' },
    text: 'The Moon Landing of July 1969 was the first occasion on which people set foot on another world. It answered a goal announced by President Kennedy in 1961, that the United States should put a man on the lunar surface and return him safely before the decade was out, a commitment made when the Soviet Union held a clear lead in spaceflight and the American programme had managed a single suborbital hop.\n\nThe mission lifted off from Florida on 16 July on a Saturn rocket, the most powerful launch vehicle then built. Three days later it entered orbit around the Moon, and on 20 July two of the crew, Neil Armstrong and Edwin Aldrin, separated in the spidery lander while Michael Collins stayed above them in the command ship. The descent did not go smoothly. The guidance computer, which held a tiny fraction of the memory of a modern telephone, repeatedly signalled that it was overloaded, and the automatic system was steering toward a field of boulders, so Armstrong flew the last stretch by hand and touched down on a basalt plain with well under a minute of fuel left by the reckoning of the controllers watching in Houston.\n\nArmstrong climbed down the ladder some six hours later and spoke a sentence about a small step, the wording of which he later said had been misheard. The two men spent about two and a half hours outside. They planted a flag stiffened with wire, took a telephone call from the White House, collected some twenty kilograms of rock and dust, and left instruments behind, among them a mirror array that observatories still bounce lasers off to measure the distance to the Moon.\n\nAn estimated six hundred million people watched the grainy live pictures. The crew splashed down in the Pacific on 24 July and were held in quarantine for nearly three weeks. Five more crews followed before the programme ended in 1972.',
  },
  {
    num: 63, quizId: 'redact-10-7-26', live: '2026-10-07', dateLabel: 'October 7, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 3, answer: 'Swan Lake', aka: { swanlake: 'swan', swann: 'swan' },
    text: 'Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky had never written for the stage of a ballet company when the Moscow theatre directorate commissioned a score from him in 1875 for a modest fee. Ballet music at the time was largely the work of house specialists who supplied danceable numbers to order, and a symphonist taking the job was unusual enough to be remarked on. The first production, staged at the Bolshoi in 1877 with choreography by Julius Reisinger, ran for some years without ever being thought a success; the dancing, the conducting and the cuts were all blamed at the time, and the composer died in 1893 believing the work had not come off.\n\nSwan Lake as it is now danced dates from 1895, when the Mariinsky Theatre in St Petersburg revived it in a version prepared by Marius Petipa and Lev Ivanov. Ivanov made the lakeside acts, and the pattern of a white corps moving in unison with tilted heads and folded arms became the defining image of classical ballet.\n\nThe story draws on German and Russian folk material. A prince out hunting finds a flock of birds who are women by night, bound by the sorcerer Rothbart; he swears himself to their queen Odette, and at the ball where he must choose a bride he is deceived into swearing the same oath to Odile, the sorcerer\'s daughter in her likeness. The two roles are usually taken by the same ballerina, which makes the part one of the hardest in the repertory, and Odile\'s variation ends in a long sequence of whipped turns on one leg that audiences count.\n\nEndings vary by production, some tragic and some redemptive, and Soviet stagings often preferred the couple to survive. It is now among the most performed ballets in the world.',
  },
  {
    num: 64, quizId: 'redact-10-8-26', live: '2026-10-08', dateLabel: 'October 8, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Zaha Hadid', aka: { zahra: 'zaha', hadeed: 'hadid', haddid: 'hadid', hadidd: 'hadid' },
    text: 'For more than a decade the practice was known for buildings that did not get built. A competition win in 1983 for a leisure club on the Peak above Hong Kong produced a set of paintings, owing something to Russian Suprematism, in which the plan is exploded and seen from several angles at once; the club was never constructed. A design that won the competition for an opera house at Cardiff Bay in 1994 was abandoned after a political quarrel over funding. Critics called the work unbuildable, which was partly a comment on the drawings and partly on the technology of the time.\n\nZaha Hadid was born in Baghdad in 1950, studied mathematics at the American University of Beirut, and came to London in 1972 to the Architectural Association, where she later taught. She worked briefly with her former tutors Rem Koolhaas and Elia Zenghelis before opening her own office in 1980.\n\nThe first completed building of note was a small fire station for the Vitra furniture factory at Weil am Rhein in Germany, finished in 1993 and made of sharp folded planes of concrete. Advances in computer modelling then allowed the curves her drawings had implied, and a run of major commissions followed: the Rosenthal Center in Cincinnati, the Phaeno science centre at Wolfsburg, the Guangzhou opera house, the London Aquatics Centre, and the Heydar Aliyev Center in Baku.\n\nShe was the first woman to receive the Pritzker Prize, in 2004, and won the Stirling Prize in two consecutive years. Her work drew steady criticism as well: for cost, for indifference to its surroundings, and, over a stadium project in Qatar, for the treatment of migrant construction workers, a charge she disputed. She died in Miami in 2016, at sixty five, with a large number of projects still on the boards, and the firm has continued under her longtime partner Patrik Schumacher.',
  },
  {
    num: 65, quizId: 'redact-10-9-26', live: '2026-10-09', dateLabel: 'October 9, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 4, answer: 'The Meiji Restoration', aka: { meji: 'meiji', mejii: 'meiji', meijii: 'meiji', restauration: 'restoration' },
    text: 'In July 1853 four American warships anchored in the bay below Edo and their commodore delivered a letter demanding that Japan open its ports. The government that received him had ruled for two and a half centuries under a shogun, kept foreign contact to a trickle through a single Dutch trading post, and had no answer to steam powered vessels carrying shell guns. The treaties signed over the following years granted foreigners low tariffs and immunity from Japanese courts, and the humiliation discredited the men who had signed them.\n\nOpposition gathered around a slogan calling for reverence to the emperor and expulsion of the barbarians. Two large domains in the southwest, Satsuma and Choshu, had themselves been bombarded by Western squadrons, concluded that expulsion was impossible without modern arms, and set the second half of the slogan quietly aside. Allied in secret from 1866, they pressed the last shogun to resign, and in January 1868 a court coup declared direct rule by the young emperor whose reign name means enlightened rule. A civil war in the north settled the matter within eighteen months.\n\nWhat followed was less a return to old ways than a rapid dismantling of them. The court moved from Kyoto to Edo, renamed Tokyo. The domains were surrendered and replaced by prefectures in 1871. A senior mission spent nearly two years touring America and Europe to study institutions. Conscription, a fixed land tax in cash, compulsory elementary schooling, railways, telegraphs and state model factories followed in quick succession.\n\nThe samurai lost their stipends, their monopoly on arms and the right to wear swords, and a rebellion by discontented former warriors in Satsuma was crushed in 1877 by a conscript army. The Meiji Restoration is conventionally treated as the moment Japan turned into a modern industrial state, a transformation completed by a constitution in 1889 and an elected assembly the following year.',
  },
  {
    num: 66, quizId: 'redact-10-10-26', live: '2026-10-10', dateLabel: 'October 10, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 4, answer: 'Journey to the West', aka: { journeytothewest: 'journey', jorney: 'journey' },
    text: 'A real monk stands behind the story. Xuanzang left Tang China in 629 against an imperial ban on foreign travel, crossed the deserts and mountains of Central Asia, studied for years at the great Buddhist university of Nalanda in India, and came home in 645 with hundreds of texts to translate. His account of the countries he passed through is still used by archaeologists, and the emperor who had forbidden the trip received him in triumph.\n\nAround that history a body of legend accumulated for nine centuries, and in the 1590s it was gathered into a hundred chapter novel published anonymously in China; authorship is traditionally assigned to Wu Cheng\'en, on evidence that scholars have long treated as thin. Journey to the West sends the pious and rather helpless monk toward India escorted by three supernatural convicts working off their sins, plus a dragon prince transformed into his horse.\n\nThe first seven chapters belong to the monkey. Born from a stone egg, trained by a Daoist immortal, armed with an iron staff taken from the dragon king of the sea and able to change shape and travel thousands of miles in a bound, he strikes his name from the ledgers of death, is bought off with a meaningless heavenly title, and wrecks a banquet of the gods before the Buddha traps him under a mountain for five hundred years. His companions are a lecherous, gluttonous pig and a reformed ogre.\n\nThe pilgrimage that follows is a sequence of eighty one ordeals against demons, most of whom turn out to be somebody\'s escaped servant. The tone mixes Buddhist and Daoist allegory with slapstick and satire on officialdom. Counted among the four great classical Chinese novels, it has fed opera, film, television and comics across Asia, and the monkey remains one of the most recognisable characters in world fiction.',
  },
  {
    num: 67, quizId: 'redact-10-11-26', live: '2026-10-11', dateLabel: 'October 11, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 5, answer: 'Murasaki Shikibu', aka: { murazaki: 'murasaki', murasake: 'murasaki', shikibou: 'shikibu', shikubu: 'shikibu' },
    text: 'Nobody knows her name. The two words by which she is known are an office and a nickname: one refers to her father\'s post in the Bureau of Ceremonial, and the other is either the name of a character in her own book or a play on her family name, which was Fujiwara. Court women of eleventh century Japan were recorded by such labels rather than by personal names, and hers has not survived.\n\nShe was born about 973 into a minor branch of the great Fujiwara clan, a family of provincial governors and scholars rather than of ministers. Her father taught her brother the Chinese classics and remarked, she reports in her diary, that it was a pity she had not been born a boy, since she picked them up faster. She married a much older kinsman, had a daughter, was widowed within a few years, and entered the household of the empress Shoshi about 1005.\n\nMurasaki Shikibu wrote The Tale of Genji in the vernacular script used by women, at a court where men wrote in Chinese. It runs to fifty four chapters and follows the son of an emperor by a low ranking consort through a long life of love affairs, exile and political manoeuvre, then carries on for another generation after his death. It is frequently called the first novel, a claim worth hedging, though few dispute that it is a psychological narrative of great length and subtlety written centuries before anything comparable in Europe.\n\nHer diary survives as well, and is sharp about her colleagues; she thought Sei Shonagon far too pleased with herself. The tale circulated in manuscript, was copied, illustrated and annotated for centuries, and shaped Japanese poetry, painting and theatre. The English translation by Arthur Waley, issued in six volumes from 1925, made it known in the West, and several later translations have followed. Her death is usually placed around 1014, though the date is uncertain.\n\nNothing survives in her hand. The text scholars use descends chiefly from an edition prepared in the early thirteenth century by the poet Fujiwara no Teika, working two hundred years after her death from copies that already disagreed with one another, and a rival line of manuscripts complicates the picture further. The oldest illustrated version, a set of handscrolls painted about 1120 and now divided between two museums, is among the treasures of Japanese painting, and a detail from one of them was printed on a banknote issued in the year 2000.',
  },
  {
    num: 68, quizId: 'redact-10-12-26', live: '2026-10-12', dateLabel: 'October 12, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 2, answer: 'The Starry Night', aka: { starrynight: 'starry', stary: 'starry', starey: 'starry' },
    text: 'The Starry Night is an oil painting made by Vincent van Gogh in June 1889, while he was a voluntary patient at the asylum of Saint Paul de Mausole outside the town of Saint Remy in Provence. He had admitted himself the previous month, after the breakdown in Arles that ended with part of his own ear cut off, and he was allowed a room to work in as well as a bedroom with a barred window facing east.\n\nThat window supplied the view. Letters to his brother Theo describe watching the morning star before sunrise over the wheatfield and the hills, and the painting keeps the hills and the sky while inventing much of the rest. The village below, with its narrow steeple, looks more Dutch than Provencal and was not there. The great dark cypress in the foreground has been pulled close and made enormous. Eleven stars and a crescent moon swirl in bands of thick paint through a sky that behaves like moving water.\n\nHe did not think much of it. He had spent years insisting on working in front of the subject, and this canvas was largely built from memory and imagination, which he associated with failure; in his letters he calls it a study and lists its faults. He sold very little in his lifetime and died the following July.\n\nThe canvas passed through several private hands and reached the Museum of Modern Art in New York in 1941, where it has hung ever since. It is now among the most reproduced images in the world, printed on everything, examined by scientists for its resemblance to turbulent flow, and the subject of a famous 1971 song about a painter the world was not listening to.',
  },
  {
    num: 69, quizId: 'redact-10-13-26', live: '2026-10-13', dateLabel: 'October 13, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'Mount Fuji', aka: { fugi: 'fuji', fujii: 'fuji', mountfuji: 'fuji', fujisan: 'fuji' },
    text: 'Mount Fuji is the highest mountain in Japan, a stratovolcano of about three thousand seven hundred and seventy six metres rising on the island of Honshu roughly one hundred kilometres southwest of Tokyo. It sits on the boundary between Shizuoka and Yamanashi prefectures, near a point where three of the plates that make up the crust of the region meet, which is one reason the cone has grown so large and so young. What visitors see is the newest of several overlapping volcanoes stacked on the same site.\n\nThe last eruption began in December 1707 and lasted about two weeks. It produced no lava flow but threw out enough ash to darken the sky over Edo, the city that became Tokyo, and to ruin farmland for years afterwards. The vent it opened on the southeastern flank is still visible as a notch in the otherwise regular profile. The mountain is classed as active, and its behaviour is closely monitored.\n\nIt has been a religious site for centuries. Shrines of the Sengen or Asama group, dedicated to a goddess associated with the volcano, stand around the base and at the summit, and organised pilgrimage societies sent parties up the trails in large numbers during the Edo period. Women were barred from the upper slopes until the ban was lifted in the nineteenth century. The official climbing season runs for two months in summer, when a few hundred thousand people make the ascent, most starting from a road head partway up rather than at the foot.\n\nThe symmetrical cone became one of the most reproduced images in art, above all in the woodblock series of the late Edo printmakers, and it remains a fixture of Japanese design and advertising. It was added to the World Heritage list in 2013, listed for its cultural significance rather than as a natural feature.',
  },
  {
    num: 70, quizId: 'redact-10-14-26', live: '2026-10-14', dateLabel: 'October 14, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 3, answer: 'The Abacus', aka: { abbacus: 'abacus', abbacas: 'abacus', abakus: 'abacus' },
    text: 'In 1846 a slab of marble was recovered on the Greek island of Salamis, ruled with parallel lines and marked with Greek numerals. It is generally dated to about the third century BC and is taken to be the oldest surviving counting board of its kind: a flat surface across which loose pebbles were shifted between columns, each column standing for a power of ten. The Romans called such a pebble a calculus, which is where calculate comes from, and the Greek name for the board itself may descend from a Semitic word for dust, a hint that the earliest versions were lines drawn in sand.\n\nThe abacus in its familiar form, beads sliding on rods inside a frame, developed in more than one tradition. The Chinese suanpan carries two beads above a divider and five below on each rod, a layout that suits decimal counting and also the older sixteen part weight system; it is well attested by the Ming dynasty and is probably older. Japanese users pared it down into the soroban, with one bead above and four below. Russian shopkeepers worked the schoty, a frame of horizontal wires with ten beads to a wire, into living memory.\n\nSkilled operators are quick. In a contest staged in Tokyo in 1946, a Japanese clerk beat an American serviceman working an electric calculating machine in addition, subtraction, multiplication and division, losing only on the largest multiplications. The result is often quoted to show that the older tool was faster, though it mainly shows how much depends on the operator.\n\nThe device survives because it does something a calculator does not. Children trained on it learn to picture the beads and then to compute with nothing in front of them at all, and versions whose beads are held steady by a soft backing are standard for blind students. Teachers still reach for the frame when explaining place value.',
  },
  {
    num: 71, quizId: 'redact-10-15-26', live: '2026-10-15', dateLabel: 'October 15, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 3, answer: 'Easter Island', aka: { esater: 'easter', eastar: 'easter', easterisland: 'easter', islnd: 'island' },
    text: 'Roughly a thousand carved stone figures stand on a triangular scrap of volcanic rock in the southeastern Pacific, some three and a half thousand kilometres from the coast of Chile and more than two thousand from the nearest inhabited neighbour. Almost all of them were cut from the compacted ash of a single extinct crater, where several hundred remain in the quarry at every stage of work, including one that would have stood about twenty one metres high had anyone managed to move it.\n\nPolynesian voyagers reached the place by canoe, most current estimates putting the settlement at around the twelfth or thirteenth century. The figures, called moai, are understood to represent ancestors. They were set on stone platforms with their backs to the sea, looking inland over the settlements they protected, and many wore a cylinder of red scoria on the head. How they were moved remains argued over; sledges and rollers have been proposed, and so has an upright rocking motion using ropes, which oral tradition describes as the statues walking.\n\nThe island lost its palm forest, and the older story that the inhabitants simply cut it all down and collapsed has been complicated by evidence that rats introduced with the canoes ate the seeds. The statues were pushed over during a period of upheaval before and after European contact. A Dutch expedition sighted the island on Easter Sunday in 1722, which is where the common name comes from; the Polynesian name is Rapa Nui.\n\nSlave raiding from Peru in the early 1860s and the diseases that followed reduced the population, which a count in the 1870s put at a hundred and eleven, breaking much of the transmission of custom and leaving the incised wooden tablets known as rongorongo undeciphered. Chile annexed the territory in 1888. Most of it is now a national park, listed by the heritage convention in 1995.',
  },
  {
    num: 72, quizId: 'redact-10-16-26', live: '2026-10-16', dateLabel: 'October 16, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 4, answer: 'The Book of Kells', aka: { kels: 'kells', kelles: 'kells', kellz: 'kells' },
    text: 'In 1007 the annals of the Irish church record that the great gospel book of Colum Cille, the finest relic of the western world, was stolen at night from the great stone church of a monastery in County Meath. It was found some months later under a piece of turf, stripped of the gold and jewelled cover that had made it worth taking, and probably lighter by a number of leaves. The theft is the earliest firm date attached to the object, and it tells us what the medieval church thought the thing was worth.\n\nThe Book of Kells is a copy of the four gospels in Latin, written and painted on calf vellum around the year 800 by several hands. Scholars generally place its beginnings at the island monastery of Iona, off western Scotland, with the work continued at Kells in Ireland after Viking raids drove the community inland. Some three hundred and forty leaves survive, which would have taken the hides of well over a hundred animals.\n\nWhat sets it apart is the decoration. Whole pages are given over to ornament: interlaced beasts and ribbons, spirals, human figures folded into the letters, and a monogram page in Matthew that fills a full sheet with a single abbreviated holy name. The pigments include yellow from orpiment, greens from copper, red lead and a blue that recent analysis suggests came from a plant dye rather than imported lapis. The text itself is careless in places, as though the writing mattered less than the display.\n\nThe manuscript reached Trinity College Dublin in the seventeenth century and has stayed there. It was rebound in four volumes in 1953, two of which are normally on show with the pages turned at intervals, and it draws hundreds of thousands of visitors a year.',
  },
  {
    num: 73, quizId: 'redact-10-17-26', live: '2026-10-17', dateLabel: 'October 17, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 4, answer: 'Indigo', aka: { indego: 'indigo', indagio: 'indigo', indigoe: 'indigo' },
    text: 'The dye is not blue in the vat. The leaves hold a colourless compound that has to be broken down by fermentation and then kept in a chemically reduced state, in which it dissolves as a murky yellow green liquor with a foul smell. Cloth lifted out of that liquor is yellow green too, and turns blue in the air over a minute or so as oxygen converts the dye back into a pigment that will not dissolve, now locked inside the fibre. Repeated dips deepen the shade. Dyers ran vats on this principle for thousands of years with no idea of the chemistry.\n\nIndigo comes from several unrelated plants. The strongest is a legume of India, whose Greek name meant simply the Indian thing; Europe made do with woad, a cabbage relative carrying far less of the same compound; Japan fermented a knotweed into a compost called sukumo; West African dyers worked their own species in the sunken pits of Kano and in the resist patterned adire cloth of the Yoruba.\n\nWhen Portuguese ships brought the Indian dye west in quantity, European woad growers fought back with law, and a German edict of 1577 branded the import a devil\'s dye. The bans failed. Plantations followed in the Caribbean and Carolina, worked by enslaved people, and in Bengal, where tenant farmers were pressed to plant it on their best land and rose against the planters in the revolt of 1859.\n\nAdolf von Baeyer established the structure in the 1880s, and a commercial synthesis launched in 1897 after enormous investment. The natural indigo crop collapsed within twenty years. Almost everything now sold is made in a factory, and its overwhelming use is denim, where it clings to the outside of the yarn and rubs away with wear, which is why blue jeans fade the way they do.',
  },
  {
    num: 74, quizId: 'redact-10-18-26', live: '2026-10-18', dateLabel: 'October 18, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 5, answer: 'The Great Trigonometrical Survey', aka: { triginometrical: 'trigonometrical', trignometrical: 'trigonometrical', trigonametrical: 'trigonometrical' },
    text: 'The instrument weighed roughly half a ton. Built in London and shipped east in 1802, the great theodolite was captured at sea by a French warship, released once its purpose was explained, and hauled up hills across the subcontinent for the next forty years on the shoulders of labourers, occasionally falling and being repaired in the field. With it an infantry officer named William Lambton began measuring a baseline on a plain near Madras, intending to fix the width of India and then to run a chain of triangles north along a line of longitude.\n\nThe method was simple in principle and punishing in practice. Measure one baseline with great care, then observe the angles of a triangle built on it from hilltops or from towers raised where the land was flat, and every further triangle can be computed rather than paced. Sightlines of fifty kilometres were taken by lamp at night or by mirrors flashing sunlight. Fever killed members of the parties in large numbers, and Lambton himself died in 1823 with the work half done.\n\nGeorge Everest took it over, completed the arc from the southern cape to the Himalayan foothills in 1841, and imposed a rigour on the calculations that made the results usable for a century. Long columns of arithmetic were worked through by Indian mathematicians employed in the offices at Calcutta and Dehradun.\n\nThe arithmetic produced a name that is rarely attached to it. Radhanath Sikdar, a Bengali mathematician employed as chief computer at Calcutta, is generally credited with the calculation that identified the peak, although the surviving record is thin. Later the work pushed into country that could not be entered openly, and Indian explorers were trained to cross into Tibet in disguise, pacing distances with a fixed stride, counting them on rosaries adapted to a hundred beads, and hiding their notes in prayer wheels.\n\nThe Great Trigonometrical Survey produced more than a map. Its plumb lines were pulled measurably off vertical near the mountains, but by less than the mass of the range should have caused, which led to the idea that mountain ranges float on denser material beneath, the beginning of the theory of isostasy. In about 1852 the office computed the height of a peak catalogued only by number and found it the highest known; the head of the survey named it in 1865 after his predecessor, who objected to the choice.',
  },
  {
    num: 75, quizId: 'redact-10-19-26', live: '2026-10-19', dateLabel: 'October 19, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Taj Mahal', aka: { tajmahal: 'mahal', mahall: 'mahal', mehal: 'mahal', tajmahall: 'mahal' },
    text: 'The Taj Mahal is a domed marble mausoleum on the south bank of the Yamuna river at Agra, in the north Indian state of Uttar Pradesh. The Mughal emperor Shah Jahan raised it for Mumtaz Mahal, the wife who had travelled with him on campaign and who died in 1631 shortly after the birth of their fourteenth child. Work on the tomb chamber occupied roughly the following decade, while the wider walled complex of gateway, mosque and assembly hall was not reported complete until about 1653.\n\nThe building stands on a raised plinth at the far end of its enclosure rather than in the middle, so that the river supplies the backdrop and there is nothing behind it but sky. The facing stone came from quarries at Makrana in Rajasthan. Its surfaces are inlaid with flowers and arabesques cut from carnelian, jasper, lapis lazuli and other coloured stones, a technique Indian workshops call parchin kari, and passages of Quranic script in black inlay frame the great arch, the letters tapered so that they read as one size from the ground. The four minarets lean very slightly outward. No designer is documented beyond dispute, though the name most often advanced is that of the architect Ustad Ahmad Lahauri.\n\nThe garden in front is a charbagh, a square quartered by water channels in the Persian manner and planted originally with fruit trees. Shah Jahan was deposed by his son Aurangzeb in 1658 and spent his last years confined in the fort upstream. He was buried beside his wife, and his cenotaph, set off centre, is the one asymmetric element in an otherwise exactly balanced building. The tradition that he meant to build a matching tomb in black marble across the water has no contemporary support.\n\nBritish officials ordered repairs around 1900 after a long period of neglect. The site was inscribed as a World Heritage property in 1983, draws several million visitors a year, and is periodically cleaned with a clay poultice to lift the discolouration left by airborne pollution.',
  },
  {
    num: 76, quizId: 'redact-10-20-26', live: '2026-10-20', dateLabel: 'October 20, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 2, answer: 'Salt', aka: { sallt: 'salt' },
    text: 'Chemically it is sodium chloride, a cubic crystal that occurs as the mineral halite and dissolves in sea water at roughly thirty five grams a litre. Human beings cannot manufacture it inside the body and cannot live without it, since sodium governs the fluid balance of every cell and the nerve signals that depend on it. Grazing animals walk long distances to natural licks for the same reason.\n\nSalt is won in three ways: mined as rock from the beds of ancient dried seas, boiled out of brine, or left behind when sea water evaporates in shallow ponds. All three are old. The workings at Hallstatt in Austria date to the Bronze Age, and the mine at Wieliczka in Poland has been cut into chapels and chandeliers by its own miners. Engineers in Sichuan sank percussion drilled brine wells more than a kilometre deep, using bamboo cable and casing, centuries before European drilling reached such depths.\n\nBecause it preserves meat and fish, it made long voyages, standing armies and winter cities possible, and it was valuable enough to tax. Caravans crossed the Sahara with slabs cut at Taghaza, trading them south toward the gold country, and that exchange helped build the cities of the Sahel. Chinese states ran a monopoly on and off from the Han dynasty onward. The French gabelle, with wildly different rates from province to province, was a standing grievance before the revolution, and a colonial monopoly in India was broken in 1930 by a protest march to the sea.\n\nRefrigeration ended its career as the main preservative and the price collapsed. Most of what is produced now goes to the chemical industry and to clearing ice from roads rather than to the table. Adding iodine to it, a public health measure introduced in the 1920s, remains one of the cheapest ways to prevent goitre.',
  },
  {
    num: 77, quizId: 'redact-10-21-26', live: '2026-10-21', dateLabel: 'October 21, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Adam Smith', aka: { smyth: 'smith', smithe: 'smith' },
    text: 'The phrase everyone remembers appears exactly once in the book. In a passage explaining why merchants tend to invest at home, the author writes that each of them is led by an invisible hand to promote an end that formed no part of his intention. The image occurs twice more across his other writings and carries less weight there than later readers have given it; he used it in passing, and most of the theory built around it belongs to his successors.\n\nAn Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations was published in March 1776 in two volumes. It opens not with markets but with a pin factory, where dividing the work into eighteen distinct operations lets ten people make far more pins than they could working singly, and it builds from there into arguments about specialisation, the extent of the market, wages, rent and the folly of restricting trade.\n\nAdam Smith was baptised at Kirkcaldy in Fife in 1723, studied at Glasgow and at Balliol College in Oxford, which he thought idle, and became professor of moral philosophy at Glasgow in 1752. His first book, The Theory of Moral Sentiments of 1759, grounds ethics in sympathy, the capacity to imagine oneself in another position and to judge conduct through an impartial spectator. He seems to have regarded it as the more important work, and scholars have long argued over how the two books fit together.\n\nHe spent three years as travelling tutor to a young duke, meeting Voltaire and the French economists, then lived on the resulting pension and wrote for a decade. He later took a post as a commissioner of customs in Edinburgh, an office his admirers find awkward. He had his unpublished manuscripts burned shortly before his death in 1790. His arguments were used to justify free trade through the following century, often more confidently than he had made them, since he also wrote about the dulling effect of repetitive labour and defended schooling at public expense.',
  },
  {
    num: 78, quizId: 'redact-10-22-26', live: '2026-10-22', dateLabel: 'October 22, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 3, answer: 'Petra', aka: { pertra: 'petra', petrah: 'petra', pettra: 'petra' },
    text: 'In 1812 a Swiss traveller, Johann Ludwig Burckhardt, was crossing southern Jordan on his way to Cairo when he heard talk of remarkable ruins in a valley the local Bedouin kept to themselves. Travelling as a Muslim scholar and claiming he wished to sacrifice a goat at a nearby shrine, he persuaded a guide to take him in, glanced about as much as he dared, and became the first European in centuries to report the place. His account, published after his death, brought a stream of artists and antiquarians.\n\nThe city was the capital of the Nabataeans, an Arab people who grew rich from about the fourth century BC by taxing and escorting the caravans that carried frankincense and myrrh north from southern Arabia. Their achievement was less architectural than hydraulic. In a landscape with almost no rain they built dams, cut channels along cliff faces, laid ceramic pipe and hollowed out cisterns, storing enough water to supply a population of perhaps twenty or thirty thousand and to defend the site against the flash floods that tear down the gorges.\n\nThe approach runs through the Siq, a winding cleft more than a kilometre long and in places only a few metres wide, which opens abruptly on the facade of the tomb known as the Treasury. Petra is a Greek word meaning rock, and the great monuments are not built but carved back into the red and banded sandstone, their columns and pediments borrowing from Greek and Egyptian models and rearranging them freely.\n\nRome annexed the kingdom in 106 and governed it as the province of Arabia. Trade shifted to sea routes, an earthquake in 363 caused heavy damage, and the city dwindled to a village. It was inscribed as a World Heritage property in 1985, and archaeologists estimate that only a modest fraction of it has been excavated.',
  },
  {
    num: 79, quizId: 'redact-10-23-26', live: '2026-10-23', dateLabel: 'October 23, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Wangari Maathai', aka: { mathai: 'maathai', maatai: 'maathai', maathei: 'maathai', wangaari: 'wangari' },
    text: 'The idea began with complaints, not with a theory. Rural women told her that the streams were drying up, that firewood had to be fetched from further away each year, and that the food they could grow was poorer than it had been. Her answer was to pay them a small sum for every seedling that survived, and to organise the planting in groups. The scheme, launched under the National Council of Women of Kenya in 1977, became the Green Belt Movement, and by most counts it has since put tens of millions of trees in the ground.\n\nWangari Muta Maathai was born in 1940 at Ihithe in the central highlands and went to the United States on a scholarship programme that brought hundreds of East African students to American universities. She studied biology in Kansas and Pittsburgh, came home, and in 1971 became the first woman in East or Central Africa to earn a doctorate, in veterinary anatomy at the University of Nairobi, where she later chaired her department.\n\nPlanting trees turned political because land was political. She campaigned against the allocation of public land to developers, most visibly in 1989 when she helped block a tower planned for Uhuru Park in Nairobi, and against the clearing of the Karura forest. She was arrested repeatedly, beaten on one occasion, and denounced in parliament during the long presidency of Daniel arap Moi.\n\nAfter multiparty elections she entered parliament in 2002 with a large majority and served as an assistant minister for the environment. In 2004 she became the first African woman to receive the Nobel Peace Prize, awarded for her contribution to sustainable development, democracy and peace, a linkage some observers questioned at the time and which has since become commonplace. She wrote several books, among them a memoir called Unbowed, sat on international commissions on forests and on Africa, and died in 2011.',
  },
  {
    num: 80, quizId: 'redact-10-24-26', live: '2026-10-24', dateLabel: 'October 24, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 4, answer: 'The Transit of Venus', aka: { venuss: 'venus', vinus: 'venus', tranist: 'transit' },
    text: 'By the early eighteenth century astronomers could describe the shape of the solar system with confidence but not its size. Kepler\'s laws gave the relative distances of the planets exactly, so that if any one gap could be measured in miles the whole scale would follow, and nobody could measure one. In 1716 Edmond Halley published a method. If observers stationed far apart on the globe timed the passage of an inner planet across the face of the Sun, the small difference in the path each of them saw would yield the angle he called the solar parallax. He knew he would not live to try it, and asked a later generation to.\n\nThe events he had in mind come in pairs eight years apart and then not again for more than a century. Those of 1761 and 1769 prompted what is often called the first international scientific campaign. Britain, France, Russia, Sweden and others sent well over a hundred observers to Siberia, Newfoundland, Madagascar, South Africa and the Pacific, in wartime, with instruments that had to survive the voyage.\n\nOne of those parties, under James Cook, sailed to Tahiti in 1769 and afterwards opened sealed instructions directing it to search the southern ocean for a continent. The French astronomer Guillaume Le Gentil had the worst of it: sent to India, he was still at sea for the first transit, waited eight years for the second, was clouded out on the morning it happened, and reached home after eleven years to find his heirs dividing his estate.\n\nThe results were undercut by an optical smear at the moment of contact that made precise timing impossible, but they fixed the distance to the Sun within a few percent of the modern figure. The Transit of Venus last occurred in 2012 and will not be seen again until the twenty second century.',
  },
  {
    num: 81, quizId: 'redact-10-25-26', live: '2026-10-25', dateLabel: 'October 25, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 5, answer: 'The Popol Vuh', aka: { popolvuh: 'popol', popul: 'popol', vooh: 'vuh', pupul: 'popol' },
    text: 'Around 1701 a Dominican friar named Francisco Ximenez, serving in the highland Guatemalan town of Chichicastenango, was shown a manuscript by his K\'iche\' Maya parishioners. He copied it out in their language in one column and set his own Spanish translation beside it. The original he worked from has never been seen since; his copy sat in a monastery library, then a university, and eventually crossed the Atlantic to Chicago, where it is now held by the Newberry Library.\n\nThe text he copied had itself been written down in the middle of the sixteenth century by K\'iche\' nobles, using the Latin alphabet learned from missionaries to record what they said was the content of an older painted book. The Popol Vuh, a name usually rendered as the book of the mat or of the council, is therefore a colonial document that preserves a pre conquest tradition, and scholars weigh those two layers carefully.\n\nHow it reached print is a story of the nineteenth century. The copy was found in the library of the university at Guatemala City in the 1850s, first by the Austrian traveller Carl Scherzer and then by the French cleric Charles Etienne Brasseur de Bourbourg, who carried it to Europe and published a French version in 1861. Modern renderings begin with the Guatemalan scholar Adrian Recinos in 1947, and a widely read English translation of 1985 was made in consultation with a K\'iche\' daykeeper, on the argument that the calendar the text assumes is still in use.\n\nIt opens with creation. Gods in the sea and sky make the earth by speaking it, then fail repeatedly at making people: animals that cannot speak the names of their makers, creatures of mud that crumble, and wooden figures with no memory and no gratitude, who are destroyed and whose descendants are the monkeys. Between these attempts comes the long central story of the hero twins Hunahpu and Xbalanque, who play the ball game so noisily that the lords of the underworld summon them, and who survive a series of lethal trapped houses before defeating death by trickery. Only afterwards are true human beings made, out of ground maize.\n\nThe last section is dynastic history, tracing K\'iche\' lineages to the Spanish arrival. Painted pottery and carvings of the Classic Maya, deciphered long after the manuscript was published, show scenes matching these episodes, which established that the book carries genuinely ancient material.',
  },
  {
    num: 82, quizId: 'redact-10-26-26', live: '2026-10-26', dateLabel: 'October 26, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Nelson Mandela', aka: { mandella: 'mandela', mandelaa: 'mandela', nelsen: 'nelson' },
    text: 'For eighteen of his twenty seven years in prison he was held on Robben Island, off Cape Town, where the inmates broke limestone in a quarry whose glare damaged his eyes for the rest of his life. Prisoners were graded by race for rations and clothing, and he spent years in a cell of about two metres square. With other political prisoners he also ran an informal teaching programme that the men called their university.\n\nNelson Rolihlahla Mandela was born in 1918 in the Transkei, in the Eastern Cape, into a family attached to the Thembu royal house. He studied law, opened with Oliver Tambo the first Black law practice in Johannesburg in 1952, and rose through the African National Congress and its Youth League as the National Party built the system of racial legislation known as apartheid. After the Sharpeville shootings of 1960 and the banning of the Congress, he helped found its armed wing and went underground.\n\nArrested in 1962, he was sentenced at the Rivonia Trial in 1964 to life imprisonment for sabotage and conspiracy. His statement from the dock, which closed by describing a democratic and free society as an ideal for which he was prepared to die, was widely reprinted and became the best known text of the movement. He refused a conditional release in 1985 and walked free in February 1990.\n\nThe negotiations he then led with President F. W. de Klerk produced an interim constitution and the first election open to all South Africans, held in April 1994, after which he served a single term as president and did not seek a second. His government created the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, chaired by Desmond Tutu, which took testimony from victims and offered amnesty in exchange for full disclosure. He shared the Nobel Peace Prize with de Klerk in 1993, left office in 1999, and died in 2013.',
  },
  {
    num: 83, quizId: 'redact-10-27-26', live: '2026-10-27', dateLabel: 'October 27, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Andes', aka: { andies: 'andes', andees: 'andes', andeas: 'andes' },
    text: 'The Andes form the longest continental mountain range in the world, running about seven thousand kilometres down the western edge of South America from the Caribbean coast of Venezuela to the southern tip of Chile and Argentina. Seven countries share them. Aconcagua, on the Argentine side, reaches close to seven thousand metres and is the highest summit anywhere outside Asia.\n\nThe range is being built by subduction. The oceanic plate beneath the Pacific is sliding under the continent, buckling its edge upward and feeding a long chain of volcanoes, and the same process makes the whole margin one of the most earthquake prone regions on the planet. In the central section the mountains split into parallel ranges enclosing the Altiplano, a high plateau at around four thousand metres that holds Lake Titicaca and some of the driest country on Earth on its seaward flank.\n\nHuman use of that altitude is old and unusually complete. The potato was domesticated in the highlands, along with quinoa and a long list of tubers now barely known outside the region, and the llama and alpaca were the only large pack and wool animals in the Americas. Populations settled there over generations show measurable physiological differences in how they carry oxygen. The Inca state tied its territory together with a road network of tens of thousands of kilometres, carried across gorges on suspension bridges of woven grass that some communities still rebuild each year.\n\nMineral wealth drew colonial attention early, above all to the silver mountain at Potosi in Bolivia, worked under a forced labour draft that killed on a large scale. The bark of a highland tree yielded quinine, the first effective treatment for malaria. Today the range supplies much of the world\'s copper and lithium, and its glaciers, which are retreating, feed the rivers on which several major cities depend.',
  },
  {
    num: 84, quizId: 'redact-10-28-26', live: '2026-10-28', dateLabel: 'October 28, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 3, answer: 'The Serengeti', aka: { serengetti: 'serengeti', serengheti: 'serengeti', serangeti: 'serengeti' },
    text: 'The grass grows on ash. Eruptions from the volcanic highlands to the southeast have laid down deposits that weather into a mineral rich soil, and beneath it a cemented layer close to the surface stops tree roots, so the southern plains stay open grassland where woodland would otherwise take hold. The grasses that result are unusually high in calcium and phosphorus, which is why the herds calve there, and the whole rhythm of the ecosystem follows from that piece of geology.\n\nAbout one and a third million wildebeest, together with several hundred thousand zebra and gazelle, move around the region in a rough annual circuit that follows the rains, spreading north and west into the Kenyan section known as the Masai Mara in the dry months and returning south to drop their calves within a few weeks of one another early in the year. The river crossings, where crocodiles wait and the banks are steep, are the part most often filmed.\n\nThe Serengeti takes its name from a Maasai word usually translated as the place where the land runs on forever. Maasai herders have grazed cattle across it for generations. The Tanzanian national park was established in 1951 and now covers close to fifteen thousand square kilometres, at the core of a much larger protected area that crosses the Kenyan border.\n\nA cattle plague introduced to Africa in the nineteenth century held the grazing animals down for decades; after it was eliminated from the region in the 1960s the wildebeest population rose several times over. The German zoologist Bernhard Grzimek and his son Michael surveyed the migration from a small aircraft in the 1950s to argue for boundaries drawn around the animals\' movements rather than around convenient lines on a map. Michael was killed when the plane came down. Their film and book made the case internationally, and proposals to drive a road across the northern corridor are still contested.',
  },
  {
    num: 85, quizId: 'redact-10-29-26', live: '2026-10-29', dateLabel: 'October 29, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Katsushika Hokusai', aka: { hokusi: 'hokusai', hokasai: 'hokusai', hokusay: 'hokusai', katsushica: 'katsushika' },
    text: 'The print is often mistaken for a picture of a tsunami. It shows a deep trough and a curling crest tipped with claws of foam, three long boats of the kind used to rush fish to the markets of Edo, and behind them, small and still, the cone of Mount Fuji. It was published about 1831 in a series of thirty six views of that mountain, printed from carved cherry blocks in an edition that eventually ran to thousands of impressions, and it sold for roughly the price of a bowl of noodles.\n\nThe artist who designed it was born in Edo in 1760 and apprenticed to a woodblock carver before entering the studio of the print designer Katsukawa Shunsho. He changed his professional name some thirty times over a long career, a habit unusual even in that trade, and is said to have moved house nearly a hundred times. Katsushika Hokusai is the name under which most of his best known work appeared, adopted in middle age.\n\nHe worked in nearly every category of the Japanese print market: actors and courtesans early on, then landscapes, studies of birds and flowers, privately commissioned greeting prints, book illustration and erotica. From 1814 he issued the Manga, fifteen volumes of sketches of people, animals, tools, gods and grotesques, intended partly as a drawing manual and now the source of the modern use of that word. The famous wave owes some of its effect to imported Prussian blue, a cheap and stable pigment newly available in Japan.\n\nHe went on working into his late eighties, writing that nothing he had made before the age of seventy was of any account and that another decade might make him a real painter. His daughter Oi was an accomplished artist who worked alongside him. After Japan opened to trade his prints reached Europe in quantity and shaped the taste of Degas, Monet and Whistler, and the wave has since become one of the most reproduced images anywhere.',
  },
  {
    num: 86, quizId: 'redact-10-30-26', live: '2026-10-30', dateLabel: 'October 30, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 4, answer: 'The Astrolabe', aka: { astralabe: 'astrolabe', astrolobe: 'astrolabe', astrolab: 'astrolabe' },
    text: 'The idea behind it is a piece of Greek geometry. Project the sphere of the sky onto a flat surface from a single point and every circle on the sphere remains a circle on the plate, so the whole visible heaven can be engraved on a brass disc a few inches wide, with the horizon and the unequal hours drawn as arcs. Theon of Alexandria wrote a treatise on the instrument in the fourth century, and its underlying projection is often traced back to Hipparchus, five centuries earlier.\n\nAn astrolabe is a stack of brass plates held in a rim and hung from a ring so that it swings level. A plate engraved for one particular latitude sits at the bottom; above it turns the rete, an openwork sky map with barbed pointers for the bright stars and a circle for the path of the sun through the year. Rotating the rete against the plate reproduces the turning of the night. On the back, a pivoted sighting bar called the alidade measures the altitude of a star or of the sun.\n\nOne sighting and some turning will then give the time, the hour of sunrise, the latitude, the height of a tower or the direction of a distant place. Astronomers of the Islamic world refined it for centuries, using it to fix the times of prayer and the direction of Mecca; makers signed their work, among them a tenth century craftswoman of Aleppo remembered as al-Ijliya. The instrument reached Christian Europe through Spain, and Geoffrey Chaucer wrote a plain English manual for its use in 1391, addressed to a boy of ten.\n\nA heavy simplified ring version went to sea for latitude before the sextant replaced it. The surviving instruments, engraved and inlaid, are prized as much for their craft as for their astronomy.',
  },
  {
    num: 87, quizId: 'redact-10-31-26', live: '2026-10-31', dateLabel: 'October 31, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 4, answer: 'One Hundred Years of Solitude', aka: { solitud: 'solitude', solitute: 'solitude', hundread: 'hundred' },
    text: 'Gabriel Garcia Marquez often said he had struggled for years to find the voice for the book he wanted to write, and that he found it by remembering how his grandmother in Aracataca told outlandish things with a completely straight face. In 1965 he stopped work on everything else, shut himself in a room in Mexico City for about eighteen months while his wife ran up debts with the butcher and the landlord, and sold the car. Publishers in Buenos Aires printed the result in 1967.\n\nOne Hundred Years of Solitude follows seven generations of the Buendia family in Macondo, an invented town on the Colombian coast founded by a restless patriarch and his wife, who are also cousins and who fear that their line will produce a child with the tail of a pig. The same handful of names recurs down the generations, along with the same temperaments, so that the family seems to be circling rather than advancing.\n\nThe fantastic is reported in the same tone as the ordinary: a plague that takes away memory, a girl who rises into the sky, rain that lasts years, a wandering scholar who leaves behind parchments nobody can read. Set against this is a great deal of Colombian history, thinly disguised, including a massacre of striking banana workers that the authorities then insist never happened, based on events in the Colombian banana zone in 1928.\n\nThe novel became the emblem of the Latin American boom and of the manner critics call magical realism. Gregory Rabassa\'s English version of 1970 was praised by the author above his own text. It has sold tens of millions of copies in dozens of languages, and its author received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1982.',
  },
  {
    num: 88, quizId: 'redact-11-1-26', live: '2026-11-01', dateLabel: 'November 1, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 5, answer: 'Great Zimbabwe', aka: { zimbabway: 'zimbabwe', zimbawe: 'zimbabwe', zimbabwee: 'zimbabwe', greatzimbabwe: 'zimbabwe' },
    text: 'When the German geologist Karl Mauch reached a complex of stone walls on the plateau of southern Africa in 1871, he cut a sliver from a wooden lintel, decided it smelled like the cedar of his own pencil, and concluded that the timber must be Lebanese and the builders Phoenician. The ruins, he proposed, were the biblical Ophir, the ruin on the hill a copy of the temple of Solomon, and the walled enclosure below it a palace of the Queen of Sheba. Nothing supported this, but it suited the settler colony that grew up around the site, and for decades officials and antiquarians treated an African origin as unthinkable.\n\nThe evidence pointed the other way from early on. The English archaeologist Gertrude Caton Thompson, excavating in 1929 with an all female team, reported that the pottery, the layout and the datable imports all indicated a medieval African settlement, and radiocarbon dating later confirmed it. The builders were ancestors of the Shona, and the town was occupied from about the twelfth to the fifteenth century, at its height holding something in the range of ten to eighteen thousand people.\n\nOfficial pressure on the archaeology continued into living memory. Under the Rhodesian government of the 1960s and 1970s, museum staff and guides were discouraged, and at times forbidden, from telling visitors that the walls were African work, and more than one archaeologist left the country over it. The site is not unique either: several hundred smaller stone enclosures of the same tradition are known across the plateau, among them Khami and Danangombe, built as the centre of power moved west after the fifteenth century.\n\nThe walls are dry stone, laid without mortar in courses of granite blocks split from the surrounding outcrops by heating the rock and dousing it. The outer wall of the largest enclosure runs about two hundred and fifty metres around, stands eleven metres high in places, and encloses a solid conical tower whose purpose is unknown. Cattle, gold and ivory underwrote the settlement, and Chinese celadon, Persian glazed ware and glass beads recovered on the site show it traded with the Indian Ocean coast.\n\nEight soapstone birds were removed in the colonial period and most were later returned. The name Great Zimbabwe derives from a Shona phrase meaning houses of stone, and when the country became independent in 1980 it took the name of the ruins. The bird carving appears on its flag. The site was listed as World Heritage in 1986.',
  },
  {
    num: 89, quizId: 'redact-11-2-26', live: '2026-11-02', dateLabel: 'November 2, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 2, answer: 'Bamboo', aka: { bambo: 'bamboo', bambboo: 'bamboo', banboo: 'bamboo' },
    text: 'It is not a tree but a grass, and the largest grass there is. The woody stem, properly called a culm, is hollow between solid joints, which is what lets it stand tall while staying light, and it comes out of the ground at its final thickness and simply extends upward. Several species are among the fastest growing plants ever measured, adding most of a metre in a day in good conditions.\n\nBamboo runs to well over a thousand species, native to Asia, the Americas and Africa, from tropical lowland to Himalayan slope, with none native to Europe. Most spread underground by rhizomes, and many flower gregariously, meaning that every plant of a species flowers at roughly the same time across a whole region, after intervals that in some cases run to several decades, and then dies. In parts of northeast India this cycle has long been linked to plagues of rats that fatten on the seed and then move on to the rice.\n\nThe uses are hard to count. Scaffolding lashed from it still rises around tall buildings in Hong Kong. Split and flattened it becomes flooring, mats, hats, fans and basketry; pulped it makes paper; young shoots are boiled and eaten across East and Southeast Asia. Chinese scribes wrote on strips of it bound with cord before paper was cheap, and Sichuan drillers used it for well casing and cable. Flutes such as the shakuhachi and the dizi are cut from it, and Thomas Edison tried a carbonised filament of it in early lamps.\n\nIts tensile strength is often compared with that of steel, a comparison that flatters it but points at something real, and engineers have worked to grade and standardise it as a structural material. Giant pandas eat almost nothing else, which ties their fortunes to a plant that dies back on a schedule of its own.',
  },
  {
    num: 90, quizId: 'redact-11-3-26', live: '2026-11-03', dateLabel: 'November 3, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Nile', aka: { nyle: 'nile', niile: 'nile' },
    text: 'The Nile is the great river of northeastern Africa, flowing north for roughly six thousand six hundred kilometres from the highlands of the interior to a broad delta on the Mediterranean. It has long been described as the longest river in the world, though careful measurement of the Amazon has made that claim a matter of argument rather than a settled fact, and the answer depends on which headstream is counted.\n\nIt has two main branches. The White Nile carries a steady flow out of the great lakes region, drawing ultimately on streams in Burundi and Rwanda that feed Lake Victoria. The Blue Nile rises at Lake Tana in the Ethiopian highlands and is fed by summer monsoon rain, so it runs low in winter and enormous in late summer. The two meet at Khartoum in Sudan, and it is the Ethiopian branch that supplies most of the water and nearly all of the silt. The Atbara, joining a few hundred kilometres further down, is the last tributary of any kind; from there the river crosses a series of rocky cataracts and runs more than two thousand kilometres to the sea without receiving another.\n\nBefore the modern dams the flood arrived each summer with great regularity, spreading fertile mud across the valley floor. Egyptian administration was built around measuring and taxing it, and stone gauges called nilometers recorded the height of each rise. The completion of the high dam at Aswan in 1970 ended the annual flood, created a reservoir several hundred kilometres long, and forced the dismantling and relocation of temples that would otherwise have been drowned.\n\nEuropean geographers argued for centuries about where the water came from. John Hanning Speke reached Lake Victoria in 1858 and named it as the source, a conclusion his companion Richard Burton disputed for years. Today the basin is shared by eleven countries, and how its water should be divided remains a live diplomatic question.',
  },
  {
    num: 91, quizId: 'redact-11-4-26', live: '2026-11-04', dateLabel: 'November 4, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 3, answer: 'The Great Fire of London', aka: { greatfire: 'fire', londen: 'london', lundon: 'london' },
    text: 'Thomas Farriner baked ship\'s biscuit for the navy, and it was in his premises on Pudding Lane, in the small hours of Sunday 2 September 1666, that the blaze began. The lord mayor was roused, looked at it, judged it trifling and went back to bed, a decision recorded with some relish by the diarist Samuel Pepys, who buried his wine and a parmesan cheese in his garden as the flames came nearer. The summer had been dry, an easterly wind was blowing, and the riverside warehouses were packed with pitch, oil, tallow and coal.\n\nIt burned for four days. Houses of timber with jettied upper storeys leaned toward one another across narrow lanes, and the usual remedy, pulling down buildings to make a gap, was applied late and grudgingly because of arguments about who would pay. Only when the king and his brother took charge of the firefighting, and gunpowder was used to clear wider breaks, did the advance slow, helped by the wind dropping.\n\nAbout thirteen thousand houses, eighty seven parish churches, the Royal Exchange and the old cathedral were destroyed, and something like four fifths of the walled city was left in ruins. Very few deaths were formally recorded, though the figure is almost certainly an undercount of the poor and unregistered. Tens of thousands camped in the fields outside. A French watchmaker confessed to starting it and was hanged, on evidence that could not have been true.\n\nThe rebuilding was governed by an act of 1667 that required brick and stone, widened the main streets and fixed standard house types. Sweeping new plans by Christopher Wren and John Evelyn were set aside because the old property boundaries could not be undone, but Wren designed the replacement cathedral and dozens of parish churches. The Great Fire of London also prompted the first businesses selling insurance against it.',
  },
  {
    num: 92, quizId: 'redact-11-5-26', live: '2026-11-05', dateLabel: 'November 5, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 3, answer: 'Gunpowder', aka: { gunpowdar: 'gunpowder', gunpower: 'gunpowder', gunpouder: 'gunpowder' },
    text: 'Chinese alchemists of the Tang period were hunting for an elixir of immortality when they found something closer to its opposite. Surviving recipes warn that saltpetre heated with sulphur and carbon rich matter such as honey will flare up, singe the beard and burn down the workshop. The earliest written formulas that survive appear in a Song military compendium of 1044, the Wujing Zongyao, which gives three of them along with instructions for bombs to be flung by catapult.\n\nGunpowder in its classic black form is roughly three quarters potassium nitrate, with charcoal and sulphur making up the rest. The saltpetre supplies the oxygen, so the mixture burns without air, extremely fast, and in a closed space that speed becomes an explosion. Chinese armies used it first in incendiary arrows and grenades, then in the fire lance, a bamboo or paper tube lashed to a spear that spat flame and scraps of metal, and by the thirteenth century in cast barrels of iron and bronze.\n\nKnowledge of it moved west along the trade and war routes of the Mongol century. Roger Bacon described a formula in Europe around 1267, cannon appear in European accounts by the 1320s, and within two centuries artillery had made high stone curtain walls obsolete and forced the low angled bastion fortress into being. A crucial refinement came in the fifteenth century: wetting the mixture into a cake and breaking it into grains, which stopped the ingredients separating in transport and burned far more powerfully.\n\nThe limiting ingredient was always saltpetre, scraped from stable floors and dung heaps until bulk imports from Bihar in India supplied the European powers. Smokeless propellants based on nitrocellulose displaced it from firearms in the 1880s, and modern explosives displaced it from quarrying. It survives in fireworks, in fuses and in the muzzle loading sports, still doing what it did in the first place.',
  },
  {
    num: 93, quizId: 'redact-11-6-26', live: '2026-11-06', dateLabel: 'November 6, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Ibn Battuta', aka: { battutah: 'battuta', batuta: 'battuta', battouta: 'battuta', ibnbattuta: 'battuta' },
    text: 'The book was not written by the traveller. Back in Morocco after nearly three decades on the road, he was assigned by the sultan a young Andalusian scholar, Ibn Juzayy, who took down the account, polished the prose, and lifted passages from an earlier writer to fill out descriptions the narrator had lost or perhaps never made. The result, whose title translates roughly as a gift to those who contemplate the wonders of cities and the marvels of travelling, is usually shortened to the Rihla, meaning simply the journey.\n\nHe set out from Tangier in 1325, aged twenty one, to make the pilgrimage to Mecca, and did not come home for twenty four years. The route ran through Egypt, Syria and the Hijaz, then east into Iraq and Persia, down the Red Sea and along the Swahili coast as far as Kilwa, back through Oman and the Gulf, and north across Anatolia and the steppe to the Golden Horde. Everywhere he sought out judges, scholars and rulers, since he was a qualified jurist of the Maliki school and hospitality to such men was expected of the pious rich.\n\nIbn Battuta reached India about 1333 and served the sultan of Delhi, Muhammad bin Tughluq, as a judge for several years, an unpredictable employer whose favour he was lucky to survive. Sent on an embassy to China, he was shipwrecked, spent time in the Maldives, where he served as a judge again, and eventually reached the Chinese coast, though this stretch of the narrative is the most doubted. A later journey took him across the Sahara to the empire of Mali.\n\nHistorians treat the Rihla as a mixture of eyewitness reporting, borrowed description and plain error, and several legs of the route are disputed. What is not disputed is its range. It remains one of the fullest portraits of the fourteenth century Muslim world, from Granada to Bengal, by a man who claimed a distance no medieval traveller is known to have matched. He died in Morocco some time in the 1360s or 1370s.',
  },
  {
    num: 94, quizId: 'redact-11-7-26', live: '2026-11-07', dateLabel: 'November 7, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Hatshepsut', aka: { hatchepsut: 'hatshepsut', hatshepsit: 'hatshepsut', hatsheput: 'hatshepsut', hatshepsute: 'hatshepsut' },
    text: 'Archaeologists clearing a terraced temple of colonnades at Deir el Bahari, cut back into the cliff on the west bank of the Nile opposite Thebes, found that a great many of its reliefs had been deliberately damaged. Faces and names had been chiselled out and in places replaced with those of other kings. Later digging nearby turned up the statues that had stood there, broken up and tipped into a quarry pit. The erasure was systematic but incomplete, and it is largely from what the workmen missed that the reign can be reconstructed.\n\nShe was the daughter of one king, the wife of the next, and on his death about 1479 BC became regent for her young stepson. Within a few years she had assumed the full titles of a king herself, and she ruled for roughly two decades. Egypt had known women who governed before, but none who reigned so long or left so much building behind.\n\nHatshepsut is usually shown in the conventional male royal costume, with kilt and false beard, which is best read as the standard iconography of kingship rather than as any attempt at disguise. Her temple reliefs advance two claims to legitimacy: that the god Amun fathered her, and that her father had named her his successor. The reign was largely peaceful and rich in trade, and its most celebrated relief records an expedition down the Red Sea to the land of Punt, whose ships come back loaded with myrrh trees, ebony, ivory and live baboons.\n\nHer chief official, Senenmut, oversaw much of the building and appears in unusual places within the temple, which has generated a great deal of speculation on very little evidence. The erasure of her name seems to have begun well into her successor\'s reign, decades after her death, which argues against a simple story of personal revenge; the likelier explanations concern the succession and the official record of kingship. Left out of later king lists, she was recovered only by excavation and by scholars reading the defaced walls.',
  },
  {
    num: 95, quizId: 'redact-11-8-26', live: '2026-11-08', dateLabel: 'November 8, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 5, answer: 'The Tambora Eruption', aka: { tambura: 'tambora', tamborra: 'tambora', tomboro: 'tambora', erruption: 'eruption' },
    text: 'The summer of 1816 did not arrive in the northern hemisphere. Snow fell in New England in June, ice formed on ponds in July, and the maize crop failed from Quebec to Connecticut. Grain harvests collapsed across Europe, prices doubled or worse, and there were food riots in Switzerland and eastern France, the last serious subsistence crisis the continent would see. In Geneva a party of English writers, kept indoors by relentless rain, passed the time inventing ghost stories, one of which became a famous novel. Nobody at the time knew why the weather had failed.\n\nThe cause lay on the island of Sumbawa in what is now Indonesia. In April 1815 a mountain there, quiet for centuries and reckoned at about four thousand three hundred metres, blew its upper third away. The explosive phase on the tenth of that month sent a column an estimated forty kilometres into the sky. The detonations were reported from Sumatra, some two thousand kilometres off, where garrisons took them for gunfire. Ash fell thickly across neighbouring islands and daylight failed for two days. The mountain was left about fourteen hundred metres shorter, with a caldera six kilometres across.\n\nThe Tambora Eruption is the largest recorded in the modern era. Its local toll was severe, both immediately and through the crop failure and disease that followed on Sumbawa and Lombok, and a small kingdom on the mountain\'s flank was destroyed, its language surviving only in a short wordlist collected shortly before. Estimates of the volume of material thrown out run to something like a hundred and fifty cubic kilometres, and the event is rated seven on the volcanic explosivity index, the highest given to any eruption of the last five centuries. Death tolls for Sumbawa and Lombok vary widely, between tens of thousands and more than a hundred thousand, most of them from starvation rather than from the blast itself. Excavation from 2004 onward uncovered a house and its occupants buried in the deposits, with carbonised furniture and bronze bowls beside them.\n\nWhat carried the effects around the globe was sulphur. Sulphate droplets injected into the stratosphere spread as a thin veil that reflected sunlight and cooled the surface by a fraction of a degree on average, enough to shorten growing seasons for two or three years. The connection between the volcano and the cold summer was not firmly established until the twentieth century, and studies of it now inform models of how a large eruption or a nuclear exchange would alter climate.',
  },
  {
    num: 96, quizId: 'redact-11-9-26', live: '2026-11-09', dateLabel: 'November 9, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Florence Nightingale', aka: { nightengale: 'nightingale', nightinggale: 'nightingale', florance: 'florence' },
    text: 'The diagram is still reproduced in textbooks on statistical graphics. A circle is divided into twelve wedges, one for each month of the war, and each wedge is shaded to separate the men who died of wounds from those who died of other causes and those who died of preventable disease. The areas for disease dwarf the rest. It was drawn to be understood by ministers and generals who would not read a table of figures, and it worked.\n\nFlorence Nightingale was born in 1820 in the Italian city she was named for, into a wealthy and well connected English family, and trained against the wishes of her parents as a nurse in Germany and in Paris. In 1854, after newspaper reports on the treatment of British casualties in the Crimean War, the war secretary Sidney Herbert asked her to take a party of thirty eight nurses to the military hospital at Scutari, across the water from Constantinople.\n\nConditions there were worse than in the field. The building stood over blocked sewers, water was short, and the death rate rose through the first winter before a sanitary commission arrived to flush the drains and improve the ventilation. She has often been credited with cutting the mortality by nursing alone, a claim she did not make herself; her later analysis of the figures concluded that most of the deaths had been preventable and that sanitation had made the difference.\n\nShe came home in 1856 a national figure, having been described in dispatches carrying a lamp through the wards at night, and she used that standing for the rest of a long life spent largely in her room in poor health. She published Notes on Nursing in 1859, founded a training school at St Thomas\' Hospital in London in 1860, and pressed committees and viceroys on the sanitary state of the army in India. She was elected the first woman member of the Statistical Society of London in 1858, and she died in 1910.',
  },
  {
    num: 97, quizId: 'redact-11-10-26', live: '2026-11-10', dateLabel: 'November 10, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Confucius', aka: { confucious: 'confucius', confusius: 'confucius', konfucius: 'confucius', confuscius: 'confucius' },
    text: 'The book that carries his teaching was not written by him. The Analects is a compilation of short sayings and exchanges, assembled by pupils and by pupils of pupils over perhaps a century and a half after his death, and its twenty books vary in style and in reliability. Much of it takes the form of replies: the master said, when asked about government, or about ritual, or about the conduct proper to a son.\n\nConfucius is the Latinised form of Kong Fuzi, meaning Master Kong. He was born about 551 BC in the small state of Lu, in what is now Shandong province, during the long decline of the Zhou dynasty. He held minor office there and then spent years travelling between courts in search of a ruler who would apply his ideas. He found none, and came back to teach.\n\nHis concern was less with gods than with conduct. He argued that order in a state grows out of order in a family and in the self, that a ruler governs best by moral example rather than by punishment, and that the old rites, properly understood, train people in benevolence. Two terms recur: ren, usually rendered as humaneness, and li, the forms of ritual and propriety. He also stated a version of the golden rule in the negative, telling a questioner not to impose on others what he did not want for himself.\n\nHis pupils carried the teaching on, and later thinkers such as Mencius and Xunzi developed it in different directions. From the Han dynasty onward his teaching was state orthodoxy, and the classics associated with him later supplied the matter of the civil service examinations, which shaped Chinese officialdom until 1905. The tradition spread through Korea, Japan and Vietnam. Temples were built in his honour, his descendants were ennobled for many centuries, and his reputation has been attacked and restored repeatedly, most sharply during the political campaigns of the twentieth century.',
  },
  {
    num: 98, quizId: 'redact-11-11-26', live: '2026-11-11', dateLabel: 'November 11, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 3, answer: 'Angkor Wat', aka: { angkorwat: 'angkor', ankor: 'angkor', angkhor: 'angkor', angcor: 'angkor' },
    text: 'The French naturalist Henri Mouhot, who died in 1861 and whose journals appeared soon afterwards, is often credited with discovering the temple in the Cambodian forest, and the story of a jungle ruin lost for centuries has proved hard to kill. It was never lost. Portuguese and Spanish missionaries described it in the sixteenth century, a Japanese pilgrim drew a plan of it around 1630, and Buddhist monks had been living on the site and maintaining it continuously since the building stopped being a Hindu shrine.\n\nIt was raised in the first half of the twelfth century under the Khmer king Suryavarman the Second, and dedicated not to Shiva, as most Khmer state temples were, but to Vishnu. Unusually, it faces west rather than east, which has led many scholars to think it was intended as the king\'s funerary monument. The plan is a mountain in stone: five towers in a quincunx standing for the peaks of Mount Meru, set on stepped terraces inside a rectangular wall and a moat some two hundred metres wide. By area it is generally called the largest religious structure in the world.\n\nThe sandstone came from quarries at the foot of a hill about forty kilometres away and appears to have been floated in along canals. Galleries running for hundreds of metres carry carved reliefs of battles, of the Hindu epics, and of gods and demons hauling on a serpent to churn the ocean for the elixir of immortality.\n\nAngkor Wat stood at the edge of a capital of perhaps several hundred thousand people, fed by vast rectangular reservoirs and a network of canals whose extent airborne laser survey has only recently made clear. French archaeologists began clearing and rebuilding work in 1908. The temple appears on the national flag, and after years of war, looting and landmines around the wider site it is again the country\'s principal destination for visitors.',
  },
  {
    num: 99, quizId: 'redact-11-12-26', live: '2026-11-12', dateLabel: 'November 12, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Harriet Tubman', aka: { tubmann: 'tubman', tubmen: 'tubman', harriett: 'harriet', harryet: 'harriet' },
    text: 'In June 1863, on the Combahee River in South Carolina, three Union gunboats worked upriver past batteries and mines, guided by a scout who had spent weeks gathering intelligence from enslaved people along the banks. The raid burned rice stores and plantation property and carried away more than seven hundred people, who waded and swam out to the boats. The scout was a woman who had been enslaved herself, and she is generally described as the first woman to lead an armed expedition in that war.\n\nShe was born Araminta Ross, about 1822, on the Eastern Shore of Maryland, and later took her mother\'s first name and her husband\'s surname. As a girl she was struck in the head by an iron weight thrown by an overseer at somebody else, an injury that left her with seizures and vivid dreams for the rest of her life. In 1849 she escaped alone to Philadelphia, guided by the North Star and by a network of sympathisers.\n\nHarriet Tubman then went back. Over about a decade she made some thirteen journeys into Maryland and brought out roughly seventy people, family members first among them, working mostly in winter and at night. The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 obliged her to take her passengers as far as Canada. Later tallies crediting her with hundreds of rescues appear to derive from an admiring early biography rather than from her own careful accounts.\n\nAfter the war she settled at Auburn in New York, cared for elderly and destitute neighbours and eventually founded a home for them, and spoke for women\'s suffrage alongside Susan B. Anthony. She spent decades petitioning for a pension for her own war service and was granted one first as a soldier\'s widow, and only in 1899 at a rate that acknowledged what she had done herself. She died in 1913. A proposal to place her portrait on United States currency, announced in 2016, has been repeatedly delayed.',
  },
  {
    num: 100, quizId: 'redact-11-13-26', live: '2026-11-13', dateLabel: 'November 13, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 4, answer: 'Chichen Itza', aka: { chichenitza: 'chichen', chitzen: 'chichen', itzah: 'itza', chichenitzah: 'chichen' },
    text: 'Northern Yucatan has no rivers. Rain drains straight through the limestone, and settlement there depended on cenotes, the sinkholes where the rock roof has fallen in and exposed the water table. One Maya city grew up beside two large ones, and the northern sinkhole was not used for drinking but for offerings. Between 1904 and 1910 an American consul, Edward Thompson, dredged and dived it, bringing up gold discs, jade, rubber figures, copal incense and human remains, and shipping much of it out of the country, which led to a long legal dispute.\n\nThe city flourished from roughly the ninth to the thirteenth century, later than the great southern lowland centres and outlasting them. Its most photographed building is a stepped pyramid of nine terraces dedicated to the feathered serpent, with four stairways of ninety one steps that together with the top platform total the days of a year. On the afternoons around the equinoxes the terrace corners throw a line of triangular shadows onto one balustrade, suggesting a serpent descending, an effect visible for some weeks and much amplified by modern crowds. An earlier pyramid, with a red jaguar throne inside, is buried within it.\n\nChichen Itza also holds the largest ball court in the Americas, about one hundred and sixty metres long, its walls carved with players and with the ritual outcome of the game, and a round tower whose windows line up with limits of the movement of the planet Venus. Much of the sculpture and layout resembles the distant city of Tula, which was long explained as a Toltec conquest and is now more often read as shared style and exchange.\n\nThe name is usually translated as the mouth of the well of the Itza, the group who ruled it. The ruins were in private hands until the Mexican state bought the core in 2010. They receive over two million visitors a year.',
  },
  {
    num: 101, quizId: 'redact-11-14-26', live: '2026-11-14', dateLabel: 'November 14, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Gabriela Mistral', aka: { mistrall: 'mistral', mistrale: 'mistral', gabriella: 'gabriela', gabrielle: 'gabriela' },
    text: 'The name on the books was invented. She was born Lucila Godoy Alcayaga in 1889 in the Elqui valley, a narrow line of vineyards between bare hills in the north of Chile, and began publishing verse and articles in provincial newspapers while still a teenager. She had almost no formal schooling and was refused a place at a teacher training college, qualifying instead by examination, which did not stop her from spending most of her working life in classrooms and later in ministries of education.\n\nHer reputation was made in 1914, when three poems entered under a pen name won a national competition in Santiago. Gabriela Mistral is often said to join the names of the Italian poet Gabriele D\'Annunzio and the Provencal poet Frederic Mistral, though she gave other explanations at different times and the question is not settled. A first collection, Desolacion, was published in New York in 1922 at the urging of an American scholarly society, and Ternura and Tala followed.\n\nBy then she was also an educational administrator of standing. The Mexican minister of education Jose Vasconcelos invited her in 1922 to help with a rural schooling and library programme, and she spent most of the rest of her life abroad, holding Chilean consular posts in Madrid, Lisbon, Nice, Petropolis and Los Angeles and teaching at American colleges. Her poems circulated in school readers across Latin America, many of them written for or about children.\n\nIn 1945 she became the first Latin American writer to receive the Nobel Prize in Literature, cited for lyric poetry that had made her name a symbol of the idealistic aspiration of her whole part of the world. She assigned the proceeds of Tala to children displaced by the Spanish civil war. She died in New York in 1957, and her body was taken back to Chile for a state funeral and buried at Montegrande, in the valley where she grew up.',
  },
  {
    num: 102, quizId: 'redact-11-15-26', live: '2026-11-15', dateLabel: 'November 15, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 5, answer: 'Ibn Khaldun', aka: { khaldoun: 'khaldun', khaldon: 'khaldun', kaldun: 'khaldun', khaldum: 'khaldun' },
    text: 'He wrote most of it in four or five months, in a castle in what is now Algeria, having withdrawn there from politics with his family in 1375. The Muqaddima, meaning the introduction, was intended as a preface to a universal history of the Arabs and the Berbers, and it grew into a book in its own right: an attempt to set out the principles by which a historian might tell true reports from false, and to explain why states rise and fall.\n\nIts central idea is asabiyya, usually translated as group feeling or social cohesion. A hard life on the desert margins, he argued, breeds solidarity; a group with strong cohesion overcomes a settled dynasty grown soft on luxury and taxation; the conquerors then settle in turn, their solidarity decays over three or four generations, and another group replaces them. He treated this as a natural cycle to be described rather than as a moral tale, and he built it from close observation of the North African dynasties he had served and watched.\n\nIbn Khaldun was born in Tunis in 1332 into a family of Andalusian officials, was orphaned by plague in his teens, and spent his career as a secretary, judge and envoy at courts in Fez, Granada, Tlemcen and Cairo, imprisoned or exiled several times as his patrons fell. Sent to negotiate with Timur outside Damascus in 1401, he was lowered over the city wall in a basket to reach him and left an account of their conversations.\n\nHe was appointed Maliki chief judge in Cairo, an office he held and lost several times, and he made himself unpopular by refusing the customary accommodations of the post. He gave chapters to demolishing the claims of alchemy and of judicial astrology, on the ground that their practitioners could show no results, and he wrote of the plague that had carried off his parents as an event which emptied the world and required a new history to be written.\n\nHe wrote too on the economics of taxation, arguing that light taxes on a growing economy yield more than heavy ones, and on the way crafts and learning flourish in cities. His work was little read outside Ottoman circles until European and Arab scholars took it up in the nineteenth century. He is now commonly described as a forerunner of sociology, economics and historiography, a description that flatters the modern disciplines as much as it does him. He died in Cairo in 1406.',
  },
  {
    num: 103, quizId: 'redact-11-16-26', live: '2026-11-16', dateLabel: 'November 16, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 2, answer: 'The Sahara', aka: { sahra: 'sahara', sahaara: 'sahara', saharah: 'sahara' },
    text: 'The Sahara is the largest hot desert on Earth, covering something over nine million square kilometres of northern Africa, an area comparable to China or to the United States. It reaches from the Atlantic coast to the Red Sea and from the Mediterranean and the Atlas ranges south to the belt of dry grassland called the Sahel, and parts of eleven countries lie within it.\n\nThe popular image of endless dunes is misleading. Only about a quarter of the surface is sand sea, and the rest is bare rock plateau, gravel plain, dry valley and mountain. The Ahaggar highlands in southern Algeria and the Tibesti massif in Chad are volcanic ranges, the second of them rising above three thousand metres, and both see occasional snow, while the Qattara Depression in Egypt lies more than a hundred metres below sea level. Rain is scarce and unreliable, and the daily swing in temperature can be extreme, since dry air holds little heat after sunset.\n\nIt has not always been dry. Between roughly eleven thousand and five thousand years ago, during a period sometimes called the green phase, monsoon rains pushed north and the region held lakes, rivers, grassland and large populations of grazing animals. Rock paintings and engravings at Tassili in Algeria show herds of cattle and giraffe, and a painted shelter in the Egyptian desert shows human figures who appear to be swimming. The shift back to desert is generally explained by slow changes in the tilt and wobble of the planet altering where the rains fall.\n\nTrade routes crossed it long before the modern borders were drawn, carrying salt cut from mines in the interior southward to the Sahel, and gold, ivory and enslaved people northward, with the camel making the long stages possible from about the third century. Dust lifted from a single dry basin in Chad is carried on the wind across the Atlantic and is thought to fertilise soils in South America.',
  },
  {
    num: 104, quizId: 'redact-11-17-26', live: '2026-11-17', dateLabel: 'November 17, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 2, answer: 'Coffee', aka: { coffe: 'coffee', cofee: 'coffee', coffey: 'coffee' },
    text: 'Coffee is brewed from the roasted and ground seeds of a shrub of the genus Coffea. Two species carry almost the whole trade: arabica, which wants altitude and cool nights and is prized for flavour, and canephora, sold as robusta, which is hardier, more bitter and higher in caffeine. The seeds sit two to a small red fruit, and are freed either by washing the pulp away or by drying the whole fruit in the sun.\n\nThe plant is native to the highlands of Ethiopia, where an often repeated legend credits a goatherd with noticing that his animals grew lively after eating the berries. The documented history begins later and across the Red Sea, in Yemen, where Sufi orders were drinking it by the fifteenth century to stay awake through night devotions, and where the port of Mocha gave its name to the trade. Houses selling the drink spread through Mecca, Cairo and Istanbul, and were shut down by nervous authorities more than once as places where men talked too freely.\n\nEurope took to it in the seventeenth century, and the same pattern followed. The London houses became clearing rooms for news and business, and the marine insurance market at Lloyd\'s grew out of one of them. Dutch and French planters carried seedlings to Java, the Caribbean and South America, where the crop was worked largely by enslaved and indentured labour.\n\nBrazil has been the largest producer since the nineteenth century, with Vietnam now second. A fungus called leaf rust destroyed the plantations of Ceylon in the 1870s and pushed that island to tea, and the disease remains a standing threat. World consumption is usually estimated at around two billion cups a day, and the crop supports many millions of smallholders whose incomes swing with a price set far away.',
  },
  {
    num: 105, quizId: 'redact-11-18-26', live: '2026-11-18', dateLabel: 'November 18, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 3, answer: 'Simon Bolivar', aka: { boliver: 'bolivar', bolivaar: 'bolivar', symon: 'simon' },
    text: 'In 1819, at the head of an army that mixed llanero horsemen from the Venezuelan plains with British and Irish veterans of the Napoleonic wars, a Creole general took a route his enemies thought impassable. He crossed the flooded lowlands of the Orinoco in the rainy season and then the Andes by the paramo of Pisba, losing horses and men to the cold at four thousand metres, and came down on the far side into New Granada. Days later he destroyed the royalist force at Boyaca and rode into Bogota.\n\nSimon Bolivar was born in Caracas in 1783 into a wealthy landowning family, was orphaned young, and was educated in part by Simon Rodriguez, a tutor of radical views who took him to Europe. He came home committed to independence from Spain. The first republics he helped proclaim collapsed twice, once after an earthquake that royalist clergy read as a judgement, and exile in Jamaica and then Haiti followed. Haitian support was given on the condition that he free the enslaved in any territory he took.\n\nHe is remembered as the Liberator, a title granted him by the city of Caracas in 1813. Campaigns he led or directed contributed to the independence of the territories that became Venezuela, Colombia, Panama, Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia, the last of which took his name. His ablest lieutenant, Antonio Jose de Sucre, won the decisive battle at Ayacucho in 1824.\n\nHis political project outlasted him less well than his military one. He founded the union usually called Gran Colombia and served as its president, argued in the Cartagena manifesto and the Angostura address for a strong central authority, and grew convinced that the region could not be governed by the constitutions it kept writing. The union was breaking apart in the year he died of tuberculosis, in 1830, at Santa Marta on the Colombian coast, aged forty seven.',
  },
  {
    num: 106, quizId: 'redact-11-19-26', live: '2026-11-19', dateLabel: 'November 19, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 3, answer: 'The Boomerang', aka: { boomarang: 'boomerang', bommerang: 'boomerang', boomerrang: 'boomerang' },
    text: 'The oldest known example is not Australian. A curved implement carved from mammoth ivory, recovered from Oblazowa Cave in southern Poland, has been dated to roughly thirty thousand years ago, and possibly a good deal older. Curved throwing sticks of similar shape were buried with Tutankhamun, and hunters in the American southwest used them on rabbits. A flat piece of shaped wood thrown at game turns out to be close to a human universal, and almost none of these devices were ever meant to return.\n\nThe boomerang is an Australian Aboriginal tool, and the word entered English from the Dharug language of the Sydney region in the early nineteenth century. Most Australian types do not come back either. The heavy non returning kind, called a kylie in parts of the west, is thrown flat and hard at kangaroo, emu or a flock, and doubles as a digging tool, a fire saw and a percussion instrument beaten in pairs to keep time at ceremonies.\n\nThe returning kind is lighter, more sharply bent and shaped in cross section like a pair of wings. Spun edge on and thrown almost vertically, it flies because each arm generates lift; the arm advancing into the airflow moves faster and lifts harder, and the resulting torque acts on a spinning object at right angles to where it is applied, so the plane of the circle tips over steadily and the flight curls back to the thrower. Its practical use was limited, mostly to startling waterfowl into nets and to teaching the throw.\n\nCompetitive throwing has world championships and record categories for distance, accuracy, fast catch and time aloft, the best flights staying up for minutes. The object has also become a national emblem and a heavily marketed souvenir, and its name is now a common word for any scheme that comes back to strike the person who launched it.',
  },
  {
    num: 107, quizId: 'redact-11-20-26', live: '2026-11-20', dateLabel: 'November 20, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 4, answer: 'Toussaint Louverture', aka: { ouverture: 'louverture', louvertur: 'louverture', toussant: 'toussaint', tousaint: 'toussaint' },
    text: 'He was about fifty before he took any part in public events, and had been free for perhaps two decades before that. Born into slavery on the Breda plantation in the north of the French colony of Saint Domingue, probably in 1743, he worked as a coachman and a healer, learned to read, and was manumitted in the 1770s. He then rented land and for a time held enslaved people himself, a fact his admirers long passed over and which historians now discuss openly.\n\nThe colony was the richest in the Caribbean, producing a large share of the sugar and coffee sold in Europe on the labour of half a million enslaved people. When they rose in August 1791 he joined the insurgents, at first under Spanish colours, and emerged as their most capable commander. In 1794, after the French republic abolished slavery in its colonies, he changed sides and fought for France.\n\nToussaint Louverture added the second name himself, and contemporaries offered various explanations for it. By 1798 he had induced the British to withdraw, and by 1801 he governed the whole island, having marched into the Spanish east and freed the enslaved there as well. The constitution he promulgated that year abolished slavery permanently and named him governor for life, leaving France a nominal role; it also compelled former field workers to stay on the plantations under paid contract, since he judged that the export economy would otherwise collapse.\n\nNapoleon Bonaparte sent an expedition under his brother in law to restore French control and, it was widely suspected, slavery with it. After months of fighting and negotiation the governor was seized at a parley in 1802 and shipped to France, where he died in a cold fortress in the Jura in April 1803. The struggle went on without him and ended in the independence of Haiti in 1804, the first state in the Americas founded by people who had freed themselves from slavery.',
  },
  {
    num: 108, quizId: 'redact-11-21-26', live: '2026-11-21', dateLabel: 'November 21, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 4, answer: 'Cuneiform', aka: { cuniform: 'cuneiform', cunieform: 'cuneiform', quneiform: 'cuneiform' },
    text: 'Before there was writing there was accounting. For thousands of years the peoples of Mesopotamia tracked sheep, grain and oil with small clay tokens, sometimes sealed inside a hollow clay ball with impressions of the tokens pressed on its surface so that the contents could be checked without breaking it open. Around 3300 BC, in the southern city of Uruk, somebody grasped that the marks on the outside were doing the work by themselves, and began pressing signs straight into flat tablets.\n\nCuneiform takes its name from the Latin for wedge, after the shape left by the corner of a cut reed pushed into damp clay. The first signs were simplified pictures, but a stylus favours straight strokes, so the pictures were rotated, pared down and rebuilt out of wedges until most were no longer recognisable as images. A sign could stand for a word or for a syllable, and that flexibility let the script be borrowed by languages unrelated to the one it was built for: Sumerian first, then Akkadian, Elamite, Hittite, Hurrian and Old Persian.\n\nIt was in use for roughly three thousand years, the last datable tablets being astronomical texts of the first century. Most of what survives is business: receipts, loans, contracts, ration lists and the exercises of schoolboys learning the signs. Among the rest are law collections, omen manuals, letters between kings and the poems gathered in the library assembled at Nineveh for Ashurbanipal. Clay is durable, and the fires that destroyed cities baked their archives hard.\n\nReading resumed only in the nineteenth century, through a trilingual inscription cut high on a cliff at Behistun in Iran by order of a Persian king, copied at some risk by Henry Rawlinson and others. Several hundred thousand tablets now sit in museum collections, and a large share of them have never been published.',
  },
  {
    num: 109, quizId: 'redact-11-22-26', live: '2026-11-22', dateLabel: 'November 22, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 5, answer: 'Quinine', aka: { quinnine: 'quinine', quinene: 'quinine', kinine: 'quinine' },
    text: 'A story long repeated in Europe held that in 1638 the wife of the Spanish viceroy of Peru was cured of a fever in Lima by a powdered bark, and carried the remedy home. Linnaeus named the tree for her a century later, misspelling the name in the process. The countess almost certainly never took it; her household diary records that her husband, not she, was ill, and she died before reaching Spain. What is not in doubt is that Jesuits in the Andes were sending the bark to Rome by the 1630s, where it was sold as the powder of the fathers and distrusted by Protestants for exactly that reason.\n\nQuinine is an alkaloid extracted from the bark of cinchona trees, which grow wild on the wet eastern slopes of the Andes. Two French pharmacists, Pelletier and Caventou, isolated it in Paris in 1820, which turned an unreliable powder into a measurable dose. It attacks the malaria parasite in the blood, and for a century it was the only drug that reliably did so, which is one reason European armies and administrators were able to press into regions of Africa and Asia that had killed earlier expeditions.\n\nDemand stripped the Andean forests, and the trade moved. Seeds obtained in Bolivia by a collector named Charles Ledger, gathered with the help of an Aymara assistant who was later arrested and died of his treatment, were sold to the Dutch and planted in Java, which by the 1930s supplied the great bulk of the world market. When Japan took Java in 1942 the Allies faced a shortage that pushed synthetic substitutes into mass production.\n\nBritain made its own attempt on the trade. Clements Markham led an expedition in 1860 that took seeds and seedlings out of Peru and Bolivia against local prohibition and established plantations in the Nilgiri hills of southern India, where a cheap mixed extract was later sold through post offices to villagers. The Indian plants proved poorer in the alkaloid than the Dutch stock and the industry there never matched Java. A laboratory synthesis announced by Robert Woodward and William Doering in 1944 was celebrated at the time, although it was far too laborious to be worth using.\n\nToo much of it causes ringing ears, deafness and nausea, a syndrome called cinchonism. It survives in medicine for severe and resistant cases, and in tonic water, whose bitterness is the reason gin was ever added to it.',
  },
  {
    num: 110, quizId: 'redact-11-23-26', live: '2026-11-23', dateLabel: 'November 23, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Amelia Earhart', aka: { earheart: 'earhart', airhart: 'earhart', earhardt: 'earhart', amelie: 'amelia' },
    text: 'On 20 May 1932, five years to the day after Charles Lindbergh left New York, a single engine Lockheed Vega took off from Harbour Grace in Newfoundland bound for Paris. Ice, a cracked exhaust manifold and a leaking fuel tank turned the crossing into a fight, and after roughly fifteen hours the pilot put down in a pasture near Culmore in Northern Ireland and asked a farmhand where she was.\n\nAmelia Earhart, born at Atchison in Kansas in 1897, had come to flying late and by way of odd jobs, taking lessons from Neta Snook in California in 1921 and buying a second hand biplane she called the Canary. In 1928 she was invited to cross the Atlantic as a passenger aboard the Friendship, becoming the first woman to make the trip by air, and she said plainly afterwards that she had been little more than baggage. Publicity organised by the publisher George Putnam, whom she later married, gave her the means to fly seriously.\n\nRecords followed. She was the first woman to fly the Atlantic alone, and in 1935 the first person to fly solo from Hawaii to California, a route on which several fliers had died. She was a founder and the first president of the Ninety Nines, an organisation of women pilots, and she wrote and lectured constantly, arguing that women should be judged on the same terms as men in aviation and elsewhere.\n\nIn 1937 she set out with the navigator Fred Noonan to fly around the world near the equator in a twin engine Lockheed Electra. They had covered about two thirds of the distance when they left Lae in New Guinea on 2 July for Howland Island, a flat speck of coral in the central Pacific. Radio contact with a coastguard cutter grew fragmentary and then stopped. A large naval search found nothing, and the disappearance has never been explained, which has kept a steady traffic of theories in circulation ever since.',
  },
  {
    num: 111, quizId: 'redact-11-24-26', live: '2026-11-24', dateLabel: 'November 24, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 2, answer: 'Mahatma Gandhi', aka: { ghandi: 'gandhi', gandi: 'gandhi', gandhii: 'gandhi', mahatama: 'mahatma' },
    text: 'In March 1930 a column of marchers left an ashram near Ahmedabad and walked for twenty four days to the Arabian Sea at Dandi, gathering people as it went. At the shore its leader picked up a lump of natural salt from the mud, an act that broke a colonial monopoly under which Indians were forbidden to make or sell their own. Tens of thousands were arrested in the campaign that followed, and the photographs travelled around the world.\n\nMohandas Karamchand Gandhi was born in 1869 at Porbandar, on the coast of Gujarat, and trained as a barrister in London. His formative political years were spent in South Africa, where he arrived in 1893 and stayed more than twenty, organising Indian merchants and labourers against discriminatory laws and working out the method he called satyagraha, usually translated as truth force or holding firmly to truth.\n\nHe returned to India in 1915 and helped turn the Indian National Congress from a body of lawyers and notables into a mass movement. He led non cooperation after the Amritsar massacre of 1919, called the campaign off when protesters killed policemen at Chauri Chaura, and used fasting repeatedly as a political instrument. He spun cotton daily and made homespun cloth a symbol of self reliance, and he campaigned against untouchability, giving the name Harijan to communities excluded by caste, a term many of them later rejected.\n\nMahatma Gandhi is an honorific rather than a name, meaning roughly great soul, and by most accounts he was uncomfortable with it. He was nominated for the Nobel Peace Prize several times and never received it. Independence came in August 1947 together with partition, which he had opposed and whose violence he spent his last months trying to quiet in Bengal and in Delhi. He was shot in Delhi on 30 January 1948 by a Hindu nationalist who objected to his conciliation of Muslims, and his methods went on to shape protest movements on several continents.',
  },
  {
    num: 112, quizId: 'redact-11-25-26', live: '2026-11-25', dateLabel: 'November 25, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 3, answer: 'Don Quixote', aka: { quijote: 'quixote', quixotte: 'quixote', quicksote: 'quixote' },
    text: 'Its author had an eventful and largely unrewarded life. Miguel de Cervantes fought at the sea battle of Lepanto in 1571 and lost the use of his left hand there, was captured by corsairs on the voyage home and spent five years enslaved in Algiers before his family and a religious order raised the ransom. Back in Spain he requisitioned grain for the Armada, worked as a tax collector, was jailed over irregular accounts and failed repeatedly as a playwright. He was in his late fifties when the first part of his novel appeared in 1605.\n\nDon Quixote is an elderly and impoverished gentleman of La Mancha who reads so many romances of chivalry that he comes to believe them, and rides out as a knight errant to revive an age that never existed. He renames his bony horse Rocinante, appoints a farm girl he has barely met as the lady Dulcinea, and persuades a neighbouring farmer, Sancho Panza, to serve as his squire on the promise of an island to govern. He charges windmills he takes for giants, mistakes inns for castles and flocks for armies, and is beaten for it again and again.\n\nA second part followed in 1615, provoked in part by an unauthorised sequel published under another name, which Cervantes attacks inside his own book. In it the characters have read the first part and are famous because of it, and people they meet stage elaborate deceptions for their own amusement. The comedy darkens as the knight is humoured rather than resisted.\n\nOften called the first modern novel, it has been translated into more than fifty languages and has never been out of print. Cervantes died in 1616, and the adjective made from his hero\'s name now means nobly and hopelessly impractical.',
  },
  {
    num: 113, quizId: 'redact-11-26-26', live: '2026-11-26', dateLabel: 'November 26, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Work', diff: 3, answer: 'Things Fall Apart', aka: { thingsfallapart: 'things', appart: 'apart' },
    text: 'Chinua Achebe read Joyce Cary and Joseph Conrad as a student at Ibadan and found that the Africans in them were scenery rather than people. He said later that this was the moment he understood that stories about his part of the world would have to be written from inside it. His first novel appeared in 1958 from a London publisher who printed a cautious two thousand hardbacks, and its title is a phrase lifted from a poem by W. B. Yeats about a world coming loose from its centre.\n\nThe book is set in Umuofia, a group of Igbo villages in what is now southeastern Nigeria, in the years just before and during the arrival of British missionaries and administrators. Its central figure, Okonkwo, is a wrestler and yam farmer who has risen by his own effort and is driven by contempt for his gentle, indebted, music loving father. His fear of any resemblance to that father makes him rigid and violent, and it carries him into the killing of a boy who had lived in his household as a son, against the advice of the oldest man in the village.\n\nThe first half is given almost entirely to the life of the village: the harvest, the marriage negotiations, the masked spirits who settle disputes, the proverbs that carry the argument. Achebe wrote in English but bent it to Igbo speech rhythms and filled it with those proverbs, an approach much argued over by writers who preferred African languages. Only in the last third does the mission arrive, and the change it brings is shown as both a violence and an opening, taken up first by those the old order had least use for.\n\nThings Fall Apart has sold many millions of copies in more than fifty languages, opened the Heinemann African Writers Series, and is taught worldwide as the founding novel of modern African fiction.',
  },
  {
    num: 114, quizId: 'redact-11-27-26', live: '2026-11-27', dateLabel: 'November 27, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Event', diff: 4, answer: 'The Bandung Conference', aka: { bandoeng: 'bandung', bandeng: 'bandung', bandhung: 'bandung' },
    text: 'In April 1955 the delegations of twenty nine countries from Asia and Africa met for a week in a hill town in West Java, at a hall built for a Dutch social club and renamed for independence. Between them they spoke for well over half the people alive. Most of the governments represented were less than a decade old, several were still fighting for recognition, and it was the first substantial international gathering at which the former colonies of two continents set the agenda without a European or American power in the room.\n\nIndonesia hosted, with India, Burma, Ceylon and Pakistan as co sponsors. Sukarno opened the proceedings by observing that the participants were united by a shared experience of being governed by others. Nehru, Zhou Enlai and Gamal Abdel Nasser were the figures most closely watched; Zhou in particular used the occasion to present a cautious face to neighbours nervous of Chinese intentions, and offered talks with the United States over Taiwan.\n\nAgreement was easier on colonialism than on the Cold War. Delegates from countries allied to the West argued that Soviet control in Eastern Europe was a form of colonialism too, and the final wording condemned it in all its manifestations, a formula broad enough for everyone to sign. The closing communique set out ten principles covering sovereignty, racial equality, non interference, peaceful settlement of disputes and economic and cultural cooperation, built on an earlier Indian and Chinese statement of five principles of coexistence.\n\nThe Bandung Conference founded no lasting organisation, and its participants soon disagreed sharply. Its influence was nonetheless real: it supplied the vocabulary and much of the membership of the non aligned movement launched at Belgrade six years later, and is widely credited with giving the phrase third world a political rather than merely geographical sense. The American writer Richard Wright attended and published a book about it.',
  },
  {
    num: 115, quizId: 'redact-11-28-26', live: '2026-11-28', dateLabel: 'November 28, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Place', diff: 4, answer: 'The Hagia Sophia', aka: { hagiasophia: 'hagia', ayasofya: 'hagia', sofia: 'sophia', hagea: 'hagia' },
    text: 'Riots in Constantinople in 532 came close to unseating the emperor Justinian and left the church at the centre of the city a burnt shell. He replaced it in five years, an implausibly short time for a building of that scale, and chose as designers not master masons but two men trained in geometry and mechanics: Anthemius of Tralles, who wrote on conic sections and burning mirrors, and Isidore of Miletus, who taught and edited mathematical texts. The result was less a scaled up basilica than an experiment.\n\nTheir problem was how to set a circular dome on a square hall. The solution, the pendentive, is a curved triangular panel filling each corner and carrying the weight down to four piers, and it allowed a dome about thirty one metres across to sit some fifty five metres above the floor with no visible support at its rim. Forty windows pierced around the base make the shell appear to hover on a ring of light, an effect the court historian Procopius described as a dome suspended from heaven by a golden chain. The original was too shallow and fell after earthquakes in 558; a steeper replacement, designed by the nephew of one of the architects, was finished in 562 and has largely survived.\n\nThe interior is faced with coloured marbles quarried across the empire and, in stages, with gold ground mosaics, many of them destroyed and later replaced during the periods when religious images were banned. The building served as the cathedral of the eastern church, was plundered by a crusading army in 1204, and after 1453 became a mosque, gaining minarets, calligraphic roundels and external buttresses added by the Ottoman architect Sinan.\n\nThe Hagia Sophia, whose name means holy wisdom, was turned into a museum by the Turkish republic in 1935 and reopened for prayer as a mosque in 2020. For roughly a thousand years it enclosed the largest interior space in the world.',
  },
  {
    num: 116, quizId: 'redact-11-29-26', live: '2026-11-29', dateLabel: 'November 29, 2026', sunday: true,
    cat: 'Person', diff: 5, answer: 'Marie Tharp', aka: { tharpe: 'tharp', tharpp: 'tharp', marrie: 'marie' },
    text: 'Women were not allowed on the ships. Through the 1950s the soundings that the Lamont Geological Observatory gathered in the Atlantic were taken by men aboard research vessels, and the work of turning columns of depth figures into pictures of the sea floor was done ashore, at a drafting table, by a geologist holding degrees in music, English, geology and mathematics. She plotted the profiles by hand, six of them across the North Atlantic, and drew the terrain between them in the oblique perspective that made the maps famous.\n\nMarie Tharp was born in Michigan in 1920, the daughter of a soil surveyor whose work moved the family constantly, and took a geology degree during the war, when petroleum programmes opened to women because the men had gone. She joined Lamont in 1948 and began working with Bruce Heezen, who supplied the data she drew.\n\nIn 1952 she noticed a notch shaped like a letter V running down the middle of the ridge that divides the Atlantic, present on all six of her profiles, and concluded that it was a rift valley, a place where the sea floor was pulling apart. Heezen is said to have called the idea girl talk, since it implied continental drift, which was then out of favour. He was persuaded when the rift was found to line up with a belt of earthquake epicentres, and the two of them published together.\n\nThe published form was itself a workaround. Detailed contour maps of the ocean floor were treated as sensitive by the United States Navy, on the ground that they would help a submarine navigate, so the results were issued instead as oblique panoramas drawn as though the water had been drained away. The compromise proved fortunate, since a painting of a mountain range reaches a reader who would take nothing from a sheet of contours.\n\nTheir physiographic diagrams of the ocean floor, painted with the Austrian landscape artist Heinrich Berann, culminated in a world map issued in 1977, and did as much as any single image to make sea floor spreading visible to people outside the field. Credit came slowly. Her name was left off some early presentations and papers, and her contribution was properly acknowledged only late in her life, after decades in which the maps were credited chiefly to the men she worked for. She died in 2006, and the rift she first drew is now understood as part of a ridge system that encircles the planet.',
  },
  {
    num: 117, quizId: 'redact-11-30-26', live: '2026-11-30', dateLabel: 'November 30, 2026', sunday: false,
    cat: 'Thing', diff: 2, answer: 'Glass', aka: { glas: 'glass', galss: 'glass', glasss: 'glass' },
    text: 'Nature makes it before people do. Lightning striking sand fuses branching tubes called fulgurites, and volcanoes throw out obsidian, which takes an edge finer than a surgical blade and was carried hundreds of miles for tools and weapons long before anyone smelted metal.\n\nThe manufactured kind appears in Mesopotamia and Egypt in the second millennium BC, at first as beads and small coloured vessels shaped around a core of sand and dung, since nobody could yet blow it. The decisive change came on the Syrian coast in the first century BC, when someone found that a gather of the molten material on the end of a hollow iron rod could be inflated with the breath. Blowing made vessels quick and cheap, and under the Roman empire glass went from luxury to kitchenware, used for storage jars, window panes and cremation urns.\n\nThe medieval trade concentrated at Venice, and in 1291 the furnaces were moved out to the island of Murano, officially to reduce the risk of fire in a city built largely of wood and conveniently also to keep the makers where the republic could watch them. They worked out cristallo, a clear and almost colourless body, and were forbidden to practise the craft abroad, a ban that leaked steadily anyway.\n\nFlat sheet was the hard problem. For centuries panes were made by spinning a disc out flat or by blowing a cylinder, splitting it and opening it into a plate, and both methods left distortion. The float process, developed by Alastair Pilkington and in production from 1959, solved it by pouring the melt onto a bath of molten tin, on which it spreads to an even thickness with two smooth faces.\n\nPhysically it is not a solid in the ordinary sense but an amorphous one, cooled too quickly for its atoms to settle into a crystal. The old story that cathedral windows are thicker at the foot because the material has flowed downward over the centuries is not supported by measurement; medieval panes were simply uneven, and were usually set with the heavy edge at the bottom.',
  },
];
